window.STUDY_SEARCH={"courses":[{"slug":"classical-myth","name":"Classical Myth in Art","home":"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","img":"/study/art-history/classical-myth/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"color-theory","name":"Color Theory","home":"/study/color-theory.html","img":"/study/color-theory/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"composition","name":"Composition","home":"/study/composition.html","img":"/study/composition/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"fauvism-and-expressionism","name":"Fauvism and Early Expressionism","home":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"impressionism","name":"Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"japanese-woodblock-prints","name":"Japanese Woodblock Prints","home":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","img":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"oil-vs-acrylic","name":"Oil vs Acrylic","home":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","img":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"pigments-and-materials","name":"Pigments and Materials","home":"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","img":"/study/pigments-and-materials/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"post-impressionism","name":"Post-Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"realism","name":"Realism","home":"/study/art-history/realism.html","img":"/study/art-history/realism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"romanticism","name":"Romanticism","home":"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","img":"/study/art-history/romanticism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"value-and-light","name":"Value and Light","home":"/study/value-and-light.html","img":"/study/value-and-light/img/","lane":"Technique"}],"items":[[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","Classical Myth in Art · Overview","What to Look For","","h1_aphrodite","The gods and heroes of Greece and Rome have been painted and carved for some 2,400 years. These works run from an Athenian wine cup of about 530 BCE to a Paris studio picture of about 1890, and each shows one way to recognize the myth, and the maker, from what is on the surface. · Sandro Botticelli · The Birth of Venus · about 1485"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/dionysus-cup.html","Classical Myth in Art","Dionysus Cup","Exekias","h9_exekias","Exekias · about 530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, about 12 in. across. Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · Exekias (Athenian potter and painter, 6th century BCE). Look at the figures: they are flat black silhouettes, with the detail scratched into them. · Ship, god, grapes and dolphins are solid black shapes against the orange-red ground. · Lines inside the shapes, like the grape bunches and the ship's planking, are cut through the black to the clay below. · The sail is the one pale shape, so it reads as a hole of light in the upper half of the bowl. · The design has no ground line: the dolphins circle the ship at every angle, so the whole inside of the cup is sea. · The god is Dionysus; the dolphins are the pirates he turned into fish. Only the inside is shown here."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/krater-with-heracles-wrestling-antaeus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus","Euphronios","h12_antaios","Euphronios · about 515–510 BCE · Red-figure terracotta, 18 in. high. Louvre, Paris · Euphronios (Athenian painter, 6th–5th century BCE). Look at the two wrestlers: they are drawn with a brush, in thin lines on the reddish clay, and the black is the background. · Muscles, ribs and knees are laid out as fine dark lines on the clay, drawn rather than scratched. · The two bodies cross as diagonals, Heracles pushing from the left, Antaeus flattened under him. · Antaeus's hair is a rough fringe and Heracles's is a cap of tiny raised dots, so the two heads read differently at a glance. · The women at either side rise upright in long dress folds, arms out, and frame the struggle between them. · Heracles kneels and strangles Antaeus; three women flee. Heracles's club, quiver and lion skin are drawn at the left."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/bronze-god-from-cape-artemision.html","Classical Myth in Art","Bronze God from Cape Artemision","Greek, sculptor unknown","h1_poseidon","Greek, sculptor unknown · about 460 BCE · Bronze, 82 1/4 in. high. National Archaeological Museum, Athens · Look at the pose: the whole body is one wide stance, arms stretched out to balance a throw that is about to happen. · The arms reach almost the figure's full height side to side, so the statue is as wide as it is tall. · Both feet are planted wide, one leg bent and one straight, a stride caught mid-step. · Chest and stomach are modelled as broad, firm planes, with no strain in the face. · The beard is a mass of tight curls and the hair a flat braid, the only fine detail on a plain body. · Nothing is in either hand, so it is read as Zeus with a thunderbolt or Poseidon with a trident. It was pulled from the sea off Euboea."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/apollo-belvedere.html","Classical Myth in Art","Apollo Belvedere","Roman, after a Greek bronze","h1_apollo","Roman, after a Greek bronze · early 2nd century CE · Marble. Vatican Museums, Vatican City · Look at the line of the body: it bends in a slow S from the turned head down through the hip to the back foot. · The head turns one way while the outstretched arm reaches the other, so the figure twists instead of facing you square. · One leg carries the weight and the other trails, which tips the hips and starts the curve. · The cloak hangs from the shoulder in deep, shadowed folds that set off the smooth skin beside them. · The surface is worked to an even white, and the face stays calm; nothing in the carving suggests effort. · A Roman copy of a Greek bronze of about 330 BCE. Apollo has just loosed an arrow; the outstretched hand once held the bow."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/venus-de-milo.html","Classical Myth in Art","Venus de Milo","Greek, sculptor unknown","h6_milo","Greek, sculptor unknown · about 150–125 BCE · Parian marble, 80 1/4 in. high. Louvre, Paris · Look at the twist: the hips turn one way and the shoulders the other, so the figure is a spiral you have to walk around. · The weight is on one hip, so the stomach curves and one side of the waist folds in. · The torso is carved as rounded, weighty masses, with soft transitions and no hard edges. · The cloth bunches in thick ridges at the hips, a heavy block that holds the smooth body above it up. · The skin surface is slightly grainy, so it catches light more gently than a polish would. · Carved in two blocks joined at the hips. Both arms are missing, all but a stub."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-birth-of-venus.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Birth of Venus","Sandro Botticelli","h1_aphrodite","Sandro Botticelli · about 1485 · Tempera on canvas, 68 × 109 3/4 in. Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Sandro Botticelli (Italian, 1445–1510). Look at the outlines: every figure is drawn with a clean contour, and the colour inside is nearly flat. · A dark line runs round the goddess's body, hair and shell, so she reads as a cut-out shape on the sea. · The sea is a sheet of pale green with small white V-marks for waves, more pattern than water. · The figures hardly cast shadows, and the light comes from nowhere in particular. · Hair, cloaks and falling roses are pulled into long curling lines that all show the same wind, blowing in from the left. · Venus comes ashore on a shell, blown by the wind gods; the woman at right holds out her cloak."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/diana-and-actaeon.html","Classical Myth in Art","Diana and Actaeon","Titian","h6_actaeon","Titian · 1556–59 · Oil on canvas, 72 3/4 × 79 1/2 in. The National Gallery, London, and National Galleries of Scotland · Titian (Italian, about 1488–1576). Look at how the picture is split: the hunter stands alone at the left and the goddess's group fills the right. · A pulled-back red curtain and a stone pillar divide the canvas into two unequal halves. · The hunter's raised hand is higher than any head in the picture; the bathers' heads sit lower, bunched across the right. · The pale bodies are packed together in the lower right, so the eye lands there after the hunter. · The blue sky shows mainly through the arch, a bright patch that fills the gap between the two sides. · One of six myth paintings for Philip II of Spain, known as the poesie, with subjects from Ovid."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-rape-of-europa.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Rape of Europa","Titian","h5_europa","Titian · 1559–62 · Oil on canvas, 70 × 80 3/4 in. Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston · Look at the brushwork: the red scarf, the clouds and the water are dragged on in loose strokes that never tidy up into detail. · The scarf at upper right is a few wide sweeps of pink and white that stay visible as strokes. · The distant mountains and shore are smudged into the sky with almost no edge between them. · The bull's head, the woman and the cupids are brought to a finish; sky, water and shore are left open. · Her pose is a loose diagonal across the lower right, legs thrown one way and arm the other. · Also painted for Philip II; signed at lower left, under the cupid on the dolphin."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/bacchus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Bacchus","Caravaggio","h9_bacchus","Caravaggio · about 1598 · Oil on canvas, 37 1/2 × 33 1/2 in. Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Caravaggio (Italian, 1571–1610). Look at the light: one source from the upper left falls on skin, cloth and fruit, and the wall behind drops away into brown. · The bare shoulder at left is the brightest patch in the picture, and the face is turned so its shaded side falls away from it. · The white drapery is modelled in big folds with hard lights and deep creases, like carved marble. · The fruit bowl is painted as closely as the face, with bruises and wormholes left in. · The wine glass is held out toward you, at the front edge of the picture. · A youth crowned with vine leaves as the wine god, offering a glass."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-fall-of-phaeton.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Fall of Phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens","h3_phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens · about 1604–05, probably reworked about 1606–08 · Oil on canvas, 38 3/4 × 51 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Peter Paul Rubens (Flemish, 1577–1640). Look at the composition: everything tumbles along one diagonal, from the rearing horse at upper right down to the tangle of horses at lower left. · Horses, chariot and bodies are bunched into a single swirling mass with no still point. · Shafts of light cut across the scene from the upper right, slicing through the clouds. · Figures at the edges are cropped by the frame, as if the action spills out of the picture. · The paint is thin and sketchy in the clouds and thicker on the horses, so the centre has the most weight. · The sun's chariot breaks apart; the butterfly-winged women at left are the seasons, in terror."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/laocoon.html","Classical Myth in Art","Laocoön","El Greco","h15_laocoon","El Greco · about 1610–14 · Oil on canvas, 54 × 68 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · El Greco (Greek, 1541–1614). Look at the shapes of the bodies: they are stretched long and thin, and their edges flicker. · Legs and torsos are pulled out past natural length, so the figures look taller than they could stand. · The flesh is grey-white with streaks of darker grey, lit from no clear direction. · The sky is painted in broken, sweeping strokes of blue, grey and white, as restless as the figures. · The city sits small in the middle distance, in hard little shapes, while the figures loom huge in front. · The Trojan priest and his sons are attacked by snakes; the city behind is Toledo."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/vulcans-forge.html","Classical Myth in Art","Vulcan's Forge","Diego Velázquez","h1_hephaestus","Diego Velázquez · 1630 · Oil on canvas, 87 3/4 × 114 1/4 in. Museo del Prado, Madrid · Diego Velázquez (Spanish, 1599–1660). Look at the paint handling: the god at left is a glow of thin, quick strokes, while the smiths are built up solidly. · Rays around Apollo's head are flicked on in short pale lines over the wall. · His orange robe is painted in long fluid strokes, with the folds barely described. · The smiths' backs and arms are modelled firmly in warm light and cool shadow, every muscle in place. · The space is made by the figures: each stands at a different depth, and the room recedes behind them. · Apollo tells the smith god that his wife is unfaithful; the workers freeze at the news."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-abduction-of-proserpina.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Abduction of Proserpina","Rembrandt","h9_bernini","Rembrandt · about 1630–31 · Oil on oak panel, 33 1/4 × 31 1/4 in. Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · Rembrandt (Dutch, 1606–1669). Look at the surface: the lit parts are thick, textured paint, and the rest sinks into a dark ground. · The woman's dress and the chariot are built up in crusted, pale strokes. · The whole right half is near-black, with only a horse's rump and some foliage picked out. · The sky at upper left is a block of pure blue against which the dark trees are silhouetted. · The light runs from the blue corner down to the horses, so the eye travels out of the day and into the dark. · Pluto drags the girl down to the underworld; her companions cling to her cloak."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-judgement-of-paris.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Judgement of Paris","Peter Paul Rubens","h15_paris","Peter Paul Rubens · probably 1632–35 · Oil on panel, probably oak, 57 × 76 1/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Look at the colour: three warm pink bodies stand against cool greens and greys, with strong red held to the cloaks at the edges and the robe of the nude seen from behind. · Each nude is a slightly different pink, from cool and pearly at left to golden in the centre. · The evening sky behind them grades from yellow at the horizon to grey-blue above. · Silk, velvet, feathers and hair are painted with soft edges that blur into the flesh beside them. · The line of figures runs across the picture like a frieze, with the landscape opening on the right. · Paris hands the apple to Venus, in the centre; the owl marks Minerva at left and the peacock Juno at right."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/oedipus-explains-the-riddle-of-the-sphinx.html","Classical Myth in Art","Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","h14_ingres","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1808 · Oil on canvas, 74 1/2 × 56 3/4 in. Louvre, Paris · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres (French, 1780–1867). Look at the edge of the body: a single unbroken contour, drawn as if with a pen, with no visible brushwork inside it. · The figure is in strict profile, so the outline from brow to heel reads as one continuous line. · Skin is smoothed to an even, waxy finish. · The lit body stands against a dark cave, which makes the contour sharper still. · Colour is kept to a few flat notes: tan skin, red cloth, ochre rock, blue sky. · Oedipus answers the Sphinx's riddle; a foot and bones lie at lower left."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/ulysses-deriding-polyphemus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","J. M. W. Turner","h16_turner","J. M. W. Turner · 1829 · Oil on canvas, 52 1/4 × 80 in. The National Gallery, London · J. M. W. Turner (British, 1775–1851). Look at the colour as light: the story is told by a sunrise of yellow, pink and blue, and the figures are hard to find. · The sun at right is the brightest point, and everything near it dissolves into pale yellow. · The giant is a shadowy brown-purple shape in the clouds at upper left, barely more solid than they are. · Thin, dry colour is dragged over the sky, so pinks and yellows sit on top of each other in layers. · The ship is the sharpest place in the picture: a gilded hull and crisp pale sails against the glare. · Ulysses, in red under a red banner, taunts the giant; sea nymphs glimmer at the bow."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/oedipus-and-the-sphinx.html","Classical Myth in Art","Oedipus and the Sphinx","Gustave Moreau","h18_moreau","Gustave Moreau · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 81 1/4 × 41 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Gustave Moreau (French, 1826–1898). Look at the surface detail: the wings, the jewels and the urn are picked out feather by feather and bead by bead. · Every edge is sharp, from the wing tips to the red cord of beads around the creature's chest. · The strokes are tiny and build up ornament rather than texture. · The two heads are locked eye to eye at the top of a tall, narrow canvas, which squeezes the drama into a vertical. · The background rocks are cool grey-blue, so the gold-brown body and the pale flesh come forward. · The same riddle scene as two slides back, now a close standoff; shown at the Paris Salon of 1864."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/pygmalion-and-galatea.html","Classical Myth in Art","Pygmalion and Galatea","Jean-Léon Gérôme","h6_pygmalion","Jean-Léon Gérôme · about 1890 · Oil on canvas, 35 × 27 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Jean-Léon Gérôme (French, 1824–1904). Look at the colour of the statue: it is chalk white at the feet and warms to living flesh as it rises. · The change from marble to skin happens in a smooth gradient up the legs, with no line where one stops. · The sculptor's blue tunic is the strongest colour, set against the grey-green upper wall. · The surface is as smooth as the statue it shows, even across the whole picture. · The pair are seen from behind, so the kiss is half hidden and the back of the figure fills the picture. · The statue is the painter's own marble group, brought to life as he kisses it."],[1,"/study/color-theory.html","Start","Color Theory","","","Color Theory"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/a-short-history-of-color-theory.html","A Short History of Color Theory · Overview","A Short History of Color Theory","","runge-farbenkugel","The thinkers and books that set the terms painters still use · Ideas about color come from physicists, poets, chemists and teachers, not only from painters. Each of them asked a different question: what light is, how the eye answers it, how neighboring colors affect each other, and how to name a color exactly. Their books and plates, like the painter Philipp Otto Runge's 1810 color sphere shown here with white and black at its poles, still shape how color is taught. · Newton's prism experiments and his circle of colors · Goethe's turn from light to the viewing eye · Chevreul's discovery at a Paris tapestry works · Munsell's measured system and the art-school teachers who followed · Plate · A full-page printed picture in a book, often printed separately from the text and sometimes colored by hand. · Philipp Otto Runge · Plate from Farben-Kugel (Color Sphere), Hamburg · 1810"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-newton-splits-white-light.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","Newton Splits White Light","","newton-opticks-circle","In Opticks (1704), Isaac Newton published his account of a prism spreading sunlight into a band of colors that can be gathered back into white. · He bent the band into a circle, sizing each color's arc like a step in a musical scale. Treating the colors in a mix of lights as weights on the ring, he proposed that the closer their balance point lay to the center, the whiter the mix. · On the plate the arcs read red, orange, yellow, green, blue (spelled “Blew”), indigo and violet. · Color, he wrote, lies in how surfaces reflect rays and rays stir the senses: “the rays to speak properly are not coloured” (1704). · He admitted a gap: two opposite colors alone had not yet given him true white, only a faint tint."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-goethe-and-the-seeing-eye.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","Goethe and the Seeing Eye","","goethe-farbenlehre-plate1","Johann Wolfgang von Goethe's Zur Farbenlehre (Theory of Colours, 1810) treated color as something that happens in the viewer's eye, not only in light. · He opened with colors the eye produces itself, such as afterimages and colored shadows, which, he complained, earlier writers had treated as illusions. His six-part ring sets each color across from a partner. His English translator judged that the open attack on Newton kept many scientists from hearing him out, at least for a time. · In the ring, yellow faces violet, orange faces blue and red faces green. · Partners, in the 1840 translation, “reciprocally evoke each other in the eye”; see Afterimages, in the module on interaction. · That translator, Charles Eastlake of the Royal Academy, first meant to publish only the parts useful to painters."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-chevreul-and-colors-side-by-side.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","Chevreul and Colors Side by Side","","chevreul-chromatic-circle","Michel-Eugène Chevreul, the chemist in charge of dyeing at the Gobelins tapestry works in Paris, set out his law of simultaneous contrast, on how neighboring colors alter each other, in a book of 1839. · Complaints came in that his black wools looked weak in blue and violet draperies. Blacks from other workshops proved no better: the fault lay not in the dye but in the hues around the black. Years of work on that finding grew into the book. · Charles Martel's English translation of 1854 carried the book to British readers. · This ring of 72 pure hues comes from a later volume of 1864 on color in the industrial arts. · The effect itself is explained on the Simultaneous Contrast page, in the module on interaction."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-from-munsell-to-the-bauhaus.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","From Munsell to the Bauhaus","","munsell-sphere-1905","In A Color Notation (1905), the Boston painter Albert Munsell argued that color needed a scale and vocabulary as settled as music's. · His starting model was a color globe he patented in 1900, drawn in pastel for the book's opening plate. He also taught for decades at the Massachusetts Normal Art School. At the Bauhaus, the German design school, Johannes Itten taught color in the required first course from 1919 to 1923; Josef Albers, there from 1920, later carried his own method to America. · His system itself is set out on the Munsell's System page. · Itten later wrote The Art of Color (1961); his contrasts are covered on Itten's Seven Contrasts. · Albers's Interaction of Color (1963) taught through cut-paper exercises; both books are in copyright, so this course only describes them."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/light-and-seeing.html","Light and Seeing · Overview","Light and Seeing","","ct-light-path","What color is before it reaches the canvas, and how the eye reads it · Color begins as light. These pages follow it from a light source, off a surface and into the eye and brain. They also show why a color that seems fixed can shift when the light does. · The band of wavelengths we can see · Why lights add up to white while paints add up to dark · Three kinds of cone and the brain's comparison · Changing light, constancy, and colors that match under only one light · Diagram: how a red surface turns white daylight into red light"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-light-and-the-spectrum.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Light and the Spectrum","","bezold-plate-i-solar-spectrum","Visible light is the narrow band of electromagnetic waves the eye can detect, roughly 380 to 700 nanometres long. · Hue changes along the band, from red at the long end through orange, yellow, green and blue to violet. Sunlight carries the whole band and looks white; Newton Splits White Light shows a prism fanning it out. Dark lines, lettered A to H, mark light absorbed by gases in the sun and air. · A cut-glass crystal or a hose's spray in sunlight shows the band. · Magenta and purple appear nowhere in the band; the eye builds them from red and violet light together. · Beyond the two ends lie ultraviolet and infrared, waves too short or too long for the eye. · Nanometre (nm) · One millionth of a millimetre, the unit used for the wavelength of light."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-mixing-light-vs-mixing-paint.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Mixing Light vs. Mixing Paint","","bezold-plate-i-mixtures","Additive mixing, as with lights, adds wavelengths with each new color; subtractive mixing, as with pigments, takes more away with each one. · Wilhelm von Bezold, a German physicist, tested this with gamboge yellow and Prussian blue: mixed as paint, only the green both reflect survives; mixed as light in the eye, they add to a dim white that reads as gray. Screens add red, green and blue light; printers subtract with cyan, magenta and yellow inks. · Look at a phone screen through a strong magnifier: tiny glowing red, green and blue points, not blended color. · In paint, only a color lighter than the mix raises its value. · Real paints mix less cleanly than ideal inks: red, yellow and blue together give a dark brown."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-how-the-eye-sees-color.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","How the Eye Sees Color","","ct-cones","Color vision starts with three kinds of cone cell in the retina, most sensitive to short, medium or long wavelengths; nerve cells, beginning in the retina itself, then compare their signals. · The three ranges overlap widely, so no single cone signals red or blue; color comes from the balance among them. That is why three lights can be mixed to match a huge range of colors. · Watch a garden at dusk: as cones give way to the more sensitive rods, colors fade before shapes do. · Color is seen most sharply at the center of gaze, where cones are packed most densely. · The eye's lens yellows with age, absorbing more of the shortest, blue-violet wavelengths before they reach the cones. · Retina · The light-sensitive layer lining the back of the eye."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-light-changes-color.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Light Changes Color","","ct-light-changes-color","Color constancy is the brain's habit of seeing roughly a surface's own color even when the light falling on it, and so the light it sends to the eye, changes. · Lamplight is rich in long wavelengths, so a gray stone under it really does send orange-tinted light to the eye; blue shade does the reverse. The brain discounts the tint by judging each color against the whole scene. · Look at a white wall lit by a lamp at one end and daylight at the other; the ends differ. · Painters chasing a particular light paint the shift the brain hides, sometimes pushing it further; Monet's cathedral series is one example. · Constancy is incomplete: in strongly colored light, or in a patch seen out of context, the tint shows."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/rouen-cathedral-west-facade.html","Light and Seeing","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade","Claude Monet","monet-rouen-west-facade","Claude Monet · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 100.1 × 65.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Blue-violet strokes fill the shadowed stone, with flecks of peach where light catches the towers; the rose window is a dark blue disc. Across more than thirty views of this façade, the gallery says, color above all sets each canvas apart. · A companion canvas in the same gallery shows the front in full sun: cream, gold and pale pink under a deep blue sky. · The gallery's description compares the blurred surface to a reflection broken up on rippling water. · He reworked the series in the studio; a curator notes he exaggerated the light and used color for mood. · The gallery's audio guide traces the series' color from pinks and yellows to deep greens and blues as the light dims."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-metamerism.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Metamerism","","ct-metamerism","Metamerism is when two colors match under one light but not under another, because their surfaces reflect different mixtures of wavelengths that happen to look alike. · The eye reduces every mixture of wavelengths to three cone signals, so quite different mixtures can give the same three signals. Change the light and each surface shifts by its own amount, so a passage matched under studio lamps can stand apart in daylight. · Check an important match both by daylight and under the light where the work will hang. · To match an earlier mixture, or to retouch, use the same pigments, not just a color that looks the same. · Viewers differ too: a pair that matches for one person may not for another, because cone responses vary."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/describing-color.html","Describing Color · Overview","Describing Color","","field-chromatography-plate-1","Words and numbers for telling one color from another · Everyday color names are too loose for a painter. This module sets out the properties every color has, the wheel painters use to arrange hues, and a system that locates each color by number. Later modules use these terms throughout. · Three properties of any color, and what white, gray or black do to it · Primaries, secondaries and tertiaries on the painter's wheel · A measured color space built around a gray axis · Achromatic · Without hue: white, black and the grays between them. · George Field · Plate I from Chromatography, London · 1835"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-hue-value-and-saturation.html","Describing Color · Concept","Hue, Value and Saturation","","ostwald-farbenfibel-triangle","Every color has three properties: hue, its color family (red, blue-green); value, how light or dark it is; and saturation, how strong or grayed it is. · Each can change while the others hold still. The chemist Wilhelm Ostwald's 1921 triangle starts from one yellow: mixed toward white it makes tints along the top edge, toward black shades along the bottom, and toward gray the tones between. All three usually also lower saturation. · Name a color by all three, such as a light, grayed blue-green, rather than by an object like sage. · Black shifts hue as well as value: stirred into yellow, it gives olive greens."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-the-color-wheel.html","Describing Color · Concept","The Color Wheel","","field-chromatography-plate-1","A color wheel arranges hues in a closed ring, running from red through violet and back to red, so every hue has neighbors on both sides. · On the painter's traditional wheel, red, yellow and blue are the primaries. Two primaries mixed give a secondary: orange, green or violet. A primary mixed with the secondary beside it gives a tertiary, such as blue-green. Twelve steps are usual; finer wheels are possible. · Read it as a map of mixing; Analogous, in the module on harmony and contrast, covers how neighbors mix. · This wheel follows paint; light and printing ink start from other primaries (see Mixing Light vs. Mixing Paint). · George Field's 1835 tertiaries mix two secondaries: citrine, russet, olive. Today's are also called intermediates."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-munsells-system.html","Describing Color · Concept","Munsell's System","","munsell-atlas-chroma-branches","Devised by Albert Munsell, this system places each color on three scales. Hue runs around a circle, value up a gray axis from black (0) to white (10), and chroma outward from that axis. · A notation such as 5R 4/14 reads hue, then value over chroma: a strong red a little darker than middle gray. The circle rests on five principal hues, not the painter's three. Munsell compared a chart that ignores chroma to 'a map of Switzerland with the mountains left out.' · On his 1915 chart, red reaches ten steps from the axis at value 4; blue, at the same value, only six. · Every hue shares the one gray axis, so colors of any hue can be compared by value alone. · Chroma has no fixed end; Munsell expected branches to lengthen as stronger pigments appeared."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/value-and-temperature.html","Value and Temperature · Overview","Value and Temperature","","munsell-atlas-value-axis","Light against dark, warm against cool, and the quiet colors in between · Before it is a set of hues, a painting is a pattern of lights and darks. This module looks at that pattern, at the pull between warm and cool, and at the grays and browns that hold a picture together. Each idea is paired with a painting that depends on it. · Reading lights and darks across different hues · Temperature as a comparison rather than a fixed label · Grays mixed from opposites, and pigments dug from the earth · Key · A picture's overall value range: high key is mostly light, low key mostly dark. · Albert H. Munsell · Chart V from Atlas of the Munsell Color System, Malden, Massachusetts · 1915"],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-seeing-light-and-dark.html","Value and Temperature · Concept","Seeing Light and Dark","","munsell-atlas-value-axis","A painting's value pattern is the arrangement of its lights and darks, and it does more than hue to show form, light and depth. · Every hue has its own value at full strength: on Munsell's 1915 chart, yellow sits at 8 of 10, red and blue at 4, purple at 3. The brain reads depth and form largely from differences in lightness, so a well-built picture still holds together in a black-and-white photograph. · Squint: it blurs detail and mutes hue, so the main lights and darks stand out. · Many painters plan a picture in three to five values before they choose any colors. · Strong hues mislead the eye; a vivid red can look lighter than a gray of the same value."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/symphony-in-white-no-1-the-white-girl.html","Value and Temperature","Symphony in White, No. 1: The White Girl","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-white-girl","James McNeill Whistler · 1861–1863, 1872 · Oil on canvas, 213 × 107.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Dress and curtain are almost all white, so the figure is modeled in very small steps of value, in a high key. Red hair and lips, a few flowers and blue carpet pattern give the only strong color; the bearskin is a pale tan. · In February 1862 Whistler told George du Maurier that, apart from the red hair, it was \"one gorgeous mass of brilliant white.\" · A gray underlayer lies in selected areas beneath thick, heavily textured white paint. · Technical study suggests a more colorful floor covering once lay where the bearskin is now. · He later grouped it with two more white pictures as Symphony in White, and said such works were about line and color, not story."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-warm-and-cool.html","Value and Temperature · Concept","Warm and Cool","","field-chromatography-fig-2","Color temperature sorts hues into warm ones, around red, orange and yellow, and cool ones, around green, blue and violet. It always works by comparison. · Any hue can lean either way: a red with a touch of blue looks cool beside a red that leans to orange. Light has temperature too, and painters often see cool shadows under warm light and warm shadows under cool light. · Set two versions of one hue side by side; which one is warmer shows at once. · Many painters lay out a warm and a cool version of each primary, so mixtures can lean either way. · The split is a convention: George Field's 1835 ring runs it from “Hot” orange to “Cold” blue, and border hues such as yellow-green are disputed."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/the-fighting-temeraire.html","Value and Temperature","The Fighting Temeraire","Joseph Mallord William Turner","turner-temeraire","Joseph Mallord William Turner · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 90.7 × 121.6 cm. The National Gallery, London · Turner divided the picture by temperature: a blazing orange sunset fills the right, a cool blue sky with a thin moon the left. The pale old warship moves on the cool side. · He painted the ship white and gold, though its actual paintwork was black and yellow. · The tug's smoke repeats the copper of the clouds, tying the dark boat to the warm side. · Around the sun, the orange, brown, pink and yellow of the clouds go on heavily, in places raised well above the canvas. · The sun’s pale disk is answered by a dark buoy at lower right, a value contrast that anchors the warm side. · John Ruskin observed that Turner often used his reddest sunsets to signal death."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-neutrals-and-earth-colors.html","Value and Temperature · Concept","Neutrals and Earth Colors","","ct-neutrals","Neutrals are colors of very low saturation: grays, browns and near-grays. Earth colors are pigments such as ochres, siennas and umbers, dug from the ground. · Mixing two opposites cancels much of their hue, giving grays that still lean toward one parent. Earths owe their color mainly to iron compounds, with manganese adding the brown of umbers, and they are among the most permanent pigments. · Hold a pure swatch beside a wall or a shadow to see how gray the wall or shadow really is. · Burnt sienna and burnt umber are the raw earths heated, which darkens them and turns sienna from yellow-brown to red."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/ville-davray.html","Value and Temperature","Ville-d'Avray","Camille Corot","corot-ville-davray","Camille Corot · 1870 · Oil on canvas, 54.9 × 80 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Nearly every passage here is a neutral: gray-greens in the foliage, green-black shadow along the bank, and a pewter sky behind the leaves. Even the pond reads as a silvery gray. · Robaut likened the dark branches against the pale sky to a spider's web, a pattern drawn almost entirely in value. · The greens never reach full strength; they lean toward silver in the light and toward near-black in shadow. · By contrast, his early oil sketches, such as those made in Italy, are clearly defined and fresh, with bright colors in fluid strokes. · After about 1850 his style softened and his range of colors narrowed. Many later studio landscapes keep mostly to tinted grays and blue-greens."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/harmony-and-contrast.html","Harmony and Contrast · Overview","Harmony and Contrast","","ct-seven-contrasts","Choosing colors that settle together, or that pull apart · Some color choices tie a picture together; others make parts of it jump out. These pages set out the classic color families a painter can pick from the wheel. They then look at contrast, using one well-known list of its kinds. · One hue, or a run of neighboring hues · Opposites, triads and four-hue schemes · A checklist of seven contrasts · Dull against intense, and small patches against large fields · Color scheme · A planned set of hues, chosen by where they sit on the color wheel. · Seven kinds of color contrast, drawn as swatch pairs"],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-monochromatic.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Monochromatic","","ct-monochromatic","A monochromatic scheme keeps to a single hue and varies only its lightness and strength. · With hue held still, the picture's structure rests almost entirely on light and dark. The result tends to feel calm and unified, and it shows at once whether the values are working. · To spot one, check whether every passage could be one paint, only lightened or darkened. · In practice many allow slight shifts warmer or cooler, or a few small touches of another color. · Without a wide enough value range, a one-hue picture turns flat rather than quiet."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/nocturne-blue-and-silver-battersea-reach.html","Harmony and Contrast","Nocturne: Blue and Silver—Battersea Reach","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-battersea-reach","James McNeill Whistler · 1870–75 · Oil on canvas, 49.9 × 72.3 cm. National Museum of Asian Art (Freer Gallery of Art), Smithsonian Institution, Washington, DC · Moored barges and a far shore of chimneys dissolve into a haze of gray, faintly blue-green. Nearly the whole river at night is held in a single hue. · The title itself names the scheme: blue and silver. · Thin, fluid blue went over a brown underlayer on rough canvas; later reworking left the surface grayer than it began. · The only breaks are tiny dots of warmer color for the lamps along the far shore and their glints on the water."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-analogous.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Analogous","","ct-analogous","An analogous scheme uses hues that sit side by side on the color wheel, usually three to five neighbors. · Neighbors share a parent color, so they read as one family and feel harmonious with little effort. The risk is sameness, which painters offset with changes of light, dark and intensity inside the family. · Look for a picture that stays on one side of the wheel: all cool, or all warm. · Stretch the arc too far and the family breaks apart into colors that contrast."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/water-lilies-agapanthus.html","Harmony and Contrast","Water Lilies (Agapanthus)","Claude Monet","monet-water-lilies-agapanthus","Claude Monet · c. 1915–26 · Oil on canvas, 201.3 × 425.6 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Violets, blues and greens fill nearly every inch of this pond. Only the pale lily pads and a few blossoms step outside the cool family. · With no banks or horizon in view, the water's colors run to every edge. · Violet passages near the top and green ones below both contain blue, so the eye reads them as one surface. · Small orange-red blossoms at the lower right are the strongest accents in the picture. · At over four meters wide, the canvas surrounds anyone standing close with this one color family."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-complementary.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Complementary","","bezold-plate-iv-pairs","Complementary colors sit opposite each other on the color wheel, and side by side each makes the other look more intense. · Between them, each pair contains all three paint primaries: red is set against green, and green is yellow mixed with blue. Bezold's 1876 pairs sit opposite on his twelve-step circle; he notes only purple and green are true complements of light. · To spot a pair, find a picture's main hue and look for its opposite elsewhere in the picture. · Where opposites of matched lightness meet, the edge can flicker; Vibrating and Vanishing Edges explains why."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/the-night-cafe.html","Harmony and Contrast","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-night-cafe","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 72.4 × 92.1 cm. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · Blood-red walls press against a green ceiling, and green tables and a pale green bar stand out against the red. Here the opposites are meant to grate, not to please. · Writing to his brother Theo, he said he had tried to show the terrible passions of humanity with red and green. · A day later his letter broke the green into several kinds, from soft malachite tones to harsh blue-greens and yellow-greens. · Saturated yellow, thick on the floor and set in dashes around the lamps, adds a third note; he likened the air to a furnace of pale sulfur. · He called it one of the ugliest pictures he had made. The clash was deliberate: the café was to feel like a place where people ruin themselves."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-triads-and-wider-schemes.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Triads and Wider Schemes","","bezold-plate-iv-triads","Wider schemes space three or four hues around the color wheel. · A triad takes three evenly spaced hues, as in Bezold's four 1876 examples, spaced on his own twelve-step circle. A split complement pairs one hue with the two neighbors of its opposite, and a square takes four evenly spaced hues. These schemes are lively but hard to balance, so most painters let one hue lead and keep the others smaller or duller. · To spot a triad, name a picture's three main colors and check their spacing on the wheel. · A split complement gives much of the punch of opposites with less of a head-on clash. · Three or four distant hues at full strength and equal size compete and look like a flag."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/the-milkmaid.html","Harmony and Contrast","The Milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer · c. 1660 · Oil on canvas, 45.5 × 41 cm. Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam · Yellow, blue and red, the painter's three primaries, carry the figure against a pale wall. The triad is plain to see, yet the picture stays calm. · Blue leads the composition; the yellow bodice and a smaller amount of red skirt support it. · The blue is natural ultramarine; as in several of his paintings, he used it even where cheaper azurite would have done. · The bodice is lead-tin yellow, and the skirt is madder lake laid over charcoal black. · The whitewashed wall, mixed from umber, black and lead white, is a neutral that lets the three hues stand apart."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-ittens-seven-contrasts.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Itten's Seven Contrasts","","ct-seven-contrasts","Johannes Itten sorted the ways colors can differ into seven contrasts: hue, light and dark, warm and cool, complements, simultaneous contrast, saturation and extension. · The list works best as a checklist for looking, not a rule book. Complements appear above, light–dark and warm–cool in Value and Temperature, and simultaneous contrast in Interaction; the last two follow here. · Hue contrast is plainest when hues sit far apart on the wheel at full strength, as in a triad. · For any two passages in a painting, ask which of the seven differences are doing the work. · The seven overlap: a red beside a green differs in hue and often in value at once."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-contrast-of-saturation.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Contrast of Saturation","","munsell-atlas-chroma-steps","This contrast sets pure, intense colors against dull, grayed ones. · An intense color looks stronger among muted ones and weaker among other intense colors. Painters use this to decide where the eye lands without changing hue or lightness. Since mixing rarely raises intensity, dulling the surroundings is often the easier route. · It needs no second hue: Munsell's red, stepped from gray to full strength, already contrasts with itself. · Painters gray down most of a picture so a few saturated passages can carry the focus. · Making every color intense backfires: when everything shouts, nothing stands out."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/manuel-osorio-manrique-de-zuniga.html","Harmony and Contrast","Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga","Francisco de Goya","goya-red-boy","Francisco de Goya · 1787–88 · Oil on canvas, 127 × 101.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A boy's red suit is the only large patch of intense color, set in a room of soft grays and browns. Much of its force comes from how muted its surroundings are. · The suit gave the portrait its nickname, the \"Red Boy,\" used in the museum's own publications. · The sash and lace collar are nearly colorless, so nothing else competes with the red. · The cats are mostly gray, black and white, with warm patches on one and amber eyes; the magpie is black and white. · The birdcage is a muted green, the red's opposite, answering the suit without matching its strength. · Small touches of red and yellow on the caged finches echo the suit on a tiny scale."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-contrast-of-extension.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Contrast of Extension","","ct-extension","Here the contrast is one of area: a small patch of one color can balance a much larger field of another. · Light, strong colors need less room than dark ones to make an equal impression. Itten set area ratios for the complement pairs from rough estimates of how strongly each hue acts on the eye. He credited Goethe, but the figures appear to go back to Schopenhauer's 1816 essay on color. · Ask which color claims the most area, and which small patch still catches the eye. · Painters often break the balance on purpose: a small, strong accent in a wide field becomes the focus. · The ratios assume full-strength hues; dull a color or change its lightness and the balance shifts."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/river-with-a-distant-tower.html","Harmony and Contrast","River with a Distant Tower","Camille Corot","corot-river-tower","Camille Corot · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 54.6 × 78.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Gray-greens in the trees and a pale, dull river and sky take up nearly all of the canvas. Specks of red mark the boatman's cap and a child at the left. · Red sits opposite green on the painter's wheel, so these small accents gain force from the field around them. · The red covers only a sliver of the surface, yet the eye can move between these few points. · Around them the picture stays low in saturation, which leaves the reds unchallenged."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/interaction.html","Interaction · Overview","Interaction","","seurat-circus-sideshow","How neighboring colors change one another in the eye · No color is ever seen on its own. This module covers the main cases: colors side by side, colors seen one after another, small marks the eye blends, and shared edges. Each page has a diagram you can test on yourself. · One paint made to look like two · Ghost colors that follow a steady stare · Dots and fine lines that merge at a distance · Edges that shimmer or vanish, and flat shapes that look like glass · Context · The colors around a color, which change how it looks. · Georges Seurat · Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque) · 1887–88"],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-simultaneous-contrast.html","Interaction · Concept","Simultaneous Contrast","","bezold-plate-iii","A color seen beside another seems to shift away from it, so two paints can look alike, or far apart, on different grounds. · The eye exaggerates differences at a boundary. On Wilhelm von Bezold's 1876 plates, two different reds look nearly alike on his purple (now faded to crimson) and yellow (left); swap the grounds and they split apart (center); on neutral paper their true difference shows (right). How Chevreul came to it is told on Chevreul and Colors Side by Side, in Module 1. · Mask a color with a scrap of gray card to see it apart from its neighbors. · Painters judge a mixture on the canvas, beside what will surround it, not on the palette. · Knowing the two disks differ does not make them look different, even to a trained eye."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-afterimages.html","Interaction · Concept","Afterimages","","ct-afterimage","An afterimage is a ghost color seen after staring at a color, usually close to its opposite: a strong red leaves a pale blue-green. · Steady looking tires the parts of the eye that respond to that color, so a white surface seen next looks tinged with its opposite. Chevreul called this successive contrast, because it unfolds over time rather than side by side. Goethe saw a girl in a scarlet bodice reappear on a white wall in sea-green. · Stare at the dot in the red disk for half a minute, then look at the blank square. · After long work on one strong color, rest your eyes before judging the next mixture. · Afterimages fade in seconds and vary between viewers, so they are only a rough guide."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-optical-mixing.html","Interaction · Concept","Optical Mixing","","bezold-plate-v-fig-3","Small touches of different colors, seen from far enough away, merge into one color in the eye instead of on the palette. · The eye averages the light from each touch, so the result stays lighter than the same paints stirred together. Bezold printed a test case: from a distance, red ornament on blue shows “a tinge of purple,” yet vermilion and ultramarine mixed on the palette make “a dirty reddish brown.” · Walk toward a dotted passage and watch one color split into its parts. · Painters set dots or broken strokes side by side to keep mixtures clean and lively. · Dots of opposite colors tend to average toward gray, so painters after a clean mixture can pair neighboring hues."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/circus-sideshow-parade-de-cirque.html","Interaction","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus-sideshow","Georges Seurat · 1887–88 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 149.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A night scene under gaslight, built from dots and dashes instead of blended paint. Up close the figures break apart into separate touches of blue, red and orange. · Cobalt blue, ground very fine and at times mixed with red lake or emerald green, supplies the strokes that read as black. · Each area began with large dots of its main color, followed by ever smaller touches. · Finishing touches of tiny orange dots, vermilion with chrome yellow, imitate gaslight on the people in the scene. · The cast is mostly violet and green, warmed in the lit areas. The canvas has never been varnished. · He called his method chromo-luminarism, stressing the glow he was after. He had read the color theories of Chevreul and Ogden Rood."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-the-bezold-effect.html","Interaction · Concept","The Bezold Effect","","bezold-plate-v-figs-5-6","Changing one color in a pattern can shift the look of all the others, seen most clearly when fine lines switch from white to black. · In Fig. 5 of Bezold's plate, from his book on color in art and the decorative trades, one red carries black ornament on the left and white on the right: it reads darker beside black, lighter beside white. In Fig. 6, red ornament on black, it darkens more still. This pull toward a neighbor is called assimilation, or spreading. · From across the room, the one red splits into a light and a dark version. · It is the reverse of simultaneous contrast, where a neighbor pushes a color away from itself. · Changing one thread or outline color can recolor a whole rug or textile design."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-vibrating-and-vanishing-edges.html","Interaction · Concept","Vibrating and Vanishing Edges","","ct-vibrating-edges","Where two colors of the same value meet, the edge can seem to shimmer or to dissolve. It shimmers when the hues are strong and far apart, and dissolves when they are dull and close. · The eye finds edges most sharply through differences in lightness. Without that cue, a bright red-green border flickers, while a muted pair gives the eye nothing firm to hold. Albers called these vibrating and vanishing boundaries. · Photograph the strips in black and white: matched values turn into nearly one flat gray. · Painters soften a contour by matching values across it, and sharpen one with a light-dark step. · Bezold advised a lighter or darker outline between closely related colors, so the eye can follow the design."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-transparency-and-space.html","Interaction · Concept","Transparency and Space","","ct-transparency","Flat, opaque colors can look like overlapping sheets of tinted glass when the shared area is painted in a color between the two. · The eye reads the in-between color as one sheet seen through another, so a flat design gains layers and depth. If the overlap looks more like one of the two colors, that color tends to seem on top and come forward. · Compare the right-hand pair: an unrelated color in the overlap breaks the illusion at once. · Painters and designers use it to suggest veils, water or tinted light without real glazes. · Albers's name for the in-between color is the middle mixture."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/paint-and-pigment.html","Paint and Pigment · Overview","Paint and Pigment","","titian-bacchus-ariadne","Where colors come from, and why some don't last · Paint is pigment held in a binder, and each pigment has its own strengths and weaknesses. The pages move from raw materials to the palette, then to what binder, grinding and light do to a color. The last page compares building color in layers with laying it on all at once. · Pigments from earth, minerals, dyes and modern chemistry · Limited and full palettes · How binder, grinding and light change a color · Glazing and direct painting · Binder · The oil, egg or gum that holds pigment and makes it stick to a surface. · Titian · Bacchus and Ariadne · 1520–23"],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-where-pigments-come-from.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","Where Pigments Come From","","ct-pigment-sources","A pigment is a colored powder that does not dissolve in its binder; painters' pigments come from colored earths, crushed minerals, plant and insect dyes, and chemistry. · Ground minerals can keep something of the stone's color. Ultramarine is made from lapis lazuli, and azurite is a copper ore. Dyes from madder root or cochineal insects must first be made into lakes. From the early 1700s chemists added new colors; Prussian blue was among the first. · Read the tube: the pigment name says what is inside, and 'hue' marks a substitute. · Painters choose by more than color: opacity, drying time, cost and permanence all differ. · Several historic pigments are toxic, among them vermilion, a mercury compound, and lead white. · Lake · A pigment made by fixing a dye onto a fine, nearly colorless powder."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/bacchus-and-ariadne.html","Paint and Pigment","Bacchus and Ariadne","Titian","titian-bacchus-ariadne","Titian · 1520–23 · Oil on canvas, 176.5 × 191 cm. The National Gallery, London · Each of the strongest pigments then sold in Venice gets its own broad area of the canvas. Each is mixed only with white, and each is used at full strength. · Ultramarine, the strongest color in the picture, fills the sky, fading from deep blue at the top to a paler band above the horizon. · The distant hills are azurite, a slightly greener blue that keeps them apart from the sky. · The rust-brown tree at upper right is iron-oxide ochre, brown on purpose in a spring landscape; Titian may have wanted a warm contrast in that corner. · Skin tones, from pale Ariadne to a satyr's dark russet, recur across the canvas and tie the strong colors together."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-limited-palettes.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","Limited Palettes","","ct-limited-palette","A limited palette is a deliberately small set of paints from which every color in a picture is mixed. · Because every mixture shares the same few ingredients, the colors hang together almost by themselves. The cost is range: without a true blue, cool colors can only be suggested, by setting grays from black and white beside warm reds and ochres. · Try it: paint a small study from white, ochre, a red and black, then add one paint at a time. · In the diagram's middle row, every mixture comes from the four paints above it. · Clear greens, violets and bright blues are out of reach, so landscapes and flowers usually need more paints."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/frieda-schiff-1876-1958-later-mrs-felix-m-warburg.html","Paint and Pigment","Frieda Schiff (1876–1958), Later Mrs. Felix M. Warburg","Anders Zorn","zorn-frieda-schiff","Anders Zorn · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 101 × 76.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A pink gown, warm skin and a background of dark red-brown at right and pale gray-beige at left keep the picture mostly warm. The coolest notes are the soft grays of the patterned hanging. · The pink of her first evening gown goes on in quick strokes that change length and direction. · Tradition says he used only four colors: white, yellow ochre, vermilion and black. · Research published by the Zorn Museum in 2023, based on his paints, palettes and orders, found more, including cobalt blue, viridian and cadmium yellow. · The museum record does not list the paints in this canvas, so its exact palette is not known."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-how-pigments-behave.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","How Pigments Behave","","ct-pigment-behavior","The same pigment can look different depending on the binder that holds it, how finely it is ground and how long it has been exposed to light. · In oil, azurite can darken or drift toward green, while ultramarine keeps its hue but looks darker. A mineral ground finer scatters more white light and turns paler. Light breaks down dyes. Red lakes are among the least stable, so pinks drain toward white and violets mixed with them turn bluer. · Look for passages that seem oddly pale, dark or cool beside the rest of a picture. · Choose lightfast pigments; the ASTM standards for artists' paints set lightfastness requirements. · Fading cannot be reversed. Museums mainly slow it by keeping light low and filtering out ultraviolet."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/irises.html","Paint and Pigment","Irises","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-irises","Vincent van Gogh · 1890 · Oil on canvas, 73.7 × 92.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Van Gogh planned this bouquet as a soft harmony: violet irises on a pink ground, tied together by green leaves. Fading has upset that balance. · He painted a second irises canvas as its opposite: violet flowers against a bright lemon yellow. · In a May 1890 letter he set them side by side: the yellow one built on complements that strengthen each other, this one on related greens, pinks and violets. · Both the pink and the violets contained a red pigment that has faded. The flowers now read bluer and the ground near-white, far from the soft effect he described. · The yellow-ground version is in the Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam. The Met reunited the four bouquets in 2015 to show what fading had changed."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-glazing-and-direct-painting.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","Glazing and Direct Painting","","ct-glazing","Glazing builds color in thin transparent layers over dry paint; direct painting mixes each color on the palette and lays it on opaque, often wet into wet. · Light passes through a glaze and reflects off the layer below, giving a glow an opaque mixture cannot match. Direct painting trades that depth for speed and freshness, since each stroke shows its final color at once. · Glazed darks look deep and jewel-like; direct painting shows solid, often thick strokes. · Transparent pigments such as red lakes suit glazing; white makes them opaque and chalkier. · Each glaze must dry before the next goes on, so the method is slow. · Alla prima · Italian for 'at the first attempt': a painting finished in one wet session."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/a-muse-calliope.html","Paint and Pigment","A Muse (Calliope?)","Cosimo Tura","tura-muse","Cosimo Tura · Probably 1455–60 · Oil over egg tempera on wood (poplar), 116.2 × 71.1 cm. The National Gallery, London · A crimson mantle, a dark blue dress, a green skirt and red sleeves shot with gold glow from layered paint. Oil was still uncommon in Italy in the 1450s, and here it carries the final layers of color. · Under the oil lies an earlier, partly finished design in egg tempera. · The richest passages are built up in translucent oil layers over more solid paint, an effect beyond egg tempera. · For pale colors he chose walnut oil, the less yellow of the two oils he used, to keep them bright."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/composition-and-meaning.html","Composition and Meaning · Overview","Composition and Meaning","","gauguin-day-of-god","What color does in a picture beyond describing things · A painter can use color to build depth, set a mood or send a message. Some of these effects rest on how the eye works; others rest on habits a culture teaches. Four paintings, from a seventeenth-century landscape to a modern image of anxiety, show each one at work. · Haze, value and hue as cues to distance · Color taken from life versus color invented · Palettes chosen to steer a viewer's feelings · Colors that stand for ideas, and who can read them · Paul Gauguin · Mahana no atua (Day of the God) · 1894"],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-color-and-space.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Color and Space","","ct-color-space","Atmospheric perspective, also called aerial perspective, shows depth by painting distant things lighter, bluer and lower in contrast than near ones. · Air scatters short-wavelength blue light more than other colors, so far-off dark forms take on a bluish cast. Thick haze from moisture or dust whitens the distance instead. Hue can also push a flat shape forward or back: on black, most viewers see red as nearer than blue, though some see the reverse. · Compare a distant ridge with the sky behind it; the value gap narrows as distance grows. · Landscape painters keep their darkest darks and sharpest edges in front and veil the background. · That red-blue effect is weaker on a white ground and tends to reverse, so it depends on the setting."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/sunrise.html","Composition and Meaning","Sunrise","Claude Lorrain","claude-sunrise","Claude Lorrain · possibly 1646–47 · Oil on canvas, 102.9 × 134 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Today the depth reads mostly through light: the brightest values sit at the low horizon, and the land steps darker toward the viewer. A pale blue-gray mountain at the left marks the farthest distance. · The Met's catalogue credits the low horizon, thin pink clouds and widely spaced, see-through trees for the sense of light and breadth. · The foreground and middle ground have darkened with age, a not uncommon change in Claude's canvases, while the horizon kept its glow; the steps from near to far are now harder to see. · Claude set his idealized countryside near Rome in the light of sunrise or sunset. A low sun puts the lightest passage at the most distant point."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-local-and-expressive-color.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Local and Expressive Color","","ct-local-expressive","Local color is the color an object has in plain daylight, apart from its surroundings; expressive color is a color the painter chooses for effect, whether or not the object could have it. · Between the two lies observed color: the object as actually lit and changed by its neighbors. Where a painter sits on this range tells the viewer how literally to take the picture. · Ask whether any real light could produce a color; in ordinary daylight, a pink hill under a green sky could not. · Painters often keep drawing and values believable while freeing hue, so the scene still reads. · Local color alone tends to look flat because it ignores light; many painters treat it as a starting point."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/mahana-no-atua-day-of-the-god.html","Composition and Meaning","Mahana no atua (Day of the God)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-day-of-god","Paul Gauguin · 1894 · Oil on linen canvas, 68 × 91 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · The pool along the bottom is not water-colored at all: it breaks into flat shapes of yellow, orange, pink, blue and near-black. The bank above it is pink. · He painted it from memory and fantasy rather than from a scene in front of him. · The Art Institute ranks it among his most abstract works; the pool's interlocking color zones show why. · The idol at the center is green and blue-black, unlike the warm browns of the people around it. · Farther back, sea, hills and sky stay nearer to natural color, so the invented foreground stands out."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-color-and-emotion.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Color and Emotion","","goethe-schiller-temperamentenrose","Color carries emotion when a painter sets hue, value and saturation to push viewers toward a feeling rather than to describe what is there. · Goethe and Schiller's 1798–99 rose matched hues to temperaments. Yet the effect comes from the whole scheme more than any one hue: how strong the colors are, how far apart their values sit, and whether they clash or settle. · Cover the subject and ask what the colors alone suggest before reading the story. · Painters often push one property hard, such as saturation or value contrast, and keep the rest quiet. · A palette alone rarely fixes a feeling; subject, drawing and title steer how viewers read it."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/the-scream.html","Composition and Meaning","The Scream","Edvard Munch","munch-scream","Edvard Munch · 1893 · Tempera and crayon on cardboard, 91 × 73.5 cm. Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo · Bands of red, orange and yellow fill the sky above the fjord, and the same curving strokes run through land, water and figure. The museum calls the colors non-realistic and ties them to the subject, anxiety. · In notebook lines written before the painting, Munch recalled a sunset sky turning blood red, with flames over the blue-black fjord. · A year earlier he used the same colors, format and landscape in Sick Mood at Sunset; chiefly the central figure changed. · In the later painted version at the Munch Museum, cadmium yellow strokes in the sky have faded to off-white, mainly from damp, not light. · Munch also made a black-and-white lithograph of the motif in 1895; some impressions were colored by hand in watercolor."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-color-and-symbol.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Color and Symbol","","goethe-farbenkreis-1809","A color becomes a symbol when a culture attaches a set meaning to it, so the color can be read as a sign. · The meaning can come from tradition, from the cost of the material, or both. European painters often gave the Virgin Mary a mantle of ultramarine, the most expensive blue, which honored her and showed what a patron could afford. · A symbol needs a viewer who knows the code; without it, the same blue is just blue. · Painters can use a traditional color to name a figure at a glance, before any detail is read. · Meanings differ by culture and period: Goethe's 1809 circle calls red “schön” (beautiful) and blue “gemein” (common)."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/the-virgin-in-prayer.html","Composition and Meaning","The Virgin in Prayer","Sassoferrato","sassoferrato-virgin-prayer","Sassoferrato · 1640–50 · Oil on canvas, 73 × 57.7 cm. The National Gallery, London · Veil, dress and mantle supply nearly the whole palette: a creamy white, a rose red and an intense blue. The blue is ultramarine, the strongest color on the canvas. · Set against a background close to black and lit from one side, the blue looks brighter still. · In a 2014 National Gallery talk, curator Caroline Campbell linked ultramarine's intense blue to the meaning of devotional pictures like this. · According to the National Gallery, the mantle was once covered with cobalt blue, a softer color more to Victorian taste; taking that layer off uncovered the ultramarine. · The same pigment could carry a worldly meaning: in Pierre Mignard's 1691 portrait of the Marquise de Seignelay, also in London, a lavish ultramarine robe displays her wealth."],[2,"/study/composition.html","Start","Composition","","","Composition"],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/a-short-history.html","A Short History · Overview","A Short History","","dow-masters-harmonies","From Renaissance windows to Japanese notan · Advice on composition goes back six centuries: a window in the Renaissance, rules in the academies, and a new vocabulary from Japan. · Alberti's Window · The Academy's Rules · Dow, Japan and Notan · Plate · Dark and Light Harmonies from the Masters · 1914"],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-albertis-window.html","A Short History · History","Alberti's Window","","poore-picture-vision","In 1435 Leon Battista Alberti wrote that a painting works like an open window onto its subject. · He made composition one of the painter's three tasks: fitting the parts into one picture. Poore's drawing shows a related idea: the frame as a cut from the whole field of view. · To copy a view, Alberti advised looking through a thin veil divided by threads into squares. · Raphael ruled the Garvagh Madonna into four equal parts; the center line became the Virgin's axis (National Gallery, London)."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-the-academys-rules.html","A Short History · History","The Academy's Rules","","poore-chiaroscuro-forms","From the 1600s, academies and writers set rules for good pictures. · The French Royal Academy, founded in 1648, ranked subjects from history painting down to still life. · In 1783 Reynolds described blotting Venetian pictures: no more than a quarter light, a quarter dark, half in between. · In 1797 J. T. Smith coined a \"rule of thirds\": over two-thirds in shadow, and sky, land or water split about 2 to 1. · He meant a ratio of areas and lines, not today's grid of nine equal boxes."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-dow-japan-and-notan.html","A Short History · History","Dow, Japan and Notan","","dow-hokusai-three-values","Japanese prints reached Western artists after Japan reopened to trade in the 1850s. · Painters such as Degas borrowed their elongated formats and off-center layouts. In Boston, Arthur Wesley Dow built a course shaped by Japanese art; his book Composition appeared in 1899. · He taught three elements: line, notan and color. · Notan, Japanese for \"dark-light,\" is the arrangement of light and dark shapes. · His preface credits Ernest Fenollosa of Boston's Museum of Fine Arts, who lectured in Dow's first such classes, in 1889."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/the-frame.html","The Frame · Overview","The Frame","","poore-view-metre","The shape of the picture, and where its edges cut · The first decisions come before any subject: the shape of the picture and what its edges leave out. · Format and the Picture Plane · Cropping and the Edge · Plate · The View-Metre · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-format-and-the-picture-plane.html","The Frame · Concept","Format and the Picture Plane","","dow-three-proportions","Format is the shape of the picture: horizontal, vertical, square or round. The picture plane is its flat surface. · Dow redrew one house and tree in three formats; in the tallest, the tree towers. Every picture is both a view into space and a flat pattern on a surface. · Pattern \"is often in conflict with pictorial space,\" says Steven Zucker of Smarthistory. · In a 2014 talk, curator Simon Kelly suggested painters in the late 1800s moved from picture-as-window to picture-as-surface."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/view-of-ipswich-or-a-bend-in-the-river.html","The Frame","View of Ipswich, or A Bend in the River","Arthur Wesley Dow","dow-view-of-ipswich","Arthur Wesley Dow · ca. 1895 · Color woodcut, image 12.7 × 5.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The Met links this tall, narrow frame, only about 13 cm high, to Japanese pillar prints. · Look how the narrow format stacks the landscape from bottom to top. · No black key block outlines the forms; flat patches of color sit side by side."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/the-alba-madonna.html","The Frame","The Alba Madonna","Raphael","raphael-alba-madonna","Raphael · c. 1510 · Oil on panel transferred to canvas, diameter 94.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A round picture, called a tondo, is \"really hard\" to fill comfortably, says Smarthistory's Beth Harris. · Look at the Virgin's bent leg and elbow and John's fur garment: the National Gallery of Art notes how they echo the round edge. · Smarthistory's Steven Zucker notes the figures \"don't feel cramped.\" · The circle, the museum adds, evokes the heavens."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-cropping-and-the-edge.html","The Frame · Concept","Cropping and the Edge","","dow-rectangles-various-shape","Cropping is where the edge of the frame cuts the scene, and what it leaves out. · Dow framed one stand of trees five ways, and each edge slices the trunks differently. · Degas took inventive cropping from Japanese prints, the Met notes. · He also saw sixteenth-century painters sometimes cut off part of a figure."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/a-woman-seated-beside-a-vase-of-flowers-madame-paul-valpincon.html","The Frame","A Woman Seated beside a Vase of Flowers (Madame Paul Valpinçon?)","Edgar Degas","degas-woman-seated-beside-a-vase-of-flowers","Edgar Degas · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 73.7 × 92.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The woman is pushed to the right and cut off by the edge, while a large bouquet takes the center. · Look at the lower right corner: only a sliver of her hand is left in. · Met curator Alison Hokanson describes her as \"squished in at the right.\" · She argues Degas planned such skewed layouts to give an effect of chance."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/paris-street-rainy-day.html","The Frame","Paris Street; Rainy Day","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-paris-street-rainy-day","Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 212.2 × 276.2 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · The main figures stand off-center, and the man at the right edge is cut in half. · The Art Institute suggests photography may lie behind the bold cropping. · The central couple look toward something outside the frame. · Yet nothing back there is accidental: the Art Institute says each distant figure was positioned to make the street feel crowded."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/shape-and-value.html","Shape and Value · Overview","Shape and Value","","dow-massing-two-values","Shapes, gaps and the pattern of dark and light · Under the subject, every picture is a pattern of shapes and of dark and light. Strong pictures plan both. · Positive and Negative Space · Notan and Value Massing · Emphasis and Focal Point · Plate · Massing in Two Values, from Corot, Daubigny and Hokusai · 1914"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-positive-and-negative-space.html","Shape and Value · Concept","Positive and Negative Space","","dow-notan-figure-ground","Positive space is the subject; negative space is the area around it. · The gaps are shapes too. In Dow's black-and-white panels, neither tone is simply background: both are designed. · Dow wrote that good notan \"is dependent upon good spacing.\" · A plain background makes a figure stand out; in a 2014 talk, curator Simon Kelly suggested Van Gogh took this from Japanese prints."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/laziness-la-paresse.html","Shape and Value","Laziness (La Paresse)","Félix Vallotton","vallotton-laziness","Félix Vallotton · 1896 · Woodcut, image 17.8 × 22.2 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · There is no gray here: only black ink and bare paper. · The Art Institute notes that black and white alone give him pattern, space and solid form. · Look at the pale body: it is mostly bare paper, shaped by the black around it."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-notan-and-value-massing.html","Shape and Value · Concept","Notan and Value Massing","","dow-notan-spotting","Value massing groups a picture's lights and darks into a few big shapes, in two or three values: Dow's notan. · Here Dow cut pictures by Manet, Millet, Corot and others down to flat black and white; what survives is the design. For value itself, see Color Theory. · Dow kept notan apart from light and shadow: it is a pattern the artist designs, not a fact of nature. · It holds, he wrote, \"whether colored or not.\""],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Shape and Value","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-under-the-wave-off-kanagawa","Katsushika Hokusai · ca. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 37.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hokusai builds the scene from a handful of dark blue and pale shapes. · Look inside the wave: its dark blue gathers into broad bands. · White foam and pale sky make the light masses around them. · Behind Fuji, a band of dark gray sky sets off its white peak."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-emphasis-and-focal-point.html","Shape and Value · Concept","Emphasis and Focal Point","","poore-lion-desert","The focal point is the part that draws the most attention. Emphasis is how the painter makes it do so. · The main tools are contrast, isolation and placement. In Gérôme's desert, one small dark shape alone in an empty field stands out. · J. T. Smith (1797) advised one main light, with the others smaller and dimmer: \"Two distinct, equal lights, should never appear in the same picture.\" · Reynolds wrote that a light looks brightest when the most shade surrounds it; Color Theory explains why."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-night-watch.html","Shape and Value","The Night Watch","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-night-watch","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1642 · Oil on canvas, 379.5 × 453.5 cm. Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam (on loan from the City of Amsterdam) · In a crowd of guardsmen, a few lit details stand out. · The Rijksmuseum points to two: the captain's gesturing hand and a young girl in the background. · The museum identifies the girl as the company's mascot. · It also calls Rembrandt the first to show the sitters in a group portrait \"actually doing something.\""],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-denial-of-saint-peter.html","Shape and Value","The Denial of Saint Peter","Caravaggio","caravaggio-denial-of-saint-peter","Caravaggio · 1610 · Oil on canvas, 94 × 125.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The Met notes that his late paintings get their force from strong light against near-total darkness. · The Met reads the hands as three accusations: the soldier's pointing finger and the woman's two fingers, matching Peter's three denials. · Light catches faces, hands, the helmet and the robes; the room behind stays dark. · The Met calls it \"a marvel of narrative concision.\""],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/balance-and-structure.html","Balance and Structure · Overview","Balance and Structure","","poore-boats-balance","How a picture holds steady · Balance keeps a picture from tipping to one side. Underneath, a simple structure can hold the parts together. · Symmetric Balance · Asymmetric Balance and Visual Weight · Radial Balance · Armatures · Thirds, Grids and the Golden Ratio · Plate · Four Sailboat Sketches (untitled figure) · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-symmetric-balance.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Symmetric Balance","","poore-salute-wounded","Balance is how a picture's parts are spread so it feels steady. Symmetric balance mirrors one side in the other around a central axis. · Poore drew a center line through Detaille's scene to show two nearly equal groups. Dow called symmetry \"the most common and obvious way of satisfying the desire for order.\" · Dow noted it turns up in \"cheap and mean design\" where beauty of form is ignored. · Poore linked strict, equal balance more with decoration than with pictures."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/madonna-and-child-enthroned-with-saints-colonna-altarpiece.html","Balance and Structure","Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints (Colonna Altarpiece)","Raphael","raphael-colonna-altarpiece","Raphael · ca. 1504 · Oil and gold on wood, main panel 172.4 × 172.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Look for the mirror: the Virgin sits on the central axis, with God the Father centered above. · Two female saints flank the throne; two weighty male saints stand in front, one on each side. · In the arched panel on top, angels and seraphim flank God the Father in the same way."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-asymmetric-balance-and-visual-weight.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Asymmetric Balance and Visual Weight","","poore-steelyard","Asymmetric balance uses unlike sides that still feel even. Visual weight is how strongly any part pulls on the eye. · Poore compared it to a steelyard, a scale where a small weight far from the pivot balances a big one close to it. · A shape alone weighs more than the same shape in a group, Poore said. · Strong contrast adds weight, Poore noted; the Getty adds color and texture. · The small balancing weight must not become a rival point of interest."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair.html","Balance and Structure","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · Oil on canvas, 89.5 × 129.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The big blue chair and the sprawling girl sit right of center; a small dark dog in a matching chair and an open stretch of floor answer them at left. · Cassatt moved the dog from the floor up to the chair, the National Gallery of Art found. · She wrote that Degas advised her; he added the room's corner, a wide diagonal that deepens the space."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-radial-balance.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Radial Balance","","dow-radial-circles","In radial balance, parts spread out from a central point, like spokes from a hub. · Dow's four two-value wheels keep one hub and vary the spokes and rings. Poore counted the circle and radiating lines among his basic forms. · The parts around the center are often alike. · A round format suits it, but a round picture need not be radial. · Look for it in rose windows, wheels and mandalas."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/mandala-of-jnanadakini.html","Balance and Structure","Mandala of Jnanadakini","Unknown artist, Tibet","mandala-of-jnanadakini","Unknown artist, Tibet · late 14th century · Distemper (glue-based paint) on cloth, image 74.9 × 83.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The six-armed goddess sits at the exact center, ringed by eight smaller figures. · Rings of lotus petals, thunderbolt symbols and flames circle outward from them. · Colors mark the four quarters, one for each direction. · Four guardian goddesses stand in the gateways."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-armatures.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Armatures","","poore-fundamental-forms","An armature is a simple shape that holds a picture's main masses together: a triangle, circle, cross, S-curve or L. · Poore's chart sets each form beside small pictures built on it. The triangle means stability; in landscape, he said, it lies on its side, pointing into the distance. · The L-shape, he thought, is the only form that needs an opposing mass to balance it. · An armature found by drawing over a print is a reading, not proof of the artist's plan."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-harvesters.html","Balance and Structure","The Harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1565 · Oil on oak, 119 × 162 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A stooping reaper, the Met's catalogue notes, forms a triangle that echoes the wheat she is cutting. · Look for the same triangle in the sheaves standing in the field. · The view itself is built: Flanders is flat, so \"this is not a real landscape,\" says the Met's Adam Eaker."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-thirds-grids-and-the-golden-ratio.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Thirds, Grids and the Golden Ratio","","poore-strong-points","Placement rules promise the best spots in a frame; the evidence is thin. · Poore's grid argues that the center is weak and unequal divisions, such as thirds, are strong: a period opinion. · In 727 paintings, the rule of thirds \"seems to play only a minor, if any, role\" (2014 study). · In UC Berkeley tests, viewers preferred a single front-facing object centered. · Martin Kemp knows of no Renaissance drawing with a hidden golden ratio. · Lab tests find a real but fragile preference for it (Green, 1995)."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/circus-sideshow-parade-de-cirque.html","Balance and Structure","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus-sideshow","Georges Seurat · 1887–88 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 149.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Golden-ratio claims about this painting come from measuring it. Roger Fischler's 1981 study found no sign Seurat used the ratio. · Look at the stiff horizontals, verticals and rows of lights: easy to fit a grid to, which is why overlays tempt. · Simple ratios such as 5:8 sit close to the golden ratio, so a near fit proves little. · His real planning shows in his studies for La Grande Jatte; see Checking a Composition."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/movement-and-depth.html","Movement and Depth · Overview","Movement and Depth","","poore-hogarth-line","Moving the eye across and into the picture · Lines, repeats and depth cues move the eye across the surface and back into space. How well they steer it is a fair question. · Line and Direction · Rhythm and Repetition · Leading the Eye · Depth by Design · Plate · Hogarth's Line of Beauty · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-line-and-direction.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Line and Direction","","poore-aesthetics-of-line","Lines, edges and gestures point the eye in a direction, and each direction can carry a mood. · Poore matched pictures to kinds of line: vertical for dignity, horizontal for calm, curve for movement, diagonal for speed. · The Getty defines movement as \"the path the viewer's eye takes through the work of art, often to focal areas.\" · In Leonardo's Last Supper, the lines of the room meet on Christ, drawing attention to him (Smarthistory)."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-death-of-socrates.html","Movement and Depth","The Death of Socrates","Jacques Louis David","david-death-of-socrates","Jacques Louis David · 1787 · Oil on canvas, 129.5 × 196.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The figures spread left and right along the couch in a flat band the Met calls \"friezelike\" and \"planar horizontal.\" · Against that calm horizontal, Socrates' raised arm points upward. · His other hand reaches for the cup of hemlock. · The disciple handing it to him turns away, unable to look. · Plato sits at the foot of the bed, closing the line at left."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-rhythm-and-repetition.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Rhythm and Repetition","","dow-repetition","Repetition uses the same line or shape again. Rhythm is what it creates: a sense of organized movement. · Dow called repetition probably the oldest form of design. Equal spacing gives a pattern, like his border; uneven spacing is what you find in landscape, like his gables. · Some variety, the Getty adds, keeps a rhythm lively. · Look for repeats in a picture's edges and gaps, not only its objects."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/sudden-shower-over-shin-ohashi-bridge-and-atake.html","Movement and Depth","Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-sudden-shower","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 34 × 24.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Rain falls in rows of fine parallel lines across the whole print. · Under it, the bridge cuts a long diagonal, with small figures hurrying across. · The bridge's posts repeat in a second, slower beat below the deck."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-leading-the-eye.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Leading the Eye","","poore-shepherdess-exit","Painters plan where the eye goes. Eye-tracking studies test whether it does. · Poore wanted one entrance and one exit for the eye. His Millet example has two, which he counted a fault: \"a case of divided interest.\" · In 1935 Buswell recorded 200 viewers' eye movements and found large differences between people. · A 2020 study of landscape art found composition pulls the eye to the main elements, but the order of looking varies."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/young-woman-with-a-water-pitcher.html","Movement and Depth","Young Woman with a Water Pitcher","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-young-woman-with-a-water-pitcher","Johannes Vermeer · ca. 1662 · Oil on canvas, 45.7 × 40.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Light from the window at left falls on the headdress and collar, then turns the pitcher and basin gold. · On the Met's audio guide, the light is said to be \"guiding me around the room.\" · A dark, shadowy corner at lower left, the guide adds, lets you discover the picture a little at a time. · Notice which part you looked at first."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-depth-by-design.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Depth by Design","","comp-depth","Depth cues make a flat surface read as space. The painter decides which to use and how strongly. · Overlap puts one thing in front of another, and size shrinks things as they recede. Linear perspective runs receding lines to a vanishing point at the viewer's eye level. A near-far jump leaves out the middle ground. · Brunelleschi worked out linear perspective around 1420, and Alberti's book on painting explained it. · Color and distance are covered in Color Theory."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/plum-estate-kameido.html","Movement and Depth","Plum Estate, Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-plum-estate-kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Woodblock print, image 34 × 22.6 cm. Brooklyn Museum, New York · A plum tree fills the foreground, close up, and the orchard beyond is seen through its branches. · Look past the trunk: small figures stand along a fence among the distant trees. · Van Gogh copied the print in 1887, keeping its composition."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/unity.html","Unity · Overview","Unity","","poore-stable-interior","Making the parts one picture, then testing it · The last step is making every part belong to one whole, at the right size, and then checking the result with fresh eyes. · Unity and Variety · Proportion and Scale · Checking a Composition · Plate · Stable Interior — A. Mauve · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/theme-unity-and-variety.html","Unity · Concept","Unity and Variety","","poore-unity","Unity is the sense that every part belongs to one whole. Variety adds difference to hold attention. · Poore set a single, tight group beside a picture split between two centers. \"Of all pictorial principles,\" he wrote, \"none compares in importance with Unity or Balance.\" · Echoing a shape or angle in several places \"helps to unify the composition,\" says Beth Harris of Smarthistory. · Poore quotes the painter George Inness on his own struggle for \"unity, unity.\""],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884.html","Unity","A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","Georges Seurat","seurat-grande-jatte","Georges Seurat · 1884–86 · Oil on canvas, 207.5 × 308.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Dozens of different figures are held in one ordered scene. · The Art Institute says Seurat meant it as a modern answer to a classical Greek frieze. · He reworked it two years later, making some figures bigger and adding new ones, such as the monkey on a leash. · Look for the repeats that bind it: upright figures in profile, parasols, curving hats and shadows."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/theme-proportion-and-scale.html","Unity · Concept","Proportion and Scale","","dow-rectangles-proportion","Proportion is how sizes and amounts relate inside a picture. Scale is how big something is next to something else, or next to us. · Dow made proportion, or \"good spacing,\" the principle behind all his others. His tracings divide rectangles unequally. · A large painting pushes viewers back; a small one draws them in (Smarthistory). · In Bellini's Madonna of the Meadow, Smarthistory notes, the Virgin takes up a third of the picture and stands higher than the horizon, which marks her as the subject."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/view-from-mount-holyoke-northampton-massachusetts-after-a-thunderstorm-the-oxbow.html","Unity","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm — The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","cole-oxbow","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 130.8 × 193 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Smarthistory's Bryan Zygmont traces a diagonal from lower right to upper left that splits the view into two unequal parts: storm and wilderness at left, sunlit farmland at right. · Find the painter, tiny at lower center: he has stopped sketching to look out at us. Smarthistory calls him \"easily overlooked.\" · Next to the huge valley, that small figure sets the scale."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/theme-checking-a-composition.html","Unity · Concept","Checking a Composition","","dow-two-value-sketches","Painters check a design by making it look strange: small, reversed, upside down or far away. · Dow told students to \"make many trial arrangements\" and keep the best; small two-value sketches are quick to compare. · Leonardo advised a mirror: reversed, a picture looks like another painter's work, so faults show. · Poore adds viewing from different distances and turning the picture upside down. · Neuroscientist Margaret Livingstone suggests the mirror and upside-down tricks work by stripping away depth cues, so you see flat shapes."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/oil-sketch-for-a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884.html","Unity","Oil Sketch for \"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884\"","Georges Seurat","seurat-oil-sketch-grande-jatte","Georges Seurat · 1884 · Oil on panel, 15.5 × 24.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · One of more than 20 painted studies, it looks at first like the layout of the final canvas. · Its 20-odd figures, though, \"have little to do with the final version,\" the Art Institute notes. · The trio at right was completely rethought. · Compare it with the finished painting under Unity and Variety."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Colour let loose, then line and paint pushed to the edge · In 1905 a group of painters in Paris, soon nicknamed the Fauves, the wild beasts, began using colour for its own force rather than to describe things. Within a few years painters in Dresden, Munich and Vienna pushed the same freedom further, into jagged line, raw paint and, by 1913, pictures with no objects at all. · Henri Matisse · Woman with a Hat · 1905"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/woman-with-a-hat.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Woman with a Hat","Henri Matisse","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 31 3/4 × 23 1/2 in. San Francisco Museum of Modern Art · Henri Matisse (French, 1869–1954). The face is built from patches of green, mauve, yellow and orange, with only a few touches the colour of skin. · A green stroke runs down the nose and along the jaw, with pink and yellow patches beside it. · The hat is a heap of blue, red, green and violet dabs that does not settle into any one object. · The background is scrubbed in loose strokes of turquoise, pink and green, with the pale ground showing through. · The dress and glove are painted as quickly as the background, so figure and setting carry the same weight. · Shown at the Salon d'Automne in Paris in 1905, the exhibition where the nickname was coined; the sitter is the artist's wife, Amélie."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/portrait-of-madame-matisse-the-green-line.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Portrait of Madame Matisse. The Green Line","Henri Matisse","matisse-the-green-line","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 16 × 12 3/4 in. Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen · A single stripe of green runs down the middle of the face and splits it into a warm yellow half and a cool pink half. · The green line runs from the hairline down the nose to the chin, and the yellow and pink halves meet along it without blending. · The background is cut into three flat fields: violet, orange-red and green, each meeting the next with a hard edge. · The hair is one dark blue mass with a few lighter strokes, not drawn strand by strand. · Thick strokes cover the whole surface, the background laid on as heavily as the face. · The museum dates it, with a 'presumably', to autumn 1905, after the summer at Collioure."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/open-window-collioure.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Open Window, Collioure","Henri Matisse","matisse-open-window-collioure","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 18 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The paint is laid on in loose, separate strokes, and bare canvas shows between them across much of the picture. · The view through the window is a patchwork of short pink, blue and white dabs for sea, sky and boats. · The left wall is green and the right wall is pink, two sides of the same room in opposite colours. · The window frames are striped in red, green and blue rather than painted as wood. · The flowerpots and leaves at the bottom are blobs of red, green and blue, each one or two strokes. · Shown at the 1905 Salon d'Automne; the view is of sailboats off Collioure, a town on the Mediterranean coast."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/mountains-at-collioure.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Mountains at Collioure","André Derain","derain-mountains-at-collioure","André Derain · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 39 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · André Derain (French, 1880–1954). The trees are made of separate dashes of pure blue, green and yellow, laid side by side without touching. · Each dash is one flat colour, so the foliage reads as a mosaic. · The tree trunks are bright red-orange lines, and the hills behind are flat zones of orange, blue and violet. · The sky is pale green and yellow with white clouds drawn in long curving strokes. · Nothing is shaded: light and shadow are replaced by changes of colour. · Derain and Matisse worked side by side at Collioure in summer 1905."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/le-bonheur-de-vivre.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Le Bonheur de vivre","Henri Matisse","matisse-joy-of-life","Henri Matisse · 1905–06 · Oil on canvas, 69 1/2 × 94 3/4 in. Barnes Foundation, Philadelphia · The figures and trees are flat areas of colour held inside long curving outlines, with almost no modelling. · The reclining women are pale pink shapes edged in red or green. · Tree trunks bend in sweeping arcs, and the foliage above is a set of flat green, pink and orange patches. · The ground shifts from yellow to orange to green in broad zones, each colour laid flat. · A ring of small dancers in the distance is painted as a loop of outlines on the yellow field. · Painted between October 1905 and March 1906 and shown at the 1906 Salon des Indépendants; bought there by Leo Stein."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/street-dresden.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Street, Dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-street-dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1908, reworked 1919 · Oil on canvas, 59 1/4 × 79 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Ernst Ludwig Kirchner (German, 1880–1938). The street is a field of acid pink, and the crowd presses to the front edge with green and yellow faces. · The pavement is a flat pink that tilts steeply upward, so there is no sense of a receding street. · The woman in the pale yellow blouse has a green, masklike face, and both women in front are rimmed with dark outlines around hats and shoulders. · Colours clash: orange coat against pink ground, lemon-yellow blouse against dark blue skirt. · The figures at the sides are cut off by the edges of the canvas, as if the crowd continued past the frame. · Kirchner helped found Die Brücke (The Bridge), a group of German painters, in Dresden in 1905; the street is the Königstrasse, and the canvas carries a date of 1907, a year before it was painted."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/franzi-in-front-of-carved-chair.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Fränzi in Front of Carved Chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-franzi-carved-chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1910 · Oil on canvas, 28 × 19 1/2 in. Museo Nacional Thyssen-Bornemisza, Madrid · The face is bright green with red lips, drawn in a few jagged dark strokes, and the chair behind is a black shape with a pink face in it. · The face is a flat yellow-green with blue around the eyes and pink patches on the cheeks and nose. · Outlines are thick, dark and angular, laid down fast, with the raw canvas showing at the edges. · The pink carved figure in the chair back sits directly above the girl's head, a second, smaller face stacked on top of hers. · The background is split into blocks of yellow, green and blue with no depth. · Fränzi was a girl from a working-class district of Dresden who posed for the Brücke painters; the chair was most likely carved by Kirchner himself."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/english-step-dancers.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","English Step Dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-english-step-dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1911 · Colour woodcut, image 16 × 10 3/4 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The lines are cut in a wood block, so every contour is stiff and splintery, and the colour comes in three flat inks printed with the black. · The black lines are thick, straight-sided and broken, the marks of a knife in wood rather than a brush. · Pink, green and ochre are printed as flat areas with no shading, and the pink sits slightly off the black. · The dancers' legs are long and angular, with the stripes of their tights cut as parallel gouges. · The plant behind is simplified to a few cut leaves, each a single shape. · Printed by the artist himself, as the inscription says; given to the Jena art society in 1918, seized from it in 1937 as 'degenerate art', and in New York by 1940."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-as-a-soldier.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait as a Soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-self-portrait-as-soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 27 1/4 × 25 in. Allen Memorial Art Museum, Oberlin College, Oberlin, Ohio · The face and uniform are cut into sharp planes, and the paint is dragged in hatched strokes that leave every edge hard. · The face is a yellow-ochre wedge with sunken blue eyes and a hard shadow down the nose. · The right sleeve ends in a flat red stump with no hand. · Blue uniform, orange wall and yellow skin are set side by side at full strength, with no blending. · The nude behind is painted in the same ochre as the face, so the two figures read as one colour against the dark ground. · Kirchner was inducted in 1915 and released for illness; in 1937 the painting hung in the Munich exhibition the Nazis called Degenerate Art."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/murnau-view-from-the-window-of-the-griesbrau.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Murnau – View from the Window of the Griesbräu","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-murnau-griesbraeu","Wassily Kandinsky · 1908 · Oil on cardboard, 19 1/2 × 27 1/2 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Wassily Kandinsky (Russian, 1866–1944). The village is melting into blocks of colour: the road is violet and pink, one roof is orange and the fields are flat green. · The road is a wide band of mauve and pink strokes filling the centre of the picture, with two tiny figures the only detail on it. · The houses are simplified to boxes of white, blue and ochre with the windows as short dark strokes. · Long brushstrokes run in one direction across each surface, giving every patch a visible grain. · The hills at the top are a thin band of dark blue and violet, with a thinner strip of yellow sky above them. · Painted from the window of the inn where Kandinsky and his friends lodged in summer 1908; the museum describes that stay as the moment their painting broke loose into free, expressive colour."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/portrait-of-the-dancer-alexander-sakharoff.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Portrait of the Dancer Alexander Sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky","jawlensky-alexander-sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky · 1909 · Oil on cardboard, 27 1/4 × 26 1/4 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Alexej von Jawlensky (Russian, 1864–1941). A thick dark contour wraps the red costume and black hair, and inside the lines the colour is flat. · The red costume is a single flat area, broken only by the darker red rose and green leaf at the chest and a dark blue shape at the bottom edge. · The face is chalk white with black-rimmed eyes and a red mouth, like stage make-up, and the eyes stare straight out. · The background is pale green brushed loosely, with brown cardboard showing through at the left. · The pose is frontal and symmetrical, head centred, shoulders square. · The museum records that Jawlensky painted it in less than thirty minutes when the dancer visited his studio in costume before a performance."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/blue-horse-i.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Blue Horse I","Franz Marc","marc-blue-horse-i","Franz Marc · 1911 · Oil on canvas, 44 × 33 1/4 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Franz Marc (German, 1880–1916). The horse is blue from mane to hoof, standing in hills of red, yellow and green that are just as unreal. · The animal is modelled in shades of blue and white, with a dark blue mane and a rounded, simplified body. · The landscape behind is a set of curved colour bands: pink sky, blue and violet hills, a yellow field, a red slope. · Complementary pairs sit next to each other: red against green at the bottom, blue horse against yellow ground. · Dark green leaves at the lower right are the sharpest shapes in a picture of soft curves. · Marc wrote to Macke in December 1910 that blue stood for the male principle, yellow for the female, red for matter; the museum says this picture shows that colour theory unusually clearly."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/lady-in-a-green-jacket.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Lady in a Green Jacket","August Macke","macke-lady-green-jacket","August Macke · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 17 1/4 × 17 in. Museum Ludwig, Cologne · August Macke (German, 1887–1914). The figures are smooth, rounded colour shapes with no faces, set in a landscape of equally smooth blocks. · The woman in green is seen from behind, her jacket one curved green form over a dark skirt. · The couple at the left and the woman in violet at the right are painted the same way, as coloured silhouettes. · The lake is a flat blue band, the path and houses are yellow and ochre blocks, and the trees are circles of green. · The surface is brushed in small, even strokes, so even the brightest colours stay calm. · Bought in 1947 for the Wallraf-Richartz-Museum in Cologne and transferred to the Museum Ludwig in 1976."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/improvisation-no-30-cannons.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Improvisation No. 30 (Cannons)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-improvisation-30-cannons","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 43 3/4 × 43 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · A few things can still be picked out, two cannons at the lower right, leaning towers at the top, but most of the canvas is colour and line with nothing to name. · Two black cannon barrels tilt up from the lower right corner, the clearest objects in the picture. · A cluster of tall buildings with a red tower leans at the upper right, drawn in thin dark lines over colour. · A big blue shape with a red outline runs diagonally through the centre, attached to nothing. · The colours are soft and watery in places, thick in others, and spread in blotches rather than filling shapes. · First shown in London in 1913; Kandinsky wrote to the collector Arthur Jerome Eddy that the cannons might reflect all the talk of war that year, but that the real content was what the viewer felt in the forms and colours."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/composition-vii.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Composition VII","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-composition-vii","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, about 79 × 118 in. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · Almost nothing can be named: the whole canvas is a storm of colour patches and black lines that pull toward the centre. · A dark, dense knot of black strokes and blue sits left of centre, and everything around it is lighter and looser. · Colour runs in every direction, with red, yellow, blue and green all at full strength and no single colour in charge. · Black lines, some thin and some thick, curve and break across the colours without enclosing them. · The edges are quieter, with large soft areas of cream, so the eye is pushed back toward the middle. · Painted in four days, 25 to 28 November 1913, after more than thirty studies; the museum notes faint echoes of Kandinsky's earlier pictures of the Apocalypse."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-laughing.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait, Laughing","Richard Gerstl","gerstl-laughing-self-portrait","Richard Gerstl · 1908 · Oil on canvas, 15 3/4 × 12 in. Belvedere, Vienna · Richard Gerstl (Austrian, 1883–1908). The paint is smeared and dabbed so thickly that the face is half dissolved into the brown dots behind it. · The background is a field of brown, ochre and green dabs, each a separate thick touch. · The face is smeared rather than dabbed, and its edges blur into the background at the right cheek and chin. · The open mouth and the two eyes are the only sharply drawn passages. · The jacket is a few dark smears at the bottom, barely indicated. · Gerstl taught himself to paint, kept apart from Vienna's artist groups and died at twenty-five; the museum reads this laugh as more menacing than cheerful."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-with-chinese-lantern-plant.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait with Chinese Lantern Plant","Egon Schiele","schiele-self-portrait-physalis","Egon Schiele · 1912 · Oil and opaque colour on wood, 12 3/4 × 15 3/4 in. Leopold Museum, Vienna · Egon Schiele (Austrian, 1890–1918). A hard, wiry contour runs around the head and shoulders, set against an almost empty pale ground. · The face is drawn with a fine dark line and shaded in streaks of grey, green and red, so the skin looks bruised rather than lit. · The head tilts one way and the eyes look the other, straight at the viewer. · The background is flat off-white with the brushstrokes showing, and the red lantern fruits on the left are the only bright colour. · The top edge cuts through the hair and the bottom edge through the chest, so the figure is cropped close. · Painted as a pair with Schiele's portrait of Wally Neuzil, which the museum says accounts for the odd framing of both pictures."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/death-and-maiden.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Death and Maiden","Egon Schiele","schiele-death-and-maiden","Egon Schiele · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 59 × 70 3/4 in. Belvedere, Vienna · The sheet and the ground are broken into jagged, crumpled shapes, and the dark robe and the ground share cracked earth colours. · The white sheet is a mass of sharp folds, each edge drawn with a dark line. · The woman's dress is a patchwork of red, pink and white marks, like a quilt of small splinters. · The ground behind is a field of brown and ochre lumps, with no horizon or sky except a thin pale strip at the top. · The two figures lie at an angle across the picture, and their poses look unstable, as if the embrace could slip apart. · The man is Schiele himself and the woman holding on to him is Wally Neuzil, whom he left to marry Edith Harms."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","Impressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","monet-impression","Impressionism is a way of painting that took shape in and around Paris from the late 1860s. Its painters first showed together in 1874, in an exhibition they organized themselves. · Claude Monet · Impression, Sunrise · 1872"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/olympia.html","Impressionism","Olympia","Édouard Manet","manet-olympia","Édouard Manet · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 51 1/2 × 75 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Édouard Manet (French, 1832–1883). Look at how little shading there is on the body: Manet paints it as a pale, nearly flat shape. · Light falls straight from the front, so there are almost no shadows. · The flowers and the shawl are quick, loose touches of color. · The whites of the sheets and pillows are laid on in broad strokes. · The dark green and brown background pushes the pale figure forward. · First shown at the Salon of 1865, the state-run exhibition in Paris, where it caused a scandal."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/la-grenouillere.html","Impressionism","La Grenouillère","Claude Monet","monet-grenouillere","Claude Monet · 1869 · Oil on canvas, 29 1/4 × 39 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Claude Monet (French, 1840–1926). Look at the water: it is built from separate strokes, not smoothly blended paint. · Short, curved strokes of blue, white, green and black sit side by side across the water. · Each stroke stays distinct, so the water seems to move and catch the light. · The figures on the round island are a few dark dabs each. · The trees behind are broad patches of yellow-green, with little detail. · Monet painted it at a riverside bathing spot near Paris, working beside Renoir."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/impression-sunrise.html","Impressionism","Impression, Sunrise","Claude Monet","monet-impression","Claude Monet · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 19 3/4 × 25 1/2 in. Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris · Look at how thin and quick the paint is: Monet painted it in a few hours. · Thin, watery paint lets the weave of the canvas show through in the upper sky. · The nearest boat is a single dark shape, with no detail. · The orange sun and its broken reflection are the brightest marks; orange streaks in the sky echo them. · Ships and cranes behind are faint blue-gray shapes in the haze. · Shown at the 1874 exhibition. A critic used its title to mock the group, and the name stuck."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-cradle.html","Impressionism","The Cradle","Berthe Morisot","morisot-cradle","Berthe Morisot · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 22 × 18 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Berthe Morisot (French, 1841–1895). Look at the veil over the cradle: it is painted in thin, see-through strokes. · Most of the canvas is white and pale pink, laid on in loose, open strokes. · The veil's edge runs on a diagonal from the woman's hand up to the top right. · Her dark blue dress and the dark wall at the top right frame the pale cradle. · Soft edges, more than outlines, separate the cradle from the curtains behind. · The sitter is Morisot's sister Edma, watching her baby daughter."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/white-frost.html","Impressionism","White Frost","Camille Pissarro","pissarro-hoarfrost","Camille Pissarro · 1873 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 36 3/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Camille Pissarro (French, 1830–1903). Look at the long tree shadows on the frosty field: they are blue, not brown or black. · The sunlit ground between the shadows is warm orange and tan. · Warm light next to cool shadow makes the field look cold and bright. · Small, thick dabs cover the furrows, giving the whole field an even texture. · Shown at the 1874 exhibition."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/boulevard-des-capucines.html","Impressionism","Boulevard des Capucines","Claude Monet","monet-capucines","Claude Monet · 1873–74 · Oil on canvas, 31 1/2 × 23 3/4 in. The Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City · Look down at the crowd: Monet paints each person with one or two strokes. · The street tilts up, so the pavement fills the lower half. · The bare trees are a haze of thin gray-violet strokes. · A cluster of pink balloons at the right is the brightest spot of warm color in a cool picture. · Monet painted this view twice; the version shown in 1874 was probably the other one."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/la-loge.html","Impressionism","La Loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 31 1/2 × 25 in. The Courtauld Gallery, London · Pierre-Auguste Renoir (French, 1841–1919). Look at the black stripes of the dress: black is used as a strong color, not just for shadow. · Black stripes and white silk make the strongest contrast in the picture. · The blacks are brushed with visible strokes, so they look soft, not flat. · The woman's face is smooth and clear, while the man behind her is looser and darker. · Pink flowers at her chest and in her hair add soft touches of color. · The models were Renoir's brother Edmond and a model known as Nini."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-dance-class.html","Impressionism","The Dance Class","Edgar Degas","degas-dance-class","Edgar Degas · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 32 3/4 × 30 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Edgar Degas (French, 1834–1917). Look at the empty floor at the lower right: the dancers are pushed to the left, the back and the right edge. · An empty stretch of floorboards runs from the lower right back into the room. · Figures crowd the left and right, and some are cut off by the frame. · A double bass and a music stand at the lower left cut into the view. · The white skirts are light, feathery strokes against the green wall. · The teacher leaning on a stick is the ballet master Jules Perrot."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/bal-du-moulin-de-la-galette.html","Impressionism","Bal du moulin de la Galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1876 · Oil on canvas, 51 3/4 × 69 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Look at the spots of light on the dancers: sun through the leaves is painted as loose, pale patches. · The patches cross the figures without following their shapes, so light and figure blend. · Dark blue jackets and hats make strong shapes among the pale patches. · Strokes of blue, pink and yellow repeat across the whole crowd, tying it together. · Some of Renoir's friends posed for it."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/paris-street-rainy-day.html","Impressionism","Paris Street; Rainy Day","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-rainy-day","Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 83 1/2 × 108 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Gustave Caillebotte (French, 1848–1894). Look at the deep space: a new Paris street runs far back, with figures close up at the front. · Building edges and paving stones run back to distant points, so the space feels deep. · A green lamppost splits the canvas into two halves. · The couple at the right is close and large; people across the square are tiny. · The paint is smooth and finely blended, like the finish taught in art schools. · It was the centerpiece of the group's 1877 exhibition, which Caillebotte did much to organize."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair.html","Impressionism","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-blue-armchair","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/4 × 51 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Mary Cassatt (American, 1844–1926). Look at the big blue shapes of the chairs: they fill the room and crowd the girl. · Blue armchairs and a sofa make large, rounded shapes across the canvas. · The gray floor is a plain, open shape between the chairs. · Quick, loose strokes of red, green and white mark the pattern on the chairs. · Her slumped body makes a long diagonal down the chair. · Cassatt wrote that Degas helped with it; he probably painted part of the background."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/summers-day.html","Impressionism","Summer's Day","Berthe Morisot","morisot-summers-day","Berthe Morisot · c. 1879 · Oil on canvas, 18 × 29 1/2 in. The National Gallery, London · Look at the speed of the strokes, from the zigzags on the dress to the flicks on the water. · Zigzag strokes run down the dress of the woman at the left. · On the water, quick flicks of paint run mostly side to side. · The far shore sits near the top edge, so water fills most of the picture. · The two women are cut off at the left and bottom edges. · Very likely shown in 1880 as The Lake in the Bois de Boulogne, a park in Paris."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/a-bar-at-the-folies-bergere.html","Impressionism","A Bar at the Folies-Bergère","Édouard Manet","manet-folies-bergere","Édouard Manet · 1882 · Oil on canvas, 37 3/4 × 51 1/4 in. The Courtauld Gallery, London · Look at the mirror behind the barmaid: her reflection and the man's are not where they should be. · The mirror fills the background, so the hall and crowd appear only as reflections. · Her reflection sits far to the right, though she faces us straight on. · A man in a top hat appears in the mirror but not in front of the bar. · Bottles and oranges in front are quick, sure strokes with bright highlights. · Manet made sketches at the hall, then painted the picture in his studio."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/woman-bathing-in-a-shallow-tub.html","Impressionism","Woman Bathing in a Shallow Tub","Edgar Degas","degas-shallow-tub","Edgar Degas · 1885 · Charcoal and pastel on paper, 32 × 22 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Look at the lines that build the body: Degas draws with color, in strokes you can count. · Pastel, sticks of dry color, is laid on in hatched lines across the back. · Pale green and lilac touches on the skin take the place of smooth shading. · The bent body folds into one compact, rounded shape over the tub. · Blue in the tub and ochre on the floor are rubbed in roughly, so the paper's grain shows. · One of a set of nudes Degas showed at the 1886 Impressionist exhibition."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer.html","Impressionism","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","monet-wheat-stacks","Claude Monet · 1890–91 · Oil on canvas, 23 1/2 × 39 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Look at the color of the light: this is one of a series, a group of pictures of one subject, painted in different seasons and light. · Low sun turns the edges of the stacks orange and pink. · The sky is pale yellow near the hills and pale green-blue above. · The same pinks, blues and greens are mixed through land, sky and stacks. · Thick strokes cover the whole surface; in the sky they all slant the same way. · Fifteen of the stack paintings hung together in one room at a Paris gallery in May 1891."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-childs-bath.html","Impressionism","The Child's Bath","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-childs-bath","Mary Cassatt · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 26 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Look at the flat patterns and clear outlines: Cassatt developed this style after an 1890 show of Japanese prints. · Broad stripes on the woman's dress make a bold, flat pattern. · Firm outlines trace the arms, legs and basin. · Carpet, wallpaper and chest add more patterns, packed edge to edge. · The skin is smooth and solid against the busy patterns around it. · First shown in Cassatt's 1893 exhibition at Durand-Ruel's gallery in Paris."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/rouen-cathedral-west-facade-sunlight.html","Impressionism","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, Sunlight","Claude Monet","monet-rouen-sunlight","Claude Monet · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 39 1/2 × 26 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Look at the surface: the stone front is built from thick, crusted paint. · The paint is rough and lumpy, like the stone it shows. · Up close, the carvings break into patches of white, yellow, blue and pink. · The façade fills almost the whole canvas; blue sky shows only between the towers. · Tiny figures at the lower left show the building's scale. · Monet worked on it in a room facing the cathedral, then finished it at Giverny."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/water-lilies.html","Impressionism","Water Lilies","Claude Monet","monet-water-lilies-1906","Claude Monet · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/2 × 37 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Look for the horizon: there is none, so the pond's surface fills the whole canvas. · Every edge of the canvas cuts through water or lily pads. · Reflected sky and trees show as soft blue and violet in the water. · Lily pads shrink and flatten toward the top, which suggests distance. · Pads and flowers are thick strokes of green, white and pink over thinner blue paint. · Shown in Paris in 1909, in an exhibition of Monet's water-lily paintings."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","Start","Japanese Woodblock Prints","","","Japanese Woodblock Prints"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/ink-line-to-hand-color.html","Ink Line to Hand Color · Overview","Ink Line to Hand Color","","anchi-courtesan-walking","Pictures for Edo's townspeople, c. 1680–1764 · Single-sheet prints grew out of Edo's illustrated books in the late 1600s and found buyers among the city's townspeople. For most of this period color was added by hand; printed color came only gradually. · Pictures cheap enough for city buyers to own · Courtesans and kabuki stars as favorite subjects · Named designers and the first family studios · Kaigetsudō Anchi · Courtesan Walking · c. 1714"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/a-parade-of-courtesans.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","A Parade of Courtesans","Hishikawa Moronobu (attributed)","moronobu-parade-courtesans","Hishikawa Moronobu (attributed) · c. 1690 · Woodblock print; ink on paper, 31 × 68 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A file of courtesans, each shaded by an attendant's parasol, seems to move through the Yoshiwara. · Unsigned and without a publisher's mark, it is attributed by the museum. · Moronobu is the first ukiyo-e designer known by name. · The figure at left is identified as a Soga brother, so the scene may draw on a kabuki play. · Edo · The old name of Tokyo, seat of the shoguns; it also names the Edo period, 1615–1868. · Ukiyo-e · “Pictures of the floating world”: prints and paintings of the city's pleasures, theater and fashions. · Yoshiwara · Edo's licensed pleasure quarter, a walled district of courtesans' houses, teahouses and entertainment. · Kabuki · Japan's popular stage drama, combining acting, music and dance. · Soga brothers · Twelfth-century heroes of a revenge story staged in many kabuki plays."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/the-entrance-to-ageya-machi.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","The Entrance to Ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu","moronobu-entrance-ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu · 1681–84 · Woodblock print; ink on paper, 25.7 × 38.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A samurai and his servant have just come through the great gate as two men joke with two women nearby. · From a set of Yoshiwara views; that gate, the Ōmon, was the only way in or out. · The figures stand on Nakamachi, the quarter's main street. · Black line alone renders the figures, roofs and lattices. · Ageya-machi · The Yoshiwara street of ageya, houses where clients met and entertained courtesans."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/on-the-yoshida-highway.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","On the Yoshida Highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed)","sugimura-yoshida-highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed) · c. 1685 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 59.1 × 29.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · An actor plays Miyako no O Kuni, the woman who founded kabuki, seated upstairs while one character shades another below. · Name tags identify the roles; the play and actors remain unknown. · Sugimura also helped popularize literary classics, John Carpenter notes. · Tan-e: orange-red lead color brushed on after printing, the first way color reached these prints."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/courtesan-walking.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Courtesan Walking","Kaigetsudō Anchi","anchi-courtesan-walking","Kaigetsudō Anchi · c. 1714 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 55.2 × 28.8 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Wind lifts a tall courtesan's robe, its fabric written over with a poem. · Look inside the robe: the poem's author is pictured on the lining. · The Art Institute contrasts its freedom with the rule-bound religious images of its day. · In early courtesan prints the costume drew as much attention as the face."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/kabuki-actor.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Kabuki Actor","Torii Kiyonobu I (attributed)","kiyonobu-kabuki-actor","Torii Kiyonobu I (attributed) · c. 1708 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 55.2 × 29.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A dancing actor, probably in a female role, whirls in a robe of swirling patterns and flowers. · Theater advertising was the studio's trade: kabuki posters and signboards. · The color was brushed on by hand over a sheet printed in black. · Thick, rounded outlines and flat pattern carry well from far off, a signboard painter's habit. · Torii · The family school of designers founded by Kiyonobu I. · I, II, V after a name · Generation numbers: stage and studio names passed from holder to holder, so Kiyonobu I is the first to use the name and Danjūrō V the fifth."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/courtesan-from-the-myogaya-house.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Courtesan from the Myōgaya House","Torii Kiyomasu I (attributed)","kiyomasu-courtesan-myogaya","Torii Kiyomasu I (attributed) · c. 1712 · Woodblock print (tan-e); ink and color on paper, 31.1 × 15.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A standing courtesan reads a poem slip; the inscription likens her to Ono no Komachi, the classical woman poet. · Her poem slip breaks off mid-line; its plum blossoms by a window hint at spring hope. · The comparison flattered the literary skill of many Yoshiwara courtesans. · Large ginger (myōga) shapes on her robes advertise her house."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/perspective-view-of-the-interior-of-the-nakamura-theater.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Perspective View of the Interior of the Nakamura Theater","Okumura Masanobu","masanobu-nakamura-theater","Okumura Masanobu · 1740 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 40.2 × 63.7 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A packed kabuki house seen from the rear, with Ichikawa Ebizō II on stage as Yanone Gorō. · A runway crossed the seats; many patrons stayed all day, eating and calling out praise. · Its hand-brushed palette keeps to yellow, red and red-brown. · Probably the first printmaker to use Western one-point perspective, a novelty for buyers."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/the-actor-sanogawa-ichimatsu-i-as-hisamatsu.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","The Actor Sanogawa Ichimatsu I as Hisamatsu","Ishikawa Toyonobu","toyonobu-sanogawa-ichimatsu","Ishikawa Toyonobu · c. 1748 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 71 × 26.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A star poses in a role from a 1743 Soga play, his jacket in a bold brown-and-black check. · Glossy black mixed with glue, called lacquer picture (urushi-e), deepens the darks. · Signed and sealed, with the publisher Urokogata-ya's seal. · The check caught on as a fashion through this actor."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/ono-no-komachi-washing-the-copybook.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Ono no Komachi Washing the Copybook","Torii Kiyomitsu I","kiyomitsu-komachi-copybook","Torii Kiyomitsu I · 1764 · Color woodblock print, 30.8 × 14 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · The poet washes an old manuscript to foil a rival who tried to discredit her. · Frank Lloyd Wright owned this impression before it came to the Art Institute. · He headed the Torii actor-print school in his day. · A water picture (mizu-e): colors printed from blocks with no black outlines, a year before full color."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/brocade-pictures-harunobu-and-1765.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765 · Overview","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","","harunobu-plum-blossoms-night","A decade of slender figures and soft color, 1765–1770s · In 1765 Edo printers began printing each color from its own carved block, one after another, so a sheet could carry many hues. Wealthy patrons first ordered them as New Year calendars, and the new prints became known as brocade pictures (nishiki-e). · Calendars, private commissions and the first full-color sheets · A delicate, youthful ideal of beauty · Old poems and legends in modern dress · Suzuki Harunobu · Woman Admiring Plum Blossoms at Night · c. 1766"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/young-woman-riding-a-phoenix.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Young Woman Riding a Phoenix","Suzuki Harunobu (attributed)","harunobu-young-woman-phoenix","Suzuki Harunobu (attributed) · 1765 · Color woodblock print, 20.5 × 27.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A girl in fashionable Edo dress rides a phoenix through the air, perhaps playing a Chinese immortal. · Look at her sleeve: numbers for the year's long months suggest a 1765 calendar. · Her panpipes suggest a flute-playing sage; the bird suggests another, often shown riding a phoenix. · If so, the print is a mitate. · Mitate · Playful parody or “likening”: a classical subject or legend recast in contemporary dress."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/visit-to-a-shrine-at-the-hour-of-the-ox.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Visit to a Shrine at the Hour of the Ox","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-shrine-hour-ox","Suzuki Harunobu · 1765 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 28.3 × 20.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Carrying hammer and nails to curse a faithless lover or a rival at about 2 a.m., a woman looks serene. · The straw doll and candle crown this rite usually calls for are missing. · A seal at left marks the private patron who commissioned it. · The carver, Sekine Kaei, is credited by name beside the designer."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/parody-of-minamoto-no-tametomo.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Parody of Minamoto no Tametomo","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-tametomo-calendar","Suzuki Harunobu · 1765 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 26.7 × 20 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A youth with a bow and arrows plays the archer-hero Tametomo as two women kneel beside him. · The year name and “Meiwa 2” flank his neckband. · Long-month numbers sit in his lining; short ones are white bamboo leaves on the dark robe by the screen. · Like other 1765 calendars, it hides the dates in the design. · Year name · One of sixty cyclical names for years, repeating every sixty years. · Meiwa 2 · The second year of the Meiwa era, 1765; Japan counted years by era names."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/evening-chime-of-the-clock.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Evening Chime of the Clock","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-evening-chime-clock","Suzuki Harunobu · c. 1766 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 28.3 × 17.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Fresh from the bath, a woman sits on the veranda; her maid turns as the clock strikes. · Slanting parallel lines carry the eye into the room's private space. · The chime may hint at a lover's coming visit. · From Eight Parlor Views: the temple bell of China's classic Eight Views becomes a household clock."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/woman-admiring-plum-blossoms-at-night.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Woman Admiring Plum Blossoms at Night","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-plum-blossoms-night","Suzuki Harunobu · c. 1766 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 32.4 × 21 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A young woman raises a lantern to plum blossoms in the dark, an old theme of East Asian verse. · The stylized cloud at top recalls the court art of the Heian period. · His elite merchant and samurai buyers admired Kyoto's literary past. · Blind embossing (karazuri) raises uncolored areas, like her socks and inner robes. · Heian · The era of 794–1185, when the imperial court flourished in Kyoto."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/lovers-walking-in-the-snow-crow-and-heron.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Lovers Walking in the Snow (Crow and Heron)","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-lovers-snow","Suzuki Harunobu · 1764–72 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 28.6 × 20.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Two lovers share an umbrella in falling snow, one in black, one in white. · Perhaps a michiyuki, a couple's last journey toward a love suicide. · Bright patterned under-robes show at collars, sleeves and hems. · The design was re-cut during or soon after his lifetime; this is an early impression."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/osen-of-the-kagiya-teahouse.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Osen of the Kagiya Teahouse","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-osen-kagiya","Suzuki Harunobu · c. 1769 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 65.7 × 12.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A celebrated waitress at an Edo shrine teahouse turns to watch a cuckoo. · The teahouse lantern reads “rest stop,” and a table of cakes stands beside her. · No story is told: her own fame is the subject. · The pillar print (hashira-e), a tall, narrow sheet for hanging, stresses her height."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/the-courtesan-nishikigi-of-the-yotsumeya-brothel.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","The Courtesan Nishikigi of the Yotsumeya Brothel","Isoda Koryūsai","koryusai-courtesan-nishikigi","Isoda Koryūsai · 1776 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 37.8 × 25.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A courtesan shows off a black robe with paulownia flowers, two young attendants beside her. · Look at the attendants' robes: trellised flowers hinted at women behind the quarter's barred windows. · The designer gave up samurai rank and began in Harunobu's manner. · From a series showing the year's first new robe patterns."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/three-types-of-drunks.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Three Types of Drunks","Isoda Koryūsai","koryusai-three-drunks","Isoda Koryūsai · c. 1770 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 69.8 × 11.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Three tipsy figures, stacked up a tall sheet, play a stock trio of Edo art and literature. · The sheet was trimmed at left, cutting into the signature. · Utamaro later took up the same theme in a triptych. · Young fashionable women take the men's usual place, a favorite twist of Koryūsai's."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/beauties-kiyonaga-utamaro-eishi.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi · Overview","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","","utamaro-reflective-love","From full figures to close-ups, 1780s–90s · The last quarter of the 1700s is often called the golden age of the print. Rival designers each offered an ideal of feminine beauty, and new formats such as the bust portrait followed. · A shifting ideal of the beautiful woman · Poetry circles, publishers and picture books · Outings, pastimes and private moments · Kitagawa Utamaro · Reflective Love · c. 1793/94"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/a-boating-party-under-azuma-bridge.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","A Boating Party under Azuma Bridge","Torii Kiyonaga","kiyonaga-boating-azuma-bridge","Torii Kiyonaga · c. 1785 · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 38.7 × 26 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Pleasure boats tie up in the cool beneath a Sumida River bridge on a summer day. · On the far shore at left, look for a shrine gate's crossbars above the river steps. · He trained under Kiyomitsu and designed actor prints too. · Recognizable landmarks set the beauties in places buyers knew."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/women-watching-a-girl-dance-on-shells.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Women Watching a Girl Dance on Shells","Torii Kiyonaga","kiyonaga-girl-dance-shells","Torii Kiyonaga · 1784 · Color woodblock print, 36.3 × 24.8 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A small girl dances on a pair of shells, the kind also used as game pieces, as a woman plays the shamisen. · His women are taller and more adult than the slight figures of the 1760s. · Dancing marked festival days and other celebrations. · Tall onlookers frame the small dancer, stressing her size. · Shamisen · A three-stringed plucked lute, the usual accompaniment for song and dance."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/three-beauties-of-the-kansei-period.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Three Beauties of the Kansei Period","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-three-beauties-kansei","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1791 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 38.7 × 25.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Three women are pressed close together, their heads and shoulders filling the sheet. · A handwritten note in one corner reads “Three Beautiful Women.” · Sarah Thompson notes that he later faced official limits on close-ups like this. · The bust portrait, or big head (ōkubi-e), brought the viewer face to face with the beauty. · Kansei · An era name covering 1789–1801."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/takashima-ohisa-using-two-mirrors-to-observe-her-coiffure.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Takashima Ohisa Using Two Mirrors to Observe Her Coiffure","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-takashima-ohisa","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1795 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 34.9 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Seen from behind, a young woman checks her hairstyle; her face shows only in the mirror before her. · A second mirror, held behind her head, shows her the back. · Mirrors let the viewer look over her shoulder, a device Utamaro used in other prints too. · Real, named townswomen, not only courtesans, became print celebrities."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/reflective-love.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Reflective Love","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-reflective-love","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1793/94 · Color woodblock print, 37.7 × 24.8 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman rests her chin on her hand and gazes past us, lost in thought. · From the series Anthology of Poems: The Love Section. · The plain pale ground isolates her face and hand. · Its publisher, Tsutaya Jūzaburō, also issued Utamaro's insect book."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/picture-book-of-crawling-creatures.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Picture Book of Crawling Creatures","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-crawling-creatures","Kitagawa Utamaro · 1788 · Page from a woodblock-printed book; ink and color on paper, 26.7 × 18.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A tree cricket clings to a seed head by comic verses; a firefly climbs the grasses opposite. · Insects and other small creatures fill all fifteen designs. · The verses were chosen, and the preface written, by the poet-scholar Yadoya no Meshimori. · Picture books like this paired prints with verse by comic-poetry (kyōka) circles."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/yamauba-and-kintaro.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Yamauba and Kintarō","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-yamauba-kintaro","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1795 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 38.4 × 22.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A legendary mountain woman and the wild boy she raised lean close in a quiet moment. · He returned to mother and boy many times. · Over his 1804 triptych of the Taikō, he was jailed and then kept in hand chains for fifty days. · In legend, Kintarō grew up to become the warrior Sakata no Kintoki. · Taikō · Title of the warlord Toyotomi Hideyoshi (1537–1598)."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/pleasure-boats-on-the-sumida-river-leftmost-sheet.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Pleasure Boats on the Sumida River (leftmost sheet)","Chōbunsai Eishi","eishi-pleasure-boats-sumida","Chōbunsai Eishi · c. 1792 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 39 × 25.6 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Elegantly dressed women gather on a roofed pleasure boat, the far bank and bridge behind them. · He worked in painting as well as prints, known for graceful line and drapery. · Cleveland holds two of its five sheets. · The five sheets join into one long river view."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ono-no-komachi-visiting-kiyomizu-temple.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Ono no Komachi Visiting Kiyomizu Temple","Chōbunsai Eishi","eishi-komachi-kiyomizu","Chōbunsai Eishi · c. 1788 · Color woodblock print, 38.7 × 25.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · At a Kyoto temple, two women admire cherry blossoms as a boy fastens a poem slip to a branch. · From The Fashionable Seven Komachi, a mitate series. · It alludes to a legend: at this temple a young woman overheard a monk praying for the aged poet and led him to her house. · Published by Izumiya Ichibei; the pale, limited colors keep the mood quiet."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/stars-of-the-stage-actor-prints.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints · Overview","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","","sharaku-otani-oniji","How fans followed their stars on paper, c. 1715–1824 · Women were banned from the kabuki stage in 1629 and boys in 1652, leaving it to adult men. Fans bought pictures of favorite stars and the roles they made famous. · Fan culture and the business of star images · How designers showed who was on stage · Private and regional prints beside the Edo mainstream · Tōshūsai Sharaku · Ōtani Oniji III as Yakko Edobei · 1794"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ichikawa-danjuro-ii-in-wait-a-moment-shibaraku.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ichikawa Danjūrō II in “Wait a Moment” (Shibaraku)","Torii Kiyotada","kiyotada-danjuro-shibaraku","Torii Kiyotada · c. 1715 · Woodblock print; ink and hand-applied color (tan-e) on paper, 28.6 × 15.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Striding out to the stage along the runway, the hero flaunts a boldly patterned costume. · He studied with Kiyonobu, in the Torii line of actor-print designers. · The museum's date predates his recorded activity, c. 1720–50. · Fans knew the actor by his family crest, three nested squares, more than by his face."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ichikawa-danjuro-v-as-a-traveler-beside-a-clump-of-iris.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ichikawa Danjūrō V as a Traveler beside a Clump of Iris","Katsukawa Shunshō","shunsho-danjuro-iris","Katsukawa Shunshō · c. 1780 · Color woodblock print, 29.9 × 14.4 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A star in a striped travel robe pauses by a bank of iris, sword at his hip. · Shunshō led the actor-print field in his day and painted as well. · The actor later took the name Ichikawa Ebizō IV. · His Katsukawa studio began drawing actors as recognizable individuals."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/danjuro-v-as-a-skeleton-and-iwai-hanshiro-iv-as-the-cherry-princess.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Danjūrō V as a Skeleton and Iwai Hanshirō IV as the Cherry Princess","Katsukawa Shunshō","shunsho-danjuro-skeleton","Katsukawa Shunshō · c. 1783 · Color woodblock print (diptych), each sheet about 32.9 × 15 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · As a skeleton, a disgraced monk's ghost closes in on the princess he loved, who shrinks away. · In the play he gave up his vows for her, was cast out and died poor. · His stage costume was black, with bones painted on. · The crest on his arm dates the scene to the Nakamura Theater, 1783."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/otani-oniji-iii-as-yakko-edobei.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ōtani Oniji III as Yakko Edobei","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-otani-oniji","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Woodblock print; ink, color and white mica on paper, 38.1 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A villainous manservant scowls, hair bristling, his hands thrust out like claws. · Behind the pen name is an artist whose identity is still debated; he issued over 140 prints in about ten months. · Mica in the dark ground gives it a soft shimmer. · Caricature-like features fuse the stage villain with the man playing him."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ichikawa-ebizo-iv-as-takemura-sadanoshin.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ichikawa Ebizō IV as Takemura Sadanoshin","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-ichikawa-ebizo","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Color woodblock print, 36 × 23.6 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A gaunt older man clasps his hands; in the play, he is allowed one last performance with his daughter. · From the same 1794 production as the scowling manservant. · The publisher Tsutaya Jūzaburō's emblem and the censor's seal kiwame, “approved,” share the sheet. · Born in 1741, the actor was in his fifties when he played the role."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/segawa-tomisaburo-ii-as-yadorigi.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Segawa Tomisaburō II as Yadorigi","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-segawa-tomisaburo","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Woodblock print; ink, color and white mica on paper, 37.1 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Playing a stylish woman, the male actor tugs at his robe with a big theatrical gesture. · Look at the square jaw beneath the woman's hairstyle. · A handwritten note gives the actor's name and the fifth month of 1794. · Audiences were fascinated by onnagata, men who specialized in women's roles."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/sawamura-sojuro-iii-as-shimada-juzaburo.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Sawamura Sōjūrō III as Shimada Jūzaburō","Utagawa Toyokuni I","toyokuni-sawamura-sojuro","Utagawa Toyokuni I · c. 1795 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 35.9 × 24.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A star poses in the play Faithful Courtesans in Yoshiwara, his trousers patterned with stylized water. · From a series of actors on stage published in 1794–96. · The actor's yagō, Kinokuniya, is printed under the series title. · Toyokuni's full-length, composed figures stand apart from Sharaku's tight close-ups. · Yagō · An acting family's house name, shared by its line of actors."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/danjuro-viis-benkei-as-fudo-myoo.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Danjūrō VII's Benkei as Fudō Myōō","Utagawa Kunisada","kunisada-danjuro-benkei-fudo","Utagawa Kunisada · c. 1824 · Woodblock print (surimono); ink and color on paper, 21.4 × 18.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The star's warrior-monk role merges with Fudō, a fierce Buddhist guardian, sword and rosary in hand. · Ichikawa actors looked to this guardian of Narita's Shinshōji temple for protection. · It might have been a New Year gift marking the future Danjūrō VIII's birth. · Surimono were privately commissioned luxury prints in small runs, often exchanged as gifts."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/nakamura-utaemon-iii-as-ishikawa-goemon.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Nakamura Utaemon III as Ishikawa Goemon","Shunkōsai Hokushū","hokushu-utaemon-goemon","Shunkōsai Hokushū · 1822 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 40 × 26.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A bandit-hero glares sideways, plotting to steal the Imperial Seal in The Golden Gate and the Paulownia Crest. · Look at the courtly cap and stiff robes, stolen from an imperial messenger as a disguise. · The actor lived from 1778 to 1838. · The designer was based in Osaka, which had its own actor prints beside Edo's."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hokusai.html","Hokusai · Overview","Hokusai","","hokusai-red-fuji","Landscapes, ghosts and poems from a long career, 1810s–1830s · The designer (1760–1849) worked in prints, books and paintings over a very long career. At seventy, around 1830, he turned to Mount Fuji, and his views helped make landscape a favorite with buyers. · Landscape as a subject in its own right · A newly affordable imported blue · Ghosts, poems and sketches beside the views · Katsushika Hokusai · South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji) · c. 1830–32"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Hokusai","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-great-wave","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 37.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A towering wave curls over three boats, its foam breaking into claw-like fingers. · Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji sold so well that it grew to 46 prints. · Debussy's orchestral work La Mer is said to owe something to it. · Look in the wave's trough: Fuji appears as a small peak, dwarfed by the water."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/south-wind-clear-sky-red-fuji.html","Hokusai","South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-red-fuji","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 24.4 × 35.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The mountain alone, glowing red under fleecy clouds, fills the sheet. · Fuji can look reddish at late-summer dawns, perhaps what he meant to show. · Janice Katz (Art Institute) notes later printings are much redder than the earliest, softer ones. · Bokashi: color wiped by hand on the block shades the cone from green to red-brown."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/storm-below-mount-fuji.html","Hokusai","Storm below Mount Fuji","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-storm-below-fuji","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 38.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Red lightning flickers over the darkened foot of the mountain as the peak climbs into a summer sky. · Another version from the same block shows rain falling below the peak. · The Met counts it among his most celebrated prints. · The series shows one mountain under changing weather and from shifting viewpoints."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/shichirigahama-in-sagami-province.html","Hokusai","Shichirigahama in Sagami Province","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-shichirigahama","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 38.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Fuji rises beyond a wooded headland and a long beach. · Prussian blue is a synthetic pigment; the Met says Dutch traders brought it to Japan. · Blue ties sea, sky and land into one view. · Blue printing (aizuri), in which blue dominates the color scheme, was then in fashion."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/kirifuri-waterfall-at-kurokami-mountain-in-shimotsuke.html","Hokusai","Kirifuri Waterfall at Kurokami Mountain in Shimotsuke","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-kirifuri-waterfall","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1832 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 37.5 × 26 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A waterfall breaks into many streams down a rock face as tiny travelers look up from below. · From A Tour of Waterfalls in Various Provinces. · Look at upper right: two more travelers pick their way across the wooded slope. · Sets of famous sights (meisho) let buyers tour the provinces on paper."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hokusai-manga-sketches-of-hokusai.html","Hokusai","Hokusai Manga (Sketches of Hokusai)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-manga","Katsushika Hokusai · 1812–78 · Woodblock-printed books (11 of 15 volumes), 22.5 × 15.5 cm closed. The Art Institute of Chicago · Flowers, grasses and leaves are scattered across an open spread. · Planned as one volume, it grew to fifteen; the last two appeared after his death. · Western collectors later bought it as a source of Japanese motifs. · Manga here means casual sketches, not the modern comic."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/oiwa-from-one-hundred-ghost-tales.html","Hokusai","Oiwa, from One Hundred Ghost Tales","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-oiwa","Katsushika Hokusai · 1831–32 · Color woodblock print, 25.6 × 16.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Scarred and pale, a betrayed wife stares out of a torn lantern, back for revenge. · Poison she took for beauty cream scarred her face, leaving one eye drooping. · The series is named for a candlelit game of telling a hundred ghost stories. · Her tale comes from a famous ghost play, The Ghost Story of Yotsuya."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/the-emperor-tenji-from-one-hundred-poems-explained-by-the-nurse.html","Hokusai","The Emperor Tenji, from One Hundred Poems Explained by the Nurse","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-emperor-tenji","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1835/36 · Color woodblock print, 26.2 × 37.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Farmers haul rice sheaves past a flimsy hut where, a story says, an emperor sheltered from rain. · He reigned 668–671 and is credited with Japan's first civil law code. · His poem sympathizes with farmers who meet such rain day after day. · The picture turns a court poem into a scene of farm work. · The Nurse · The series imagines an old nursemaid explaining each classic poem in plain terms."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/basket-of-peonies.html","Hokusai","Basket of Peonies","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-basket-peonies","Katsushika Hokusai · 1810 or 1814 · Color woodblock print (surimono), 38.3 × 52.1 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Red and white peonies spill from a woven basket beside a poem. · The signature notes his change of name to Taito; the printer is named too. · Made for an occasion in the life of the actor Danjūrō VII. · The peony, a summer flower, was also the actor's emblem."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hiroshige.html","Hiroshige · Overview","Hiroshige","","hiroshige-kanbara-snow","Travel, weather and the city, 1830s–1858 · The designer (1797–1858) made his name in the early 1830s with views of the Tōkaidō, the highway between Edo and Kyoto. His last great series, on Edo itself, appeared in the late 1850s. · Mood made from weather, season and hour · Bird's-eye and close-up viewpoints · City places and pastimes in his last years · Utagawa Hiroshige · Evening Snow at Kanbara · c. 1833–34"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/morning-view-of-nihonbashi.html","Hiroshige","Morning View of Nihonbashi","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-nihonbashi","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833–34 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 24.1 × 35.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · At dawn a lord's procession leaves Edo over the bridge that marked the start of the great road. · Fishmongers with the morning's catch walk ahead of the procession. · The first sheet of Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō, published by Hōeidō. · A landmark shown as street life: rank and trade crowd the same crossing."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hara-mount-fuji-in-the-morning.html","Hiroshige","Hara: Mount Fuji in the Morning","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-hara","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833–34 · Woodblock print, 24.1 × 35.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Two women and a porter halt on the road, apparently struck by the view. · The name Hara means “field”; from here the view of Fuji was almost unbroken. · This edition is often called the First or Great Tōkaidō. · The summit pushes past the top border, too big for its frame."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/evening-snow-at-kanbara.html","Hiroshige","Evening Snow at Kanbara","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kanbara-snow","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833–34 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 22.5 × 34.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Snow blankets a village at nightfall as a few travelers struggle on. · Look for three small figures, bent under umbrellas and straw capes. · Records suggest he came through in summer, and deep snow is rare there: the snow is invented. · The snow is bare paper, set against a dark gray sky."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/driving-rain-at-shono.html","Hiroshige","Driving Rain at Shōno","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-shono-rain","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1833 · Color woodblock print, 25.4 × 38 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Travelers bent double hurry along a slope as a sudden squall whips the bamboo. · The publisher's name, Takenouchi, appears on one traveler's umbrella. · Shōno was a minor station on a mostly coastal route. · Three tones of gray print the bamboo, each fainter with distance."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/the-monkey-bridge-in-kai-province.html","Hiroshige","The Monkey Bridge in Kai Province","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-monkey-bridge","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1841–42 · Vertical diptych of woodblock prints, mounted as a hanging scroll; print 73 × 24.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A bridge spans a deep gorge, with a village, far hills and a rushing river below. · Look for travelers crossing the bridge, tiny against cliff and sky. · Look at the moon, framed between the bridge and the cliff. · Two sheets joined top to bottom let landscape run vertically, from treetops to the gorge floor. · Monkey Bridge, Kai Province · Saruhashi, a famous bridge in Kai, a mountainous old province west of Edo (today Yamanashi Prefecture)."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/wild-geese-flying-under-the-full-moon.html","Hiroshige","Wild Geese Flying under the Full Moon","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-geese-moon","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833 · Woodblock print, 38.1 × 12.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Geese slant down across a huge full moon in a tall, narrow sheet. · In East Asian poetry, the eighth month's full moon marked the height of autumn. · The poem at upper left wonders whether so lovely a night will ever return. · Fixed seasonal pairings let picture and poem work as one."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/fukagawa-susaki-and-jumantsubo.html","Hiroshige","Fukagawa Susaki and Jūmantsubo","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-fukagawa-susaki","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 33.3 × 22 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A golden eagle swoops over a snowy shore, its wings spanning the top of the sheet. · Below lies Edo Bay, now Tokyo Bay, seen from high above. · One of the most popular designs in One Hundred Famous Views of Edo. · The aerial view is one his viewers could only imagine."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/fireworks-at-ryogoku.html","Hiroshige","Fireworks at Ryōgoku","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-ryogoku-fireworks","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1858 · Color woodblock print, 36 × 24.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A rocket bursts in a moonless sky above lantern-lit boats and a crowded bridge. · On late-summer nights, those who could afford it took pleasure boats onto the cool river. · Published in the year he died, thought to be of cholera. · Its dying sparks light up the crowd and the far bank."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/plum-garden-at-kameido.html","Hiroshige","Plum Garden at Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kameido-plum","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 36 × 24.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A dark plum trunk sprawls across the foreground: Edo's most famous tree, said to resemble a sleeping dragon. · Van Gogh copied the design in 1887; see Post-Impressionism. · Comparing many impressions, British Museum scientist Capucine Korenberg found the blocks changed over time. · Its surprising color, above all the red sky, makes it easy to recognize."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/heroes-ghosts-and-the-west.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West · Overview","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","","kuniyoshi-taira-ghosts","Late Edo, Meiji, and prints abroad, 1827–1927 · In the 1800s battle scenes, ghosts and foreign newcomers grew into major subjects, even as censors banned actor prints for several years. Abroad, Western painters and printmakers borrowed the prints' ways of composing, and by the 1920s Yoshida Hiroshi had carried the woodblock into modern times. · Heroes and ghosts as censorship tightened · Foreign trade and the Western newcomers · Western borrowings and a modern Japanese revival · Utagawa Kuniyoshi · Ghosts of the Taira at Daimotsu Bay · 1840s"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hu-sanniang-from-one-hundred-and-eight-heroes-of-the-popular-water-margin.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Hu Sanniang, from One Hundred and Eight Heroes of the Popular Water Margin","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-hu-sanniang","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · c. 1827/30 · Color woodblock print, 39.5 × 27 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman warrior of the Chinese outlaw novel braces amid flying arrows, a sword in each hand. · Sarah Thompson (MFA Boston) calls this series his breakthrough, launching the warrior print. · Armor, robes and foliage crowd the sheet in dense, swirling pattern. · She adds that Chinese novels were a fairly safe subject under the censors."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/ghosts-of-the-taira-at-daimotsu-bay.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Ghosts of the Taira at Daimotsu Bay","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-taira-ghosts","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · 1840s · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 35.4 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York (this impression dated 1849–52) · From a churning sea, a host of ghosts surges toward the ship of the clan that destroyed them. · After the Minamoto victory at sea in 1185, warriors ruled Japan. · Drawn as flat silhouettes, the ghosts suggest how fleeting life is. · Soft-toned ghosts against a crisp, colorful ship and wave set two worlds side by side. · Minamoto and Taira · Rival warrior clans who fought for control of Japan in the 1180s."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/popular-otsu-e-phenomenon.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Popular Ōtsu-e Phenomenon","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-otsu-e","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · 1848 · Woodblock print triptych; ink and color on paper, 36.4 × 73.1 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art · Folk-painting characters spring to life to protect their painter, a scene from a puppet play. · Ōtsu-e were sold to travelers at Ōtsu, a way station on the Tōkaidō. · The figures carry famous actors' faces, slipping past the ban on actor prints. · Look for the warrior-monk Benkei in kabuki face paint, as if mocking censors."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/artisans-from-an-up-to-date-parody-of-the-four-classes.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Artisans, from An Up-to-Date Parody of the Four Classes","Utagawa Kunisada","kunisada-artisans","Utagawa Kunisada · 19th century · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 36.8 × 25.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A busy workshop staffed by women prepares blocks and paper for printing. · Artisans were one of the four Edo classes, with warriors, farmers and merchants. · Women were not normally in the trade, so the scene is a playful fiction. · Read right to left: carving through the pasted design, clearing wood, sizing paper, a printer waiting."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/an-american-mercantile-building-in-yokohama.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","An American Mercantile Building in Yokohama","Utagawa Sadahide","sadahide-american-building","Utagawa Sadahide · 1861 · Color woodblock print triptych, 37 × 75.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Westerners and their Japanese companions crowd the veranda of a trading house above a busy harbor. · Look for the American flag on a tall flagpole at left. · The Met holds a related 1861 triptych by the same designer, of foreigners indoors. · After Yokohama opened to foreign trade, prints showed buyers the newcomers' clothes and buildings."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/moon-over-daimotsu-bay-benkei-from-one-hundred-aspects-of-the-moon.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Moon over Daimotsu Bay: Benkei, from One Hundred Aspects of the Moon","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi","yoshitoshi-benkei-moon","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi · January 1886 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 35.2 × 24.5 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art · In a storm, the monk prays from the prow to hold back the Taira ghosts, unseen here. · Benkei and his lord Yoshitsune are fleeing by sea from the lord's half-brother. · Every design in this Meiji-era series (1885–92) builds on a moon. · It followed by two months the kabuki premiere of Funa Benkei, adapted from Noh. · Meiji · The reign of Emperor Meiji, 1868–1912, when Japan rapidly modernized. · Noh · Japan's classical masked drama, older and more stately than kabuki."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/caprice-in-purple-and-gold-the-golden-screen.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Caprice in Purple and Gold: The Golden Screen","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-caprice-golden-screen","James McNeill Whistler · 1864 · Oil on wood panel, 50.1 × 68.5 cm. Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian National Museum of Asian Art, Washington, DC · A model in Japanese dress sits before a gilded folding screen, looking through color prints. · The Irish model Joanna Hiffernan is surrounded by Asian objects from Whistler's own collection. · Shown at the Royal Academy in London in 1865. · Look at the print she studies: it comes from Hiroshige's Sixty-odd Provinces series."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/the-letter.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","The Letter","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-letter","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · Color aquatint and drypoint, 34.5 × 21.1 cm (plate). The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman seals a letter at her writing desk, her patterned dress pressed against patterned wallpaper. · Printed by the artist with Leroy in an edition of 25. · Domestic life, bound by social custom, was one of her main themes. · Flat pattern and bright color squeeze the space, a lesson she took from Japanese woodblocks."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/boats-at-rest.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Boats at Rest","Arthur Wesley Dow","dow-boats-at-rest","Arthur Wesley Dow · c. 1895 · Oil on canvas, 66 × 91.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Rowboats rest on a marshy bank near the painter's home town, Ipswich, Massachusetts. · His bold palette has much in common with Gauguin, Émile Bernard and other French Post-Impressionists. · His Japanese-inspired teaching on composition, at Ipswich and Pratt Institute, was widely influential. · From ukiyo-e came bold cropping, a raised viewpoint and flattened space."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/evening-on-the-chikugo-river.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Evening on the Chikugo River","Yoshida Hiroshi","yoshida-chikugo-evening","Yoshida Hiroshi · 1927 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 27.6 × 41.1 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Lamplit houses line the river at dusk as a long bridge fades into the blue distance. · The Japanese title names the town, Hita, which the English leaves out. · The artist gave this impression to the museum himself, within a few years. · A seal reads jizuri, “self-printed”; Edo designers had left printing to specialists."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","Start","Oil vs Acrylic","","","Oil vs Acrylic"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/a-short-history.html","A Short History · Overview","A Short History","","oa-h-plastic","Five centuries of oil; less than one of acrylic · Oil has been the painter's main paint since the 1400s. Acrylic came out of the plastics industry in the mid-1900s. · Oil's long run and the paint tube · Plastic paint · Using both · Diagram · Acrylic paint arrives"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-five-centuries-of-oil.html","A Short History · History","Five Centuries of Oil","","rand-1841-tube-patent","For more than five hundred years, oil has been the paint most painters reached for. · Its early story is told in Pigments and Materials. What matters here is one change in the 1800s: the paint tube, patented by John Rand in 1841. · Before tubes, paint was kept in animal bladders that couldn't be resealed once pricked. · A screw-cap tin tube kept paint fresh and easy to carry outdoors. · Painters already worked outdoors before tubes; many outdoor sketches were finished in the studio."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-plastic-paint.html","A Short History · History","Plastic Paint","","oa-h-plastic","Acrylic paint comes from the same plastics family as Plexiglas, first made by industry, not for artists. · Paint makers adapted it twice: a solvent paint, then a water-based one. · Early ads pushed quick drying and flexibility; mixing with water came near the end. · By 1965 most companies making artists' paint had a water-based acrylic of their own. · For David Hockney it ended the wait: in oil he worked on two or three pictures while each dried; in acrylic, one at a time."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-using-both.html","A Short History · History","Using Both","","oa-h-both","Oil and acrylic now share the studio, and sometimes the same canvas. · Tate reported in 2009 that acrylic paints and primers had been about half of artists' paint sales for 30 years. Painters who use both give each the job it does best. · Roy Lichtenstein's labels list oil and Magna; Art Institute of Chicago scientists found which is where. · Magna gave him fast-drying, flat color. · Through a metal stencil Magna dried too fast and stuck, so he used oil for the dots."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/whats-in-the-tube.html","What's in the Tube · Overview","What's in the Tube","","oa-binders","Same pigment, two very different binders · Both paints can carry the same pigment; the difference is what holds it. Acrylic itself comes in two kinds. · Two binders · Solvent acrylic and water acrylic · Diagram · Oil and acrylic binders"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-two-binders.html","What's in the Tube · Concept","Two Binders","","oa-binders","Oil paint is pigment in a drying oil, a plant oil that turns solid in air. Acrylic is pigment among tiny plastic droplets in water. · The droplets are less than a thousandth of a millimeter across. Binders in general: see Pigments and Materials. · Linseed is the main paint oil; makers use safflower or poppy in whites because they yellow less. · Oil paint holds few extras, such as driers; acrylic holds many, to keep the droplets apart, thicken the paint and stop mold."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/the-harvesters.html","What's in the Tube","The Harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1565 · Oil on oak, 119 × 162 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The same oil binder carries paint thin enough to see through and thick enough to cover, side by side. Thin see-through layers like these are glazes, covered in Color Theory. · Where the paint is thinnest, the drawing underneath is not fully covered. · Thicker, opaque strokes on top describe the details. · The Met's essay on oil in the Low Countries calls this a quicker, more economical way of painting than earlier work."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-solvent-acrylic-and-water-acrylic.html","What's in the Tube · Concept","Solvent Acrylic and Water Acrylic","","oa-solvent-water","Magna, the first artists' acrylic, had its plastic dissolved in solvent. Most acrylic sold today is water-based instead. · Magna thinned like oil but often dried in hours rather than days. Solvent or the next layer could dissolve it again. · It could even be mixed with oil paint. · It never sold well and was dropped in the mid-1970s."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/how-they-dry.html","How They Dry · Overview","How They Dry","","oa-acrylic-dries","Air hardens one paint; evaporation sets the other · The two paints become solid in different ways, and on very different clocks. Those clocks shape how each one is handled. · Oil cures · Acrylic dries · Open time · Color shift as it dries · Diagram · How an acrylic film forms"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-oil-cures.html","How They Dry · Concept","Oil Cures","","oa-oil-cures","Oil cures: it reacts with oxygen in the air, and its molecules link into one solid network. · Follow the arrows: oxygen reaches the top first, so the paint underneath lags behind, most of all in thick paint. · Driers added to the paint speed the reaction. · Cured oil can't be thinned back into wet paint. · In a 2022 lab study, oil paint films were still stiffening after 30 years."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-acrylic-dries.html","How They Dry · Concept","Acrylic Dries","","oa-acrylic-dries","Acrylic dries by losing water, not by reacting with air. · The surface dries fast, but the film keeps losing water for about a month and can take up to a year to fully fuse. · For about a week, sometimes longer, freshly dried paint can still dissolve in water. · Even fully dry, it can swell in water and lose some additives. · Dried too cold, the droplets fuse poorly, leaving a porous or powdery film."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/early-still.html","How They Dry","Early, Still","David Nicholson","dn-early-still","David Nicholson · 2025 · Acrylic on canvas, 55.9 × 71.1 cm · Because acrylic sets quickly into a film, each flat block of color could be laid over the last without a long wait. · Look where colors meet: the yellow tree against the red, the lit windows against the dark wall. · None of the color beneath has smeared into the new layer. · Brush marks show inside the red and yellow, yet each block still reads as one color."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-open-time.html","How They Dry · Concept","Open Time","","oa-open-time","Open time is how long paint stays wet enough to move around on the canvas. · Acrylic is dry to the touch in minutes to a few hours; oil takes about two days. That window decides how a painter works. · Oil invites wet-in-wet: new color worked into paint that is still wet, for soft blends. · Once acrylic has set, the next color sits on top with a clean edge. · Acrylic can be blended wet too, but only quickly."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer.html","How They Dry","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","monet-stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer","Claude Monet · 1890–91 · Oil on canvas, 60 × 100.5 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Monet used both timings in one canvas: some passages worked wet, others painted over dry paint. · Look at the edges of the large stack: paint built up wet-in-wet. · In the sky, zigzag strokes were blended wet, then more color went on once they had dried. · Some thick strokes look slightly flattened, possibly while still soft; the signature went on over dry paint."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-color-shift-as-it-dries.html","How They Dry · Concept","Color Shift as It Dries","","oa-color-shift","Wet acrylic looks milky; as it dries, the binder turns clear and the color shows through. · Getty-published research explains the change. One paint maker says colors dry slightly darker. How binders change color in general is covered in Color Theory. · A second, different effect: dry paint can look less rich than the wet paint did. · A varnish brings that richness back, conservator Chris Stavroudis notes."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/handling-and-layering.html","Handling and Layering · Overview","Handling and Layering","","oa-blending","Blending, building up and painting over · Drying time shapes every stroke. This part follows each paint from blending and thick paint to layering rules, and what to thin and clean it with. · Blending · Brushwork and impasto · Painting over dry paint · Fat over lean · Mixing the two in layers · Mediums, solvents and cleanup · Diagram · Wet-in-wet and onto dry"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-blending.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Blending","","oa-blending","Soft blends in acrylic depend on keeping the paint wet long enough to work. · Oil needs no such help. · Mist the palette with water to keep acrylic wet. · Add a little retarder, an additive made for acrylic that slows drying. · Too much retarder leaves a weak film that may stay sticky, and slows full drying."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/ginevra-de-benci.html","Handling and Layering","Ginevra de' Benci","Leonardo da Vinci","leonardo-ginevra-de-benci","Leonardo da Vinci · c. 1474/1478 · Oil on panel, 38.1 × 37 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Some of the blending here was done with fingers, not a brush. · Where the sky meets the juniper bush, the paint was softened by hand; the National Gallery of Art notes his fingerprints still show. · Oil dried more slowly than egg tempera and could go on in thin layers, the NGA adds. · That let him paint the soft shading of the face and the distant haze."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-brushwork-and-impasto.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Brushwork and Impasto","","oa-impasto","Impasto is paint laid on thick enough to stand up from the surface and keep the mark of the brush. · Some oil colors come stiff and buttery from the tube; others flow. · Acrylic gels and pastes are thickened acrylic mediums; fillers such as calcite or silica change their texture. · A Jules Olitski impasto at MoMA, probably built from gel, was found still slightly soft and uncracked; the gel had discolored somewhat."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/self-portrait.html","Handling and Layering","Self-Portrait","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-self-portrait-1659","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1659 · Oil on canvas, 84.5 × 66 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The thickest paint sits where light hits the face. · The lights went in first as stubby dabs of heavy paint. · After they dried, he went back over them with small touches of darker color that he left unblended. · In the hair, lines were scratched into the wet paint with the end of the brush. · The background and hands are thin by contrast."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-painting-over-dry-paint.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Painting Over Dry Paint","","oa-painting-over","Once a layer is dry, a painter can cover it, change it or start over on top. · Changes painted over are called pentimenti. In oil paintings, X-rays and other scans often find them. · Oil: if a fingernail doesn't dent the layer, it's ready to paint over; go easy on solvent. · Both paints: don't start a new painting over a finished one. Dirt, varnish or a slick surface can make the new paint lift or flake."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/venus-with-a-mirror.html","Handling and Layering","Venus with a Mirror","Titian","titian-venus-with-a-mirror","Titian · c. 1555 · Oil on canvas, 124.5 × 105.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Under this goddess lies another painting: a portrait of a man and a woman, turned on its side. · X-rays show the double portrait; in the scholar Shapley's view, it was abandoned only shortly before the canvas was reused. · The man's jacket was left showing and became the red velvet drape over her hip. · Her elbow, her fingers and a cupid's legs were changed too."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/the-milkmaid.html","Handling and Layering","The Milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer · c. 1660 · Oil on canvas, 45.5 × 41 cm. Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam · Scans by the Rijksmuseum and the Mauritshuis in 2022 found objects painted out of this kitchen. · At lower right, a fire basket was later painted over; the foot stove, the tiles and the floor now cover it. · A rack of jugs sketched behind her head was never developed."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/umbrella-reverie.html","Handling and Layering","Umbrella, Reverie","David Nicholson","dn-umbrella-reverie","David Nicholson · 2024 · Acrylic on canvas, 101.6 × 76.2 cm · Painting over dry paint can build an image, not only hide one. · The orange and yellow shapes and the black outlines all went on over dry layers. · Look at the yellow jacket: its black fold lines are drawn on top of the dried color."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-fat-over-lean.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Fat Over Lean","","oa-fat-lean","Fat over lean is the rule for layering oil: the oiliest paint goes on top. · University of Delaware conservators phrase it as flexible over less flexible. · The National Gallery, London, traces wrinkling and cracks to a top layer setting while paint below is still soft. · Acrylic has a similar rule: avoid watery washes over thick, glossy acrylic."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-mixing-the-two-in-layers.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Mixing the Two in Layers","","oa-mixing-layers","Oil can go over acrylic, but acrylic should not go over oil. · Research finds oil on a good-quality, well-applied acrylic ground holds up fairly well. Keep acrylic mediums out of oil paint. · Why oil sometimes peels off acrylic is debated: additives in cheap grounds, or compounds forming in the oil. · In Christina Young's lab tests, fresh oil gripped acrylic relatively poorly; aged oil gripped better but could crack the primer beneath. · Thin acrylic underpainting can go under oil, but only on an acrylic ground."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-mediums-solvents-and-cleanup.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Mediums, Solvents and Cleanup","","oa-mediums","A medium is added to paint to change how it handles. A solvent thins oil paint and cleans the brushes. · Water does both jobs for acrylic. Oil's solvents call for care. · Turpentine fumes irritate eyes, nose and throat; one university art department treats mineral spirits the same. · Ventilate, cover jars and wear nitrile gloves; latex doesn't block solvents. · Low-odor mineral spirits are safer than regular spirits or turpentine, not harmless. · Oily rags can self-heat and catch fire; keep them in water in a closed can or a metal fireproof bin."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/surfaces.html","Surfaces · Overview","Surfaces","","oa-grounds","What goes under the paint · Under most canvases today is an acrylic ground. A few painters work on bare canvas. · Acrylic gesso · Staining raw canvas · Diagram · Primed and stained canvas"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-grounds-for-each.html","Surfaces · Concept","Grounds for Each","","oa-grounds","The most common canvas ground today is acrylic, sold as gesso, and both paints go over it. · The name is borrowed from traditional gesso, a different ground covered in Pigments and Materials. Some painters skip the ground altogether and stain the canvas. · Morris Louis stained with Magna, the solvent acrylic. · Helen Frankenthaler's first stains, in 1952, were oil; she moved to acrylic in 1962."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/aging-and-care.html","Aging and Care · Overview","Aging and Care","","oa-oil-ages","How each paint changes, and how to look after it · Oil hardens, yellows and cracks with age. Acrylic stays flexible but soft, and needs different care. · How oil ages · How acrylic ages · Varnishing and cleaning · Choosing between them · Diagram · Young and old oil"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-how-oil-ages.html","Aging and Care · Concept","How Oil Ages","","oa-oil-ages","Old oil paint is stiffer than new, and it can yellow and turn see-through. · Brittle paint cracks as the canvas swells and shrinks with humidity; paint on a rigid panel cracks less. The example that follows is an extreme case. · Transparency comes mainly from metal soaps, which form when lead or zinc in pigments reacts with the oil. · Yellowing that builds up in the dark largely clears after a short time in daylight, one study found."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/moonlight-marine.html","Aging and Care","Moonlight Marine","Albert Pinkham Ryder","ryder-moonlight-marine","Albert Pinkham Ryder · 1870–90 · Oil and possibly wax on wood, 29.2 × 30.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Thick paint laid on layer after layer has moved here: since the painting was finished, the Met reports, the edges of the clouds, sails and hull have changed shape. · Look at the wide cracks running across the whole surface. · His biographers say he wanted glass over his pictures so their still-tacky surfaces would not be touched."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-how-acrylic-ages.html","Aging and Care · Concept","How Acrylic Ages","","oa-acrylic-ages","Acrylic ages gently but stays soft, and how soft depends on the temperature. · It yellows and cracks far less than oil, Tate finds, with no sign that it is less stable. Its weak points are different. · It scratches easily, and warm acrylic can take surface dirt into the paint. · A soap-like additive, a surfactant, can drift to the surface as a cloudy, greyish film. It can come back after wiping."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-varnishing-and-cleaning.html","Aging and Care · Concept","Varnishing and Cleaning","","oa-varnish","Taking off an old varnish is usually simpler on oil than on acrylic. · Solvents that lift varnish from oil, such as xylene, can soften dry acrylic; Tate warns oil-painting cleaners can damage it for good. · Give an oil painting six months or more to dry before varnishing it. · For acrylic, paint makers recommend an isolation coat: a permanent clear layer of acrylic medium beneath the varnish. · Whether to varnish acrylic at all is still debated."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-choosing-between-them.html","Aging and Care · Concept","Choosing Between Them","","oa-choosing","Neither paint is better; each suits a different way of working. · Choose by how you like to paint and where the work will live. · To blend and rework over days, pick oil. · To build crisp layers fast and clean up with water, pick acrylic. · To use both, keep thin acrylic underneath and oil on top, on an acrylic ground."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","Start","Pigments and Materials","","","Pigments and Materials"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/a-short-history-of-paint.html","A Short History of Paint · Overview","A Short History of Paint","","egyptian-blue-lump","From colored earth and furnaces to the chemist's lab · For thousands of years painters worked from a short list of colors, most dug from the ground and a few made with fire. Some were priced like treasure. Then chemistry widened the palette. · The first palettes, and the first synthetic pigment · Ultramarine and the apothecary's shop · The chemists' colors and the paint tube · Egyptian, New Kingdom, Dynasty 18 · Lump of Blue Paste (Egyptian blue) · about 1473–1458 BCE"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-earth-and-fire-the-first-palettes.html","A Short History of Paint · History","Earth and Fire: The First Palettes","","pm-h-first-palettes","The first palettes held what could be dug up or charred: red and yellow iron earths, black charcoal and white chalk. · Fire also made Egyptian blue, at right, the oldest known synthetic pigment: a new compound cooked in a furnace, not dug up. It is very stable, though a binder that yellows around it changes how it looks. · At Blombos Cave in South Africa, a kit for mixing red ochre paint is about 100,000 years old. · Egyptian blue fell out of use in the Middle Ages; Raphael's Galatea fresco (1511–12) is a rare late use."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/lump-of-blue-paste-egyptian-blue.html","A Short History of Paint","Lump of Blue Paste (Egyptian blue)","Egyptian, New Kingdom, Dynasty 18","egyptian-blue-lump","Egyptian, New Kingdom, Dynasty 18 · about 1473–1458 BCE · Paste, L. 1 × H. 0.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · This is the pigment before it became paint: a crumb about 1 cm long, found in a burial inside the tomb of Senenmut at Thebes. · Copper gives it its blue."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-gold-lapis-and-the-guild-shop.html","A Short History of Paint · History","Gold, Lapis and the Guild Shop","","pm-h-costly-blue","Some pigments were luxury goods. The finest, ultramarine, came from lapis lazuli mined in Badakhshan, in present-day Afghanistan. · The chart compares top grades in mid-1400s Florence: ultramarine could cost as much as gold leaf, so it was usually saved for the most important figure, often the Virgin's mantle. · Grinding alone gave a grayish powder; the blue had to be kneaded out of a dough of wax and resin. · Painters bought colors from apothecaries, and in Florence joined their guild. · Cennino Cennini's handbook (about 1400) calls ultramarine \"perfect beyond all other colours\" (Herringham translation, 1899)."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-the-chemists-palette.html","A Short History of Paint · History","The Chemist's Palette","","rand-1841-tube-patent","After centuries with few new colors, chemistry began producing them, most of them after 1800. · Prussian blue came first, found by accident in Berlin early in the 1700s. The rest came in a rush after 1800; several, such as cadmium yellow, came from metals discovered around that time. · Synthetic ultramarine is chemically the same as the natural pigment but far cheaper, so the costliest blue became an everyday one. · The patent drawing shows the collapsible tin tube (1841) of John Goffe Rand, an American portrait painter; it replaced paint stored in pig bladders."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/pigments-by-color.html","Pigments by Color · Overview","Pigments by Color","","pm-blues","The main historical pigments in each color, and how each behaves · Each color family had a few workhorse pigments. These pages say what each was, when it was used, and which ones change color or are poisonous. · Whites · Blacks · Earth pigments · Reds · Blues · Greens · Yellows and oranges · Diagram · The main blues, by date"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-whites.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Whites","","pm-whites","For most of history the painter's white was lead white, made by letting vinegar fumes corrode strips of lead. · It is dense, covers well and helps oil dry. Zinc white covers less and in oil can contribute to cracking; titanium white covers best of all. · Lead white is poisonous; the US banned lead in household paint in 1978. · Tube \"titanium white\" often contains some zinc white too."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/self-portrait-at-the-age-of-63.html","Pigments by Color","Self Portrait at the Age of 63","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-self-portrait-63","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1669 · Oil on canvas, 86 × 70.5 cm. The National Gallery, London · The lit cheek is built up thick, in lead white warmed with ochres, over a brown underlayer. · Thick, raised paint like this is called impasto."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-blacks.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Blacks","","pm-blacks","Painters' blacks get their color from carbon: soot collected from a flame, or charred vine twigs or bone. · Lampblack is soft and bluish and can leave oil slow to dry, so umber was often mixed in to speed it up. · \"Ivory black\" today is almost always bone black, which is mostly bone mineral (calcium phosphate) with only some carbon. · Cima da Conegliano used vine black for a cool gray robe and bone black for warmer areas (1502–04). · Carbon blacks barely change in light."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/music-in-the-tuileries-gardens.html","Pigments by Color","Music in the Tuileries Gardens","Édouard Manet","manet-music-tuileries","Édouard Manet · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 76.2 × 118.1 cm. The National Gallery, London · The crowd's top hats and coats are mostly ivory black, mixed with cobalt blue and orange-brown ochre. · The gray clothes mix the same black with cobalt blue, viridian and ochre. · Black even goes into the glazes of the foliage (see Color Theory, Glazing and Direct Painting), so it works as a color, not just for shadow."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-earth-pigments.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Earth Pigments","","pm-earths","Heat turns yellow earth red: it drives the water out of goethite, the yellow iron mineral, and leaves hematite, the red one. · People used this trick about 40,000 years ago. For earth colors in general, see Color Theory, Neutrals and Earth Colors. · Hilary Becker (Art Institute of Chicago lecture, 2024) notes that the heat of Vesuvius turned some yellow walls at Herculaneum red."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/self-portrait.html","Pigments by Color","Self-Portrait","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-self-portrait-1659","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1659 · Oil on canvas, 84.5 × 66 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Almost everything here is earth: red, yellow and brown ochres, with lead white, carbon black and tiny traces of vermilion. · Scientists at the National Gallery of Art identified the ochres area by area: flesh, collar, hatband and background (2014). · Umber, the browner earth, shows at the edge of the collar."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-reds.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Reds","","pm-reds","Reds came in two kinds: mineral and made reds such as vermilion and red lead, and lakes, which turn root or insect dyes into pigment (see Color Theory, Where Pigments Come From). · Vermilion is mercury sulfide, and toxic; red lead is made by heating lead white. By Cennini's time vermilion too was made chemically and bought ready-made from the apothecary. He warned that on walls it turns black. · Cochineal insects, raised on prickly-pear cactus, earned Spain more than almost anything it took from the Americas except silver. · Synthetic alizarin, the madder dye made from coal tar in 1868, quickly ended the madder-growing trade."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/venus-and-adonis.html","Pigments by Color","Venus and Adonis","Titian","titian-venus-adonis","Titian · about 1555–60 · Oil on canvas, 160 × 196.5 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · Both kinds of red sit side by side: vermilion in Adonis's orange-red sleeve, a red lake in his pinker tunic and in the cloth over Venus's seat. · The lake contains textile fibers. It was probably made from clippings of red-dyed cloth. · Tests matched the dye to cochineal, with some madder-type colorant too."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-blues.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Blues","","pm-blues","From the Middle Ages to 1700, painters had only a few blues: azurite, ultramarine, smalt (powdered cobalt glass) and indigo, a plant dye. · Each had a weakness. Ultramarine survives light and lime, so it works in fresco, but acid destroys it. Azurite can turn green, and smalt can lose its color in oil (see Fading and Darkening). · Violet pigments came late (cobalt violet 1859, manganese violet 1868). Before that, painters mixed purples, such as lake with ultramarine."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/girl-with-a-pearl-earring.html","Pigments by Color","Girl with a Pearl Earring","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-girl-pearl-earring","Johannes Vermeer · about 1665 · Oil on canvas, 44.5 × 39 cm. Mauritshuis, The Hague · The turban is natural ultramarine of high quality, mixed with lead white where the light falls. · In the shadow, the blue is cut with about half chalk and laid over a black underlayer."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Pigments by Color","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-great-wave","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 37.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The museum's record names a palette of indigo and imported Prussian blue, which probably reached Japan in the early 1800s. · Marco Leona, a Met scientist, argues that its strong, saturated blue helped make landscape prints popular. · Tests on other Met impressions found the blue outlines mix Prussian blue with indigo. · Despite cyanide in its structure, Prussian blue is safe enough to be an approved drug for removing radioactive cesium."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-greens.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Greens","","pm-greens","Most old greens were copper: malachite, a ground mineral, and verdigris, made by letting vinegar corrode copper. · Cennini called verdigris and lead white \"mortal enemies\" and kept them apart. The 1800s added emerald green, bright but full of arsenic, and viridian, a cool chromium green that hardly changes. · Emerald green also colored wallpapers; by the 1850s doctors were reporting illness in the people who lived with them."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/the-battle-of-san-romano.html","Pigments by Color","The Battle of San Romano","Paolo Uccello","uccello-battle-san-romano","Paolo Uccello · probably about 1438–40 · Egg tempera with some oil on poplar, 182 × 320 cm. The National Gallery, London · The hedge behind the horsemen was green. The museum's record says it has darkened to almost black. · The rose leaves mix verdigris with lead-tin yellow under a copper-green glaze, and those glazes have darkened. · Later additions at the corners use a different copper green, artificial malachite."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-yellows-and-oranges.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Yellows and Oranges","","pm-yellows","Many of the brightest early yellows were poisons: orpiment is an arsenic mineral, and lead-tin yellow and Naples yellow are lead compounds. · The bars show yellows coming and going. Lead-tin yellow was a favorite for centuries, then seems to have dropped out of use and was forgotten until analysts rediscovered it in 1941. Chrome and cadmium yellows added vivid new yellows and oranges. · Cennini calls orpiment \"like gold in colour\" and warns painters to keep it away from the mouth. · Adding selenium to cadmium yellow shifts it to orange and red."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/sunflowers.html","Pigments by Color","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 92.1 × 73 cm. The National Gallery, London · The yellows and oranges are several grades of chrome yellow, from pale lemon to deep orange. Soon after, in September 1888, he ordered three grades of it by letter. · Chrome yellow is lead chromate; small changes in its chemistry gave the different shades. · It can darken in light; see Fading and Darkening."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/binders-and-media.html","Binders and Media · Overview","Binders and Media","","pm-binder","What holds the pigment, and how each binder changes the paint · These pages take the main binders one by one: what each is made of, and what it lets a painter do. · What a binder does · Egg tempera · Fresco · Oil · Watercolor and gouache · Pastel · Encaustic · Diagram · One pigment in four binders"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-what-a-binder-does.html","Binders and Media · Concept","What a Binder Does","","pm-binder","A binder turns loose colored powder into paint: it glues the grains to each other and to the surface. · For how a binder changes a pigment's look, see Color Theory, How Pigments Behave. · Glue paints (distemper) were once common, but they are fragile and few survive."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-egg-tempera.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Egg Tempera","","pm-tempera","Egg tempera binds pigment with egg yolk. In medieval and early Renaissance Italy it was the usual paint for panels. · Egg dries quickly, so wet colors can't be blended as in oil. Painters modeled form with many small strokes side by side, as on the sphere. · By the late 1400s Italians were often combining egg with oil, and after about 1500 tempera fell out of favor."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/madonna-and-child.html","Binders and Media","Madonna and Child","Giotto","giotto-madonna-child","Giotto · about 1310–15 · Tempera on poplar panel, 85.4 × 61.8 cm (painted surface). National Gallery of Art, Washington · In the flesh, a green underpaint sets the shadows, then fine hatching and broader strokes model the form. · Under the gold is red bole, a layer of clay that warms the gold's color. · The dotted patterns in the halos were drawn freehand with a point, not stamped with punches."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-fresco.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Fresco","","pm-fresco","In fresco the painter works on lime plaster while it is still wet; as it sets, the color becomes part of the wall. · Follow the arrows: carbon dioxide from the air turns the lime back into a kind of stone, with the color inside it. Paint added once the plaster has dried, called secco, rests on the surface and may flake off. · Each day's patch of fresh plaster, a giornata, was only as big as the painter could finish before it dried. · Not every color suits wet lime: Pliny says indigo does not."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/lion-from-the-chapter-house-of-san-pedro-de-arlanza.html","Binders and Media","Lion, from the Chapter House of San Pedro de Arlanza","Spanish (Castile)","arlanza-lion","Spanish (Castile) · after 1200 · Fresco, mounted on canvas, 332.7 × 335.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art (The Cloisters), New York · This lion was painted on a monastery wall, taken down in the early twentieth century and mounted on canvas. · Lifting paintings off walls goes back to at least the 1500s. · Other wall paintings from the same monastery are in Barcelona, at the Museu Nacional d'Art de Catalunya."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-oil.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Oil","","stradanus-color-olivi","Oil paint binds pigment in an oil that dries, usually linseed; it hardens slowly as it takes up oxygen from the air. · Slow drying gives the painter time: colors can be blended on the surface, laid on thick or put on as glazes. · Stradanus's print (about 1600) repeats Vasari's legend that Van Eyck invented oil; it was used centuries earlier. · Oil then moved south; Giovanni Bellini used it in Venice by the 1460s."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/the-magdalen-reading.html","Binders and Media","The Magdalen Reading","Rogier van der Weyden","vanderweyden-magdalen-reading","Rogier van der Weyden · about 1435 · Oil on wood, transferred to mahogany, 62.2 × 54.4 cm. The National Gallery, London · Tests on this panel and four related ones found the paint is bound mostly in linseed oil. · Look at the lips, blended wet into wet, and the fur trim, where the brush dragged through paint that was still wet. · In one red glaze of the group, pine resin had been added to make the oil clearer."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-watercolor-and-gouache.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Watercolor and Gouache","","pm-watercolor","Watercolor is pigment in gum arabic, thinned with water. Its glow comes from the white paper under the color. · Gouache carries more pigment for its gum, so it hides the paper. · Highlights are often bare paper, left unpainted. · Watercolor paper is usually coated with gelatin to control how fast it drinks in the wash."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/the-return-tynemouth-recto.html","Binders and Media","The Return, Tynemouth (recto)","Winslow Homer","homer-return-tynemouth","Winslow Homer · 1881 · Transparent and opaque watercolor with gum glaze over graphite on paper, 34.2 × 34.3 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · Look at the pale pink in the clouds and on the boat. Homer used colors that resist light here; in many of his other watercolors, similar washes made with less stable reds have faded. · The museum lists rewetting, blotting and scraping on this sheet: ways of taking color off the paper to win back lights."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-pastel.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Pastel","","pm-pastel","Pastel is pigment mixed with a white filler such as chalk, held in a stick by a trace of weak gum. · With so little binder, the color rests on the paper's tooth as loose powder. The powder scatters light, giving pastel its soft, velvety look, and it smudges at a touch. · Fixative sprays hold the powder, but they darken the colors and flatten the surface, so many pastellists did without. · Pastel needs no drying time, and a portrait took fewer sittings. It became a fashion after Rosalba Carriera visited Paris in 1720–21."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/portrait-of-maria-frederike-van-reede-athlone-at-seven-years-of-age.html","Binders and Media","Portrait of Maria Frederike van Reede-Athlone at Seven Years of Age","Jean-Étienne Liotard","liotard-maria-frederike","Jean-Étienne Liotard · 1755–56 · Pastel on vellum, 54.9 × 44.8 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · The pastel is laid on vellum, a fine prepared animal skin, and blended with a stump, a pointed blending tool. · Liotard disliked showing his strokes; look at the face and the velvet, where none can be seen."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-encaustic.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Encaustic","","pm-encaustic","Encaustic binds pigment in beeswax; the name is Greek for \"burnt in\". · The wax must be kept warm to brush, so painters worked quickly and went over the surface with heated tools. Some wax paint may have been worked without heat; the sources disagree. · Around 1,000 painted portraits survive from mummies of Roman Egypt, mostly from about the 1st to 3rd centuries CE; some are in glue tempera rather than wax. · These thin wooden panels survived because Egypt's climate is so dry."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/portrait-of-a-young-woman-in-red.html","Binders and Media","Portrait of a young woman in red","Roman Egypt","fayum-young-woman-red","Roman Egypt · 90–120 CE · Encaustic on limewood with gold leaf, 38.1 × 18.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Met study (published by the Getty) identifies the paint as pigment in beeswax, laid straight onto a sliver of linden wood only millimeters thick, with no ground. · Its colors: lead white, iron ochres, a see-through red lake for the garment and gold leaf; a copper pigment darkens the shadows of the face. · Look around the nose and under the lower lip: parallel marks left by a heated tool."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/supports-and-grounds.html","Supports and Grounds · Overview","Supports and Grounds","","pm-layers","What the paint is laid on, and the layer that prepares it · A panel or canvas painting rests on a support, wood or cloth, usually coated with a ground before any paint goes on. Both shape how the picture looks and how it ages. · Wood panels · Canvas · Grounds · Diagram · Layers of a panel painting"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-wood-panels.html","Supports and Grounds · Concept","Wood Panels","","pm-panel","From the 1200s to the early 1500s, most surviving portable paintings are on wood: one plank or several glued and doweled into a board. · Wood takes up moisture and gives it back as the air changes, swelling and shrinking. Over centuries a panel can warp and split, as at right, and loosen the paint on it. · Earlier restorers planed warped panels thinner and glued wooden lattices (cradles) to the back; conservators now use gentler methods."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/portrait-of-a-lady.html","Supports and Grounds","Portrait of a Lady","Rogier van der Weyden","vanderweyden-portrait-lady","Rogier van der Weyden · about 1460 · Oil on oak panel, 37 × 27 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The panel is a single oak board, and its tree rings can be dated. · Its 265 rings end in 1438. Adding the missing outer rings and time for the wood to season, the panel was probably not painted before about 1463, so it may be a few years later than its usual date. · The oak probably came from the Baltic region. · A bare margin around the paint shows it was painted with a frame already fixed to the panel."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-canvas.html","Supports and Grounds · Concept","Canvas","","encyclopedie-peinture-pl4-chassis","Canvas is a tough cloth, usually linen, pulled tight over a wooden frame. · It weighs less than wood and can be rolled up. Venice switched to canvas from panels and walls between about 1450 and 1550; damp air and the sailcloth trade are the usual reasons given, though recent research doubts any single one. · Seen from the back: a crossbar and corner braces keep the frame square. · Until the 1800s, big canvases were usually sewn from strips; the Titian on the next page is an exception."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/the-vendramin-family-venerating-a-relic-of-the-true-cross.html","Supports and Grounds","The Vendramin Family, venerating a Relic of the True Cross","Titian","titian-vendramin-family","Titian · about 1540–45 · Oil on canvas, 206.1 × 288.5 cm. The National Gallery, London · Nearly 3 m wide, this canvas is a single piece with no seam, woven in a patterned twill. · Patterned cloth like this was woven far wider than a meter. · It has been lined, backed with a second canvas, probably three times, most recently with wax-resin in 1961."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-grounds.html","Supports and Grounds · Concept","Grounds","","pm-layers","A ground is the coat laid on the wood or canvas before any paint, giving the paint a smooth, even surface to hold on to. · Its color glows through thin paint and affects the colors laid over it. · Gesso: white gypsum and glue, the Italian panel ground, sanded glass-smooth. · North of the Alps, chalk and glue did the same job. · From about 1600 many painters chose warm brown or red grounds instead of white. · In the 1800s some painters, the Impressionists above all, preferred white."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/samson-and-delilah.html","Supports and Grounds","Samson and Delilah","Peter Paul Rubens","rubens-samson-delilah","Peter Paul Rubens · about 1609–10 · Oil on oak, 185 × 205 cm. The National Gallery, London · Over the white chalk ground, Rubens brushed a streaky yellow-brown layer, and he let it show. · In the folds of Delilah's white sleeve he left the layer bare as a warm half-shadow. · The layer is an earth color like umber, with a little lead white, in oil. · A 1983 National Gallery study by Joyce Plesters traced the layers, down to the six oak planks."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/how-paintings-change.html","How Paintings Change · Overview","How Paintings Change","","pm-fading","Fading, darkening and yellow varnish, and how scientists see beneath the surface · No painting looks exactly as it did when it left the studio. Some pigments fade or darken, varnish yellows, and scientists use X-rays, infrared and tiny samples to work out what has changed. · Fading and darkening · Varnish · Looking beneath the surface · Diagram · Four pigments, then and now"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/theme-fading-and-darkening.html","How Paintings Change · Concept","Fading and Darkening","","pm-fading","Some pigments change color because they react with light, air or the oil around them. · Red lakes fade in light; see Color Theory, How Pigments Behave. As potassium seeps out of smalt's glass into the oil, the blue fades and the oil yellows, leaving gray-brown. Copper greens darken in light. Lead white can turn black in polluted air, mainly on paper and in water-based paint. · Copper greens hidden under a frame often stay green. · Chrome yellow can darken too: a 2011 X-ray study of two Van Gogh paintings found light had likely altered a thin surface layer. · Darkened lead white on drawings can be turned white again with hydrogen peroxide, though it may darken again."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/the-bedroom.html","How Paintings Change","The Bedroom","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-bedroom","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 73.6 × 92.3 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · Researchers suspect the walls and doors, now light blue (cobalt blue with zinc white), were once violet, and that the red in the mix has faded. · Dark red lake particles turned up in the door paint. · The floor contains geranium lake, a red that has probably faded. · In the Amsterdam version, a strip under tape since 1931 keeps a peachier floor, and one stroke hidden by the frame is still lilac (Hendriks and colleagues, 2011)."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/theme-varnish.html","How Paintings Change · Concept","Varnish","","pm-varnish","Varnish is a clear top coat that evens out the gloss, deepens the colors and protects the paint. · Natural-resin varnishes such as mastic and dammar yellow, cloud and crack with age, as the middle panel shows. Because varnish is the top layer, conservators can usually remove it and apply fresh varnish. · The National Gallery of Art suggests the look of varnish matters more than its protection. · Cleaning is not always safe: Reynolds mixed mastic into his paint, so varnish solvents attack it too (David Peggie, National Gallery scientist, in a 2014 Royal Society of Chemistry lecture)."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/theme-looking-beneath-the-surface.html","How Paintings Change · Concept","Looking Beneath the Surface","","pm-analysis","Conservators identify materials with methods that see through paint or read its chemical elements, most of them without taking a sample. · X-rays show changes the painter covered over, where heavy paint such as lead white is involved. Infrared can reveal a carbon drawing under the paint. X-ray fluorescence (XRF) names the elements at a spot, and the pigment is worked out from them. · Scanning XRF across a whole painting can map hidden earlier versions. · When a sample is needed, it is smaller than a pinhead and comes from an already damaged spot, as a last resort."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","Post-Impressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","vangogh-starry-night","Structure, feeling, flat colour and pattern after Impressionism · Post-Impressionism is the label given, after the fact, to painters working in France from about 1884 to 1906 who started from Impressionism and went past it. They kept its bright colour and visible brushwork, and used them for something other than recording light: solid structure, strong feeling, flat colour, or pattern. · Vincent van Gogh · The Starry Night · 1889"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/bathers-at-asnieres.html","Post-Impressionism","Bathers at Asnières","Georges Seurat","seurat-bathers","Georges Seurat · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 79 1/4 × 118 in. The National Gallery, London · Georges Seurat (French, 1859–1891). The whole surface is made of small separate touches of paint, and the figures hold as still as statues. · Short crisscross strokes cover the grass, water and sky, so nothing is blended smooth. · Every figure is seen in profile or straight from behind, and the group is spaced along the bank from the lower left corner up to the far right. · The light is hazy and even, with no hard shadows anywhere. · Rejected by the official Salon and shown instead at the first Salon des Indépendants, a jury-free exhibition, in 1884. Seurat later reworked the orange hat in the dot technique."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-circus.html","Post-Impressionism","The Circus","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus","Georges Seurat · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 73 1/4 × 59 3/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · The colour is built entirely from small dots, and the rising curves of the ring are set against the straight rows of the stands. · Dots of red, yellow and blue sit side by side on a white ground, and the eye mixes them at a distance. · The horse, rider and clowns are all arcs and spirals; the seats and audience behind are a grid of straight lines. · White dominates, so the three colours read as bright accents. · A dark blue border is painted on the canvas itself, part of the picture rather than a frame. · Shown unfinished at the Salon des Indépendants in March 1891; Seurat died days after the opening."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-basket-of-apples.html","Post-Impressionism","The Basket of Apples","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-basket","Paul Cézanne · about 1893 · Oil on canvas, 25 1/2 × 31 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Paul Cézanne (French, 1839–1906). The table does not line up: its front edge sits lower on the left of the cloth than on the right, as if seen from two places at once. · The tabletop rises steeply toward the viewer, so the fruit seems about to roll forward. · The basket tilts and the bottle leans, and neither is corrected. · Each apple is a solid ball of colour, shaded from green through orange to red in broad flat strokes. · Dark blue outlines run around the bottle, the cloth folds and the plate. · Shown at Cézanne's first one-man exhibition, at the Vollard gallery in Paris, 1895."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-card-players.html","Post-Impressionism","The Card Players","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-card-players","Paul Cézanne · 1890–92 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 32 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The men are built like solid blocks, with cylinders for arms and bodies and the hats as plain dark shapes on top. · The three seated players form a stable, balanced group around the table, and nobody moves. · Coats and sleeves are shaded in broad planes of blue, brown and cream, not in soft gradations. · The wall behind is loosely scrubbed in pale blue and lilac, so the figures stand out as the solid parts. · The four pipes on the wall are each a few dark strokes with a touch of white on the bowl, set straight onto the scrubbed blue. · The earliest of five pictures of card players; each sitter, a local farm worker, posed alone for studies that were then combined."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/mont-sainte-victoire.html","Post-Impressionism","Mont Sainte-Victoire","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-sainte-victoire","Paul Cézanne · 1902–04 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 36 1/4 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art · The valley and the sky are both made of patches of parallel brushstrokes, and colour alone tells which is near and which is far. · Green and ochre patches in the lower half are each a cluster of short strokes running the same way. · The mountain and the sky share the same violets and blues, so the peak almost dissolves into the air. · The darkest greens and browns gather along the bottom edge, and the picture lightens step by step up to the pale peak. · A broken dark line marks the mountain's outer edge and a few roofs; most other shapes have no drawn edge at all. · The mountain stands near Aix-en-Provence, Cézanne's home town, and he painted it again and again in his last years."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-large-bathers.html","Post-Impressionism","The Large Bathers","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-bathers","Paul Cézanne · 1900–06 · Oil on canvas, 82 3/4 × 98 3/4 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art · The bending tree trunks form a huge arch over the figures, and the two groups of bathers fit inside as a triangle. · The trunks lean in from both sides and meet near the top, locking the composition into a pointed arch. · The bathers are simplified to a few planes each, with faces barely marked. · Patches of bare canvas show through across the sky and the bodies. · The same blues run through the sky, the water and the shadows on the bodies. · The largest and last of Cézanne's bathers pictures, left unfinished."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-potato-eaters.html","Post-Impressionism","The Potato Eaters","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-potato-eaters","Vincent van Gogh · 1885 · Oil on canvas, 32 1/4 × 45 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam (Vincent van Gogh Foundation) · Vincent van Gogh (Dutch, 1853–1890). Before the bright colour came the opposite: a dark room lit by one lamp, painted in earth colours. · The single oil lamp is the only light source, and every face and hand is modelled by its glow. · The palette is browns, greys and muddy greens, with hardly a touch of clear blue or red. · Faces and hands are deliberately coarse and knobbly, drawn with dark contours. · The paint is thick and the brushwork rough, with the strokes left visible in the dark. · Painted at Nuenen, in the Dutch countryside, as a showpiece; Van Gogh compared the earth colours to a dusty, unpeeled potato."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-night-cafe.html","Post-Impressionism","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-night-cafe","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/2 × 36 1/4 in. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · Red walls clash against a green ceiling and billiard table, and the floorboards rush away in steep diagonals. · Blood red walls and a green ceiling and table, opposites on the colour wheel, fight each other across the room. · The floor is the largest area, a field of yellow strokes that makes the room feel tilted. · Each gaslamp is ringed with concentric strokes of yellow and orange, painting the glare itself. · The owner's white suit turns pale green and lemon yellow under the lamps. · Shows a café in Arles, the Alcazar, where Van Gogh took his meals; he wrote that the clashing colours express 'the terrible passions of humanity'."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/sunflowers.html","Post-Impressionism","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 36 1/4 × 28 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Almost the whole picture is one colour: yellow flowers in a yellow vase on a yellow table against a yellow wall. · The yellows run from pale lemon in the background through gold to deep ochre in the seed heads. · The seed heads are built up in thick ridges of paint, so they stand off the canvas. · A thin blue line marks the table edge and a band around the vase; apart from the green stems and leaves, nothing else breaks the yellows. · The background is pale yellow, brushed thin in short crossing strokes, against the heavy paint of the heads. · Painted to decorate the house at Arles before Gauguin's visit, one of a series; the flowers run from fresh bud to withered head."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-starry-night.html","Post-Impressionism","The Starry Night","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-starry-night","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 29 × 36 1/4 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · The sky is made of long curling strokes that spiral and roll across the canvas, each one visible. · Two great spirals turn in the centre of the sky, built from short strokes laid end to end. · Each star and the moon sits inside a ring of pale strokes. · The cypress rises as a dark flame, painted in strokes that lick upward. · The village below is small and still, with straight little houses that set off the churning sky. · Based on the view from Van Gogh's window at the asylum of Saint-Rémy, painted in daytime sessions; the village was added from other views."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/self-portrait.html","Post-Impressionism","Self-Portrait","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-self-portrait-1889","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 25 1/2 × 21 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · The same short curved strokes cover the face, the jacket and the background, so the man and the wall behind him move together. · The background is a field of swirling curls in pale green and blue, with no flat area anywhere. · The strokes in the hair and beard run in the same curling rhythm as the background. · Cool greens and turquoise fill the picture, and the orange hair and beard are the main warm note. · The eyes are the only still point, painted in sharp focus. · One of more than forty self-portraits Van Gogh made in about ten years; here he is dressed in a suit instead of the work clothes he usually painted himself in."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/vision-of-the-sermon-jacob-wrestling-with-the-angel.html","Post-Impressionism","Vision of the Sermon (Jacob Wrestling with the Angel)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-vision","Paul Gauguin · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/2 × 35 3/4 in. Scottish National Gallery, Edinburgh · Paul Gauguin (French, 1848–1903). The ground is a flat field of pure red, and every shape sits on it inside a dark outline. · The red runs almost unbroken from edge to edge, with no shading and no grass. · The white Breton bonnets are flat shapes with hard edges, outlined in dark blue. · A tree trunk cuts the picture on a diagonal, separating the praying women from the small wrestlers. · The cow and the wrestlers are small, as if far away, but the flat red gives them no distance from the large heads in front. · Breton women are praying after a sermon about Jacob wrestling the angel; Gauguin wrote that the fight exists only in their imagination."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-talisman.html","Post-Impressionism","The Talisman","Paul Sérusier","serusier-talisman","Paul Sérusier · 1888 · Oil on wood, 10 3/4 × 8 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Paul Sérusier (French, 1864–1927). The landscape is reduced to patches of pure colour, with almost no drawing left. · Yellow, orange, blue, violet and green sit as separate blocks, each a single unmixed tone. · The trees at upper left, a red path and a blue mill at right can still be read, but only just. · Reflections in the water below are the same flat patches as the trees above them, with no ripple or blur. · Dark brown and violet masses at the right balance the bright yellow, and nothing is shaded. · Painted at Pont-Aven in October 1888 under Gauguin's direction; shown in Paris to the painters who formed the Nabis, for whom the panel became a talisman."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/where-do-we-come-from-what-are-we-where-are-we-going.html","Post-Impressionism","Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going?","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-where","Paul Gauguin · 1897–98 · Oil on canvas, 54 3/4 × 147 1/2 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · The picture is a long band that reads from right to left, a sleeping baby at one end and an old woman at the other, in muted blue-green. · Figures stand or sit in a row across the front, with the landscape behind them. · Deep blue and green fill most of the ground, and the bodies are ochre and brown with little shading. · The two upper corners are yellow, like the worn edges of an old wall painting. · A blue idol at the left stands stiff and symmetrical, in a different pose from everyone else. · Painted in Tahiti; Gauguin wanted the effect of a wall painting, and first showed it at Vollard's Paris gallery in 1898."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/at-the-moulin-rouge.html","Post-Impressionism","At the Moulin Rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-moulin-rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–95 · Oil on canvas, 48 1/2 × 55 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec (French, 1864–1901). A railing slices across the lower left, and the face cut off by the right edge is turned green by electric light. · The woman at the right is lit from below in acid green, with her hair bright yellow. · The orange railing runs diagonally through the foreground, blocking the viewer's way in. · The paint is thin and streaky, with the brown of the canvas showing through the figures and floor. · Black top hats and dark coats make flat silhouettes against the green wall behind. · Lautrec's own friends fill the table at the Moulin Rouge; he is in the centre background beside his cousin."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/divan-japonais.html","Post-Impressionism","Divan Japonais","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-divan","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–93 · Lithograph printed in four colours, 31 3/4 × 24 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The seated woman is a single flat black shape, and the singer on stage is cut off at the neck by the top edge. · The black dress and hat are one unshaded silhouette; only the orange hair and a sliver of face are picked out. · The stage, the orchestra and the audience are flat planes of yellow, grey and olive with thick outlines. · The performer at upper left is known only from her long black gloves, with her head outside the picture. · The lettering is drawn by hand and sits inside the design rather than in a separate band. · An advertisement for a Montmartre café-concert; the spectator is Jane Avril, the performer on stage Yvette Guilbert, two stars of the district."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/woman-in-a-striped-dress.html","Post-Impressionism","Woman in a Striped Dress","Édouard Vuillard","vuillard-striped","Édouard Vuillard · 1895 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 23 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Édouard Vuillard (French, 1868–1940). The woman's dress, the flowers and the wall are all pattern, so the figure nearly disappears into the room. · Red and white stripes on the sleeves are painted in loose dabs that match the dabs of the flowers beside them. · The background is dark red and mottled, the same red as the stripes, so there is no clear edge between dress and wall. · Faces are blurred to a few strokes while the patterns are sharp. · The palette is held to reds, whites, greens and yellows across the whole canvas. · Made in 1895 for Thadée Natanson, who published the magazine La Revue blanche, as one of five decorations for his home; the red-haired woman may be his wife Misia."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-sleeping-gypsy.html","Post-Impressionism","The Sleeping Gypsy","Henri Rousseau","rousseau-sleeping-gypsy","Henri Rousseau · 1897 · Oil on canvas, 51 × 79 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Henri Rousseau (French, 1844–1910). Every edge is hard and every surface smooth, with no visible brushwork and no haze in the distance. · The lion, the sleeper and the mandolin are outlined cleanly, like cut-outs placed on the sand. · The far mountains are as crisp as the foreground, so the distance does not soften. · The stripes of the robe are painted one by one in flat, even bands. · The sky is a smooth gradient from dark blue to pale, with the moon a plain white disc. · Shown in Paris at the 1897 Salon des Indépendants."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism.html","Start","Realism","","","Realism"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/barbizon-and-the-open-air.html","Barbizon and the Open Air · Overview","Barbizon and the Open Air","","corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","Landscape painters around Fontainebleau and the Oise, 1830s–1860s · Beginning before Realism had a name, a group of landscape painters worked from close study in the forest near the village of Barbizon, south of Paris, and along the Oise to the north. They painted real French places, and their example opened the way for painting ordinary life. · Corot's forest and a remembered lake · Rousseau, Diaz and Daubigny: named places, real light · A State-commissioned farm scene · A photographer in the same woods · Camille Corot · Forest of Fontainebleau · 1834"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/forest-of-fontainebleau.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Forest of Fontainebleau","Camille Corot","corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","Camille Corot · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 175.6 × 242.6 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A large forest view, painted for the Salon of 1834 from sketches made outdoors. · It was still framed as a historic landscape: viewers took the woman reading in the foreground for Mary Magdalene. · The National Gallery notes the \"clarity and freshness of vision\" that set Corot apart from formula landscapes. · The forest, not the small figure, holds the eye. · Salon · the official annual or biennial art exhibition in Paris, where artists built their reputations. · Historic landscape · a landscape with a story from history, myth or the Bible, a category meant to raise landscape's status."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/souvenir-of-mortefontaine.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Souvenir of Mortefontaine","Camille Corot","corot-souvenir-of-mortefontaine","Camille Corot · about 1864 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 89 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A silvery lake scene recalled rather than recorded; the title means a remembrance. · Napoleon III bought it at the Salon of 1864; it entered the Louvre in 1889. · Look at the feathery trees and soft gray-green light, Corot's late manner. · It shows the road Realism did not take: a dreamy, remembered landscape, the kind of official taste Realism pushed against."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/sunset-from-the-forest-of-fontainebleau.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Sunset from the Forest of Fontainebleau","Théodore Rousseau","rousseau-sunset-fontainebleau","Théodore Rousseau · 1848 · Oil on paper over black chalk or conté crayon, laid down on canvas, 32.2 × 45.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A small study of the setting sun through the trees, with cattle near water. · Rousseau settled at Barbizon and was a leading figure of the group. · It is painted on paper over a chalk drawing, a small, quick format suited to recording light. · Its subject is one hour of light in one real place, not an ideal or a story. · Barbizon school · landscape painters, among them Rousseau, Diaz, Daubigny and Troyon, who worked in and around the Forest of Fontainebleau from the 1830s."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-edge-of-the-forest-at-les-monts-girard-fontainebleau.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard, Fontainebleau","Narcisse Diaz de la Peña","diaz-edge-of-forest","Narcisse Diaz de la Peña · 1868 · Oil on canvas, 97.8 × 125.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Storm light breaks over dark trees at a named spot in the forest. · Look at the heavy, broken paint in the foliage. · Dark, dense paint gives the trees weight and the light its brightness by contrast."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/washerwomen-at-the-oise-river-near-valmondois.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois","Charles-François Daubigny","daubigny-washerwomen-oise","Charles-François Daubigny · 1865 · Oil on wood, 24 × 46 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A wide, quiet river with two small figures doing laundry at the water's edge. · The panel is small, the size of a study, though it is signed and dated. · Valmondois is on the Oise, north of Paris, the river Daubigny painted again and again. · A low horizon gives most of the panel to sky and water."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/oxen-going-to-work-morning-effect.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Oxen Going to Work, Morning Effect","Constant Troyon","troyon-oxen-going-to-work","Constant Troyon · 1855 · Oil on canvas, 262 × 391 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris (on deposit from the Louvre) · Oxen head out to plow at sunrise, painted on a canvas nearly four meters wide. · The State commissioned it in 1854 and showed it at the 1855 Exposition Universelle. · Look at the low sun behind the team and the long shadows thrown toward the viewer. · A farm-animal subject on a canvas nearly four meters wide, at the scale of history painting. · History painting · large pictures of history, religion or myth, ranked highest by the academies."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/carrefour-de-lepine.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Carrefour de l'Epine","Eugène Cuvelier","cuvelier-forest-scene","Eugène Cuvelier · early 1860s · Salted paper print, 19.3 × 25.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A photograph of bare trees at the Carrefour de l'Épine, a named crossing in the Forest of Fontainebleau. · Cuvelier worked in the circle of Corot and photographed the same woods the painters used. · The Met's Cuvelier study notes that he printed from paper negatives, which give the soft, grainy tone. · The photograph shares the painters' aim: a real spot, recorded without arranging it. · Salted paper print · an early photographic print on paper coated with salt and silver, with a matte surface."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/courbet.html","Courbet · Overview","Courbet","","courbet-painters-studio","The painter who claimed the name Realist · Gustave Courbet, from the village of Ornans in eastern France, painted laborers, villagers and his own friends, often at the size of history painting. Critics attacked him, and he answered with his own exhibition and a manifesto. · The road workers and the village funeral · The great studio allegory · Village girls, a roadside meeting and two young women by the Seine · Landscape and sea from his own region and the coast · Gustave Courbet · The Painter's Studio · 1854–55"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-stonebreakers.html","Courbet","The Stonebreakers","Gustave Courbet","courbet-stonebreakers","Gustave Courbet · 1849 · Oil on canvas. Formerly Gemäldegalerie, Dresden; lost during the Second World War · Two road workers, one too old and one too young for the job, break stones by a roadside. · Shown at the Salon of 1850–51; the Dresden picture is known today from photographs, and a smaller version survives in Winterthur. · In a letter he said the two men he saw by the road were \"the most complete expression of poverty.\" · Their faces are turned away or shaded, so they stand for a kind of work rather than two individuals. · Jonathan Ribner of Boston University contrasts its stiff, piece-by-piece composition with the flowing unity of Millet's figures. · One of the first pictures to show manual labor plainly, without uplift."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/a-burial-at-ornans.html","Courbet","A Burial at Ornans","Gustave Courbet","courbet-burial-at-ornans","Gustave Courbet · 1849–50 · Oil on canvas, 315 × 668 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A village funeral, with Courbet's family and neighbors as the mourners, on a canvas almost seven meters long. · Shown at the same Salon as The Stonebreakers; critics called it vulgar and ugly. · The Musée d'Orsay points to the visible brushwork and deep blacks, applied with brush and palette knife, against academic polish. · Who is being buried is unknown. · Ordinary villagers painted life-size, a scale normally given to religious and mythic subjects, made it the manifesto of the new style."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-painters-studio.html","Courbet","The Painter's Studio","Gustave Courbet","courbet-painters-studio","Gustave Courbet · 1854–55 · Oil on canvas, 361 × 598 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Courbet paints a landscape at the center of a crowded studio. · Its full title calls it a \"real allegory\" of seven years of his artistic life. · On the right are friends and backers, among them Baudelaire reading; a hunter on the left may have Napoleon III's features. · From 1899 to 1919 it hung as the backdrop of a private amateur theater in Paris. · He turns his own studio and career into the subject of a monumental picture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/theme-the-pavilion-of-realism.html","Courbet · Theme","The Pavilion of Realism","","courbet-burial-at-ornans","In 1855, after the Exposition Universelle jury turned down The Painter's Studio, Courbet built his own pavilion and showed his work there. · He paid for it himself and put it within sight of the official exhibition. The show included The Painter's Studio and A Burial at Ornans, with more than forty works in all. · Its catalogue carried his Realist manifesto, promising subjects from modern life. · In the catalogue preface he wrote that the title of Realist had been imposed on him. · It was a step outside the official system that later independent shows, including the Impressionists' in 1874, took further."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/young-ladies-of-the-village.html","Courbet","Young Ladies of the Village","Gustave Courbet","courbet-young-ladies-village","Gustave Courbet · 1851–52 · Oil on canvas, 194.9 × 261 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Courbet's three sisters, out walking near Ornans; one gives alms to a young cowherd. · Salon critics in 1852 found it crude and mocked the models' plain features and country dress. · X-rays show Courbet enlarged the figures as he worked. · Calling country girls \"demoiselles\", young ladies, blurred class lines, part of what unsettled Paris critics."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-meeting-bonjour-monsieur-courbet.html","Courbet","The Meeting (Bonjour Monsieur Courbet)","Gustave Courbet","courbet-bonjour-monsieur-courbet","Gustave Courbet · 1854 · Oil on canvas, 132 × 150 cm. Musée Fabre, Montpellier · Alfred Bruyas, a collector, and his servant greet Courbet on a country road; the painter has his gear strapped to his back. · Courbet himself reported the nickname \"Bonjour Monsieur Courbet\" in an 1855 letter. · Unlike The Painter's Studio, it was accepted for the 1855 Exposition Universelle, where caricaturists mocked its vanity. · Bruyas gave it to the Musée Fabre in 1868. · Painter and patron meet as equals on a dusty road, with the artist shown as a working traveler."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/young-ladies-on-the-banks-of-the-seine-summer.html","Courbet","Young Ladies on the Banks of the Seine (Summer)","Gustave Courbet","courbet-young-ladies-seine","Gustave Courbet · 1856–57 · Oil on canvas, 174 × 206 cm. Petit Palais, Musée des Beaux-Arts de la Ville de Paris · Two young Parisian women lie on the riverbank on a summer day, one half asleep. · It caused a critical scandal at the Salon of 1857. · The Petit Palais finds its realism in \"the frankness of the faces and postures.\" · Rumpled clothes and unguarded poses, with no classical pretext, carry the picture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-stream-le-ruisseau-du-puits-noir-vallee-de-la-loue.html","Courbet","The Stream (Le Ruisseau du Puits-Noir; vallée de la Loue)","Gustave Courbet","courbet-stream-puits-noir","Gustave Courbet · 1855 · Oil on canvas, 104 × 137 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A shaded stream in a rocky valley near Ornans, thought to be the first of several views of the site. · Shown at the 1855 Exposition Universelle. · Courbet laid on paint with a palette knife in irregular layers, the National Gallery notes. · Rough paint for rough rock: the handling itself feels like the place."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-wave.html","Courbet","The Wave","Gustave Courbet","courbet-the-wave","Gustave Courbet · 1869–70 · Oil on canvas, 65.6 × 92.4 cm. Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main · A wave breaks under a stormy sky on the Normandy coast near Étretat. · The Städel says he painted the scene about sixty times between 1865 and 1870. · Look at the thick paint of the foam against the dark water. · Sea and weather alone, with no figures or story, are the whole subject."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/peasants-and-labor.html","Peasants and Labor · Overview","Peasants and Labor","","millet-gleaners","Millet, Bonheur and the painters of rural France, 1849–1884 · After the Revolution of 1848, peasants and farm work became a major subject. Some painters gave farm labor dignity and grandeur; others showed its toll, and were accused of politics or ugliness. · Millet's sower, gleaners, angelus, hoe and rake · Rosa Bonheur's working animals · Breton's and Bastien-Lepage's later naturalism · Jean-François Millet · The Gleaners · 1857"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-sower.html","Peasants and Labor","The Sower","Jean-François Millet","millet-sower","Jean-François Millet · 1850 · Oil on canvas, 101.6 × 82.6 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · At dusk a peasant flings seed as he strides downhill, oxen harrowing the field behind him. · Ribner sees a near-biblical dignity in the sower that the stonebreakers lack. · The MFA quotes a 19th-century critic: he \"seems painted with the earth that he sows.\" · The American painter William Morris Hunt bought it from Millet around 1851. · One field worker given the scale and stride of a hero, painted soon after the 1848 Revolution gave male farm laborers the vote."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-gleaners.html","Peasants and Labor","The Gleaners","Jean-François Millet","millet-gleaners","Jean-François Millet · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 83.5 × 110 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Three women bend to pick up stray grain left after the harvest, while the main harvest goes on far behind them. · Shown at the Salon of 1857, after nearly a decade spent on the subject. · A rider at the right, probably an estate overseer, marks who is in charge. · Ribner notes that the women never look capable of revolt, though some viewers read the picture as subversive. · Three bent figures given the weight of sculpture, not the pathos of charity. · Gleaning · gathering what the harvesters leave behind, a right traditionally allowed to the rural poor."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-angelus.html","Peasants and Labor","The Angelus","Jean-François Millet","millet-angelus","Jean-François Millet · 1857–59 · Oil on canvas, 55.5 × 66 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A man and woman stop digging potatoes to pray as the evening bell rings. · Millet said the idea came from his grandmother, who stopped work to pray whenever the bell rang. · It became so famous that its sale abroad in 1889 caused an outcry in France; a man slashed it in 1932. · Prayer appears as part of the working day, in a real field with real tools. · Angelus · a Catholic prayer said at morning, noon and evening when a church bell rings."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/man-with-a-hoe.html","Peasants and Labor","Man with a Hoe","Jean-François Millet","millet-man-with-a-hoe","Jean-François Millet · 1860–62 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 100.3 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · An exhausted laborer leans on his hoe in a stony field full of thistles. · At the Salon of 1863 it caused a storm; Parisian viewers found the man brutish and frightening. · It was read as socialist protest; Millet denied being a socialist or a political agitator. · Millet wrote that he tried to say \"what I saw and felt\" at work in the fields. · No ennobling here: the cost of labor is written on the body."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/woman-with-a-rake.html","Peasants and Labor","Woman with a Rake","Jean-François Millet","millet-woman-with-rake","Jean-François Millet · probably 1856–57 · Oil on canvas, 39.7 × 34.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A single field worker rakes hay beside a haystack, the harvest going on behind her. · Millet first used the subject in a woodcut for a popular series on farm work, published in 1853. · It was shown in 1860 at the Martinet gallery, where Courbet and Manet also exhibited. · A subject first made for cheap prints became an easel painting."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/ploughing-in-the-nivernais.html","Peasants and Labor","Ploughing in the Nivernais","Rosa Bonheur","bonheur-ploughing-nivernais","Rosa Bonheur · 1849 · Oil on canvas, 133 × 260 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two teams of oxen make the first ploughing of fallow land in central France, known as sombrage. · The State commissioned it in 1848; shown at the Salon of 1849, it was praised almost unanimously. · The Musée d'Orsay calls it a hymn to work in the fields. · The animals are studied as closely as portraits, and the land is a real region."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-horse-fair.html","Peasants and Labor","The Horse Fair","Rosa Bonheur","bonheur-horse-fair","Rosa Bonheur · 1852–55 · Oil on canvas, 244.5 × 506.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Grooms lead and ride horses at the Paris horse market on the boulevard de l'Hôpital. · Shown at the Salon of 1853, where it was widely praised; she retouched it in 1855. · The dome of the Salpêtrière shows in the left background. · A working Paris market scene, painted about five meters wide."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-song-of-the-lark.html","Peasants and Labor","The Song of the Lark","Jules Breton","breton-song-of-the-lark","Jules Breton · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 110.6 × 85.8 cm. Art Institute of Chicago · A young peasant woman with a sickle stops at sunrise to listen to a lark. · First shown at the Salon of 1885. · In 1934 it was named \"America's Best Loved Picture\" in a Chicago newspaper contest. · Breton was raised in Courrières, northern France, and painted its fields for much of his career. · A softer, more idealized vision of rural life than Millet's or Courbet's."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/haymaking.html","Peasants and Labor","Haymaking","Jules Bastien-Lepage","bastien-lepage-haymaking","Jules Bastien-Lepage · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 180 × 195 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two hay-makers rest in a mown field near his home village of Damvillers. · A great success at the Salon of 1878; the State bought it from his heirs in 1885. · Émile Zola called him \"grandson of Millet and Courbet\" and the picture the masterpiece of naturalism in painting. · The horizon sits near the top and the figures crowd the front edge, a cropping close to photography. · A later generation's naturalism: the workers' exhaustion shown plainly. · Naturalism · a later, more detailed form of Realism; the name Zola used for art that recorded life as observed."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/joan-of-arc.html","Peasants and Labor","Joan of Arc","Jules Bastien-Lepage","bastien-lepage-joan-of-arc","Jules Bastien-Lepage · 1879 · Oil on canvas, 254 × 279.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A peasant girl in a farm garden in Lorraine starts up as the saints appear to her. · Shown at the Salon of 1880. · After France lost part of Lorraine to Germany in 1871, Joan became a patriotic symbol. · Critics praised her expression but thought the ghostly saints clashed with the realism of the rest. · A history subject painted as if observed on an ordinary farm."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-city-and-the-street.html","The City and the Street · Overview","The City and the Street","","daumier-third-class-carriage","Daumier, early Manet and the changing face of Paris · In Paris, Realism meant the people of the street and the politics of the day. Daumier drew both for the press; Manet painted the city's poor and passers-by; a photographer recorded streets about to be torn down. · Daumier's political prints · Daumier's paintings of working Paris · Manet's street people, singer and dead bullfighter · Photographing old Paris · Honoré Daumier · The Third-Class Carriage · 1864"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/le-ventre-legislatif-the-legislative-belly.html","The City and the Street","Le Ventre législatif (The Legislative Belly)","Honoré Daumier","daumier-ventre-legislatif","Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 28.2 × 43.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The government ministers and deputies of 1834, sitting in tiers, each drawn as a recognizable caricature. · Daumier had modeled many of these politicians as small painted clay busts, which curator John Daniel Tilford of the Oglethorpe University Museum of Art describes as models for his lithographs. · The French title puns on the legislative body: ventre means belly. · Satire in the press made current politics a subject for art. · Lithograph · a print drawn in greasy crayon on stone, so it could be printed quickly in large editions for the illustrated press and print series."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/rue-transnonain-april-15-1834.html","The City and the Street","Rue Transnonain, April 15, 1834","Honoré Daumier","daumier-rue-transnonain","Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 28.6 × 44.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A man lies dead beside his bed, a child crushed under him, in an apartment after soldiers stormed the building and killed people inside. · It records a real event of the April 1834 riots in Paris. · After it appeared in a print-seller's window, Smarthistory reports, police tried to track down copies and confiscated the stone. · No hero and no symbol: one room, one family, as a witness might have found it."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-third-class-carriage.html","The City and the Street","The Third-Class Carriage","Honoré Daumier","daumier-third-class-carriage","Honoré Daumier · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 65.4 × 90.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A nursing mother, an old woman with a basket and a sleeping boy ride on a crowded wooden bench. · The Met says it takes up a lithograph from about ten years earlier. · It is unfinished; look for the grid of squaring lines used to transfer the design. · Daumier showed how ordinary people lived with the new railways and the industrial city."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-laundress.html","The City and the Street","The Laundress","Honoré Daumier","daumier-laundress","Honoré Daumier · about 1863 · Oil on wood, 48.9 × 33 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A washerwoman with a heavy bundle helps a child up the steps from the Seine. · Daumier could see laundresses returning from the river's laundry boats from his studio on the quai d'Anjou. · The paint has decayed, so the final digit of the date can no longer be read. · The figure is a dark mass against a bright city wall, a type of the urban laborer rather than a portrait."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-old-musician.html","The City and the Street","The Old Musician","Édouard Manet","manet-old-musician","Édouard Manet · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 187.4 × 248.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Five ragged figures gather around a bearded street violinist on waste ground. · First shown at the Galerie Martinet in Paris in 1863. · His models came from a poor neighborhood at the city's edge, just as Paris was being rebuilt around new boulevards. · Street people painted at a scale the National Gallery says was normally kept for the wealthy and powerful."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-dead-toreador.html","The City and the Street","The Dead Toreador","Édouard Manet","manet-dead-toreador","Édouard Manet · probably 1864 · Oil on canvas, 75.9 × 153.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A bullfighter lies dead on the ground, painted alone against a plain dark background. · It began as part of a larger picture shown at the Salon of 1864. · After critics and cartoonists mocked that canvas, Manet cut it up; the other piece, The Bullfight, is in the Frick Collection. · A contemporary death treated like a fact, not a drama."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/street-singer.html","The City and the Street","Street Singer","Édouard Manet","manet-street-singer","Édouard Manet · about 1862 · Oil on canvas, 171.1 × 105.8 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A singer with a guitar steps out of a café, eating cherries from a paper parcel. · Manet saw a singer leave a café and later had Victorine Meurent pose for the figure in his studio. · The MFA quotes Zola praising Manet's ability to \"state frankly what he sees.\" · A chance moment of city life, with an awkward, unguarded gesture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/rue-saint-jacques.html","The City and the Street","Rue Saint-Jacques","Charles Marville","marville-rue-saint-jacques","Charles Marville · 1864–67 · Albumen silver print, 30.6 × 27 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A narrow street in the Latin Quarter, photographed from a corner, looking down its length. · From 1862 Marville was the city of Paris's official photographer. · He photographed old streets slated for demolition in Baron Haussmann's rebuilding of the city. · Peeling posters and painted shop signs on the walls: the old street itself is the subject. · Albumen print · a photographic print made with egg white to hold the image, giving fine detail; the usual process from the 1850s to the 1890s."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/britain-and-europe.html","Britain and Europe · Overview","Britain and Europe","","repin-barge-haulers","Realism in Britain, Germany and Russia · Painters across Europe took up modern life and working people, each in their own way: moral storytelling in Britain, close detail in Germany, social witness in Russia. · Britain: work, emigration, poverty and the railway · Germany: the factory, the village church, a mocked debut · Russia: the haulers and the returning exile · Ilya Repin · Barge Haulers on the Volga · 1870–73"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/work.html","Britain and Europe","Work","Ford Madox Brown","brown-work","Ford Madox Brown · 1852–65 · Oil on canvas, 137 × 197.3 cm. Manchester Art Gallery · Navvies dig up a Hampstead street while people of every class pass by. · Brown showed it at his own exhibition in 1865, with a catalogue essay explaining the figures. · The two men at the right are portraits of the writers Thomas Carlyle and F. D. Maurice. · A street scene packed with real types becomes an argument about the value of labor. · Navvy · a laborer digging roads, canals or railways."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-last-of-england.html","Britain and Europe","The Last of England","Ford Madox Brown","brown-last-of-england","Ford Madox Brown · 1852–55 · Oil on panel, 82.5 × 75 cm. Birmingham Museums Trust · A young couple stare grimly ahead as their ship leaves England, the woman holding a baby's hand under her shawl. · The circular picture sits inside a painted gold border carrying its title. · Emigration, a real choice facing many Victorians, painted as a modern drama."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/applicants-for-admission-to-a-casual-ward.html","Britain and Europe","Applicants for Admission to a Casual Ward","Luke Fildes","fildes-casual-ward","Luke Fildes · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 137.1 × 243.7 cm. Royal Holloway, University of London · Homeless men, women and children wait in the snow outside a London night shelter. · It grew from Fildes's 1869 illustration Houseless and Hungry for the weekly The Graphic. · At the Royal Academy in 1874, Royal Holloway says, the painting needed \"a barrier and a police officer\" to protect it from the crowds. · It was bought for Thomas Holloway in 1883. · The homeless poor, unidealized, given the finish of an Academy picture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-railway-station.html","Britain and Europe","The Railway Station","William Powell Frith","frith-railway-station","William Powell Frith · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 116.7 × 256.4 cm. Royal Holloway, University of London · Crowds board a train at Paddington Station: families, a wedding party, porters and passengers of every kind. · The dealer Louis Flatow bought the painting together with the rights to exhibit and reproduce it; more than 21,000 people came to see it in seven weeks. · At the right, two real, well-known London detectives arrest a man about to board. · Modern public life in all its variety, sold as a popular spectacle."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-iron-rolling-mill-modern-cyclopes.html","Britain and Europe","The Iron Rolling Mill (Modern Cyclopes)","Adolph Menzel","menzel-iron-rolling-mill","Adolph Menzel · 1872–75 · Oil on canvas, 158 × 254 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Workers drag a white-hot iron bloom through the rollers to make a railway rail. · In 1872 Menzel traveled to an ironworks in Upper Silesia to draw the machines and the men. · The Berlin museum notes that no factory owner commissioned it. · The museum calls a factory scene on this scale unprecedented in Europe."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/three-women-in-church.html","Britain and Europe","Three Women in Church","Wilhelm Leibl","leibl-three-women-in-church","Wilhelm Leibl · 1878–82 · Oil on mahogany panel, 113 × 77 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle · A young woman and two older ones sit in a pew in a Bavarian village church, one praying, two reading their prayer books. · Leibl worked on it for some three and a half years inside the church, very close to his models. · Look at the detail: embroidered clothes, rough hands, dust on the carved pew. · Real country women in an upright, altar-like format that honors Holbein; the museum calls it a main work of Realism."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/women-plucking-geese.html","Britain and Europe","Women Plucking Geese","Max Liebermann","liebermann-women-plucking-geese","Max Liebermann · 1871–72 · Oil on canvas, 118 × 172 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Women sit in a dim shed stripping feathers from geese while an old man brings more. · It was the first picture he showed in public, at about twenty-five, and it was mocked for its shabby subject and dark colors. · The sale paid for a journey to Paris, where he saw work by Millet and Courbet for the first time. · He took the broad, dark handling from the Hungarian painter Mihály Munkácsy, whose studio he had visited in 1871."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/barge-haulers-on-the-volga.html","Britain and Europe","Barge Haulers on the Volga","Ilya Repin","repin-barge-haulers","Ilya Repin · 1870–73 · Oil on canvas, 131.5 × 281 cm. State Russian Museum, St Petersburg · Eleven men in harness drag a barge along the riverbank under a hot sky. · Repin first saw haulers on the Neva in 1868 and made two trips to the Volga to study them. · Unlike Courbet's faceless stonebreakers, each hauler is based on a portrait of a real man. · Shown at the Vienna World's Fair in 1873; the Russian Museum says it opened a new era in genre painting. · Labor shown as a long, grinding line of exhaustion across the whole width of the canvas."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/they-did-not-expect-him.html","Britain and Europe","They Did Not Expect Him","Ilya Repin","repin-did-not-expect-him","Ilya Repin · 1884–88 · Oil on canvas, 160.5 × 167.5 cm. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · A political exile walks into his family's sitting room; the maid and the family freeze. · First shown in 1884 with the Wanderers. · Repin kept reworking the exile's face until 1888. · Family and friends posed, which keeps the scene grounded in a real home. · A political subject told through one ordinary moment of surprise. · The Wanderers (Peredvizhniki) · Russian Realist painters who formed in 1870 independent of the Academy, touring their exhibitions from 1871."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/america.html","America · Overview","America","","eakins-gross-clinic","Homer, Eakins and Tanner after the Civil War · American painters applied Realism to the decades after the Civil War: soldiers, schoolboys, a rower, a surgeon at work, a music lesson, and the sea. · Homer's war and its aftermath · Childhood and the sea · Eakins's rowers and surgeons · Tanner's banjo lesson · Thomas Eakins · Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic) · 1875"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/prisoners-from-the-front.html","America","Prisoners from the Front","Winslow Homer","homer-prisoners-from-the-front","Winslow Homer · 1866 · Oil on canvas, 61 × 96.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Union officer faces three captured Confederate soldiers on a stripped battlefield. · The Met says it was inspired by the courage of Barlow, a Union general and friend of Homer's. · The critic Eugene Benson saw the prisoners as types standing for \"the elements in our Southern society that fomented and fed the rebellion.\" · The war is shown through faces and clothes rather than battle."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-veteran-in-a-new-field.html","America","The Veteran in a New Field","Winslow Homer","homer-veteran-new-field","Winslow Homer · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 61.3 × 96.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A man with his back to us scythes ripe wheat; his Union jacket and canteen lie in the corner. · Finished in the months after Lee's surrender and Lincoln's assassination. · The Met notes the single-bladed scythe also recalls the Grim Reaper. · A soldier's return to farm work, shown plainly, which the Met reads as both grief and hope."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/snap-the-whip.html","America","Snap the Whip","Winslow Homer","homer-snap-the-whip","Winslow Homer · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 30.5 × 50.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Barefoot boys play a rough chain game outside a red country schoolhouse. · This is the smaller of two versions; the larger is at the Butler Institute of American Art. · The Met reads the linked boys as a hopeful image of the reunited nation. · A schoolyard game, observed, becomes a picture of national memory as America grew more urban."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-gulf-stream.html","America","The Gulf Stream","Winslow Homer","homer-gulf-stream","Winslow Homer · 1899, reworked by 1906 · Oil on canvas, 71.4 × 124.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Black sailor lies on the deck of a dismasted boat, sharks circling, a waterspout behind. · The Met notes that the current links the sugarcane on board to the history of transatlantic slavery. · Homer gives no outcome: whether the sailor is rescued is left open."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-champion-single-sculls-max-schmitt-in-a-single-scull.html","America","The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull)","Thomas Eakins","eakins-champion-single-sculls","Thomas Eakins · 1871 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 117.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · His friend Max Schmitt rests his oars on the Schuylkill River in Philadelphia, in clear, raking light. · It is believed to mark Schmitt's racing victory of October 1870. · Eakins put himself in the picture, rowing farther back. · The Met calls it the work that launched his career. · Modern sport and a real place, worked out with careful perspective and light."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/portrait-of-dr-samuel-d-gross-the-gross-clinic.html","America","Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic)","Thomas Eakins","eakins-gross-clinic","Thomas Eakins · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 243.8 × 199.4 cm. Philadelphia Museum of Art and Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts · The surgeon Samuel Gross pauses, scalpel in his bloody hand, to lecture during an operation. · Made for the 1876 Centennial Exhibition; the fair's art jury turned it down, so it hung in a model army hospital. · Many viewers, the Philadelphia Museum of Art says, were repelled by its \"ugly and inartistic realism.\" · In a 2009 lecture, Kathleen Foster related its dark ground and dramatic light to old masters such as Rembrandt and Velázquez. · A modern operation, painted from close knowledge of the clinic, with nothing softened."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-banjo-lesson.html","America","The Banjo Lesson","Henry Ossawa Tanner","tanner-banjo-lesson","Henry Ossawa Tanner · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 124.5 × 90.2 cm. Hampton University Museum, Hampton, Virginia · An older man helps a boy hold a banjo, cool light from one side and warm firelight from the other. · It was admitted to the Paris Salon of 1894. · The same year Robert C. Ogden, chair of Hampton's board, bought it and gave it to Hampton. · In the same 2009 University of Pennsylvania lecture, Tanner was introduced as Eakins's \"great student.\" · An intimate lesson between generations, observed closely, offered a respectful picture of Black life."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","Start","Romanticism","","","Romanticism"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-dark-side-of-reason.html","The Dark Side of Reason · Overview","The Dark Side of Reason","","fuseli-nightmare","Wright of Derby, Fuseli, Blake, Martin, Palmer and Turner, 1768–1835 · In the decades around 1800, British artists turned to dreams, visions and catastrophe, often against the confident science of their age. Some made pictures for London's crowded public exhibitions; others, like Blake, worked mainly for a few patrons and friends. · A forerunner: a science lesson lit by a single candle · Fuseli's bedroom demon and Blake's visionary prints and watercolors · A defiant poet, a moonlit harvest and a city fire as subjects for awe · Henry Fuseli · The Nightmare · 1781"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/an-experiment-on-a-bird-in-the-air-pump.html","The Dark Side of Reason","An Experiment on a Bird in the Air Pump","Joseph Wright of Derby","wright-air-pump","Joseph Wright of Derby · 1768 · Oil on canvas, 183 × 244 cm. National Gallery, London · A lecturer draws the air out of a glass bowl while a household watches a trapped bird struggle for breath. The National Gallery calls it a modern vanitas, a picture about the fragility of life. · First shown at the Society of Artists in London, then again that September for the visiting King of Denmark. · Look at the young couple beside the lecturer, Wright's friends Thomas Coltman and Mary Barlow, absorbed in each other. · A first idea for the composition survives, painted on the back of a Wright self-portrait. · Science becomes drama: candlelight picks faces out of the dark, each reacting differently to the bird's fate. Romantic artists took up this staging of feeling around a scene of reason. · Presented to the National Gallery by Edward Tyrrell in 1863."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-nightmare.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Nightmare","Henry Fuseli","fuseli-nightmare","Henry Fuseli · 1781 · Oil on canvas, 101.7 × 127.1 cm. Detroit Institute of Arts, Detroit · A sleeping woman lies sprawled across a bed while a demon squats on her chest and a horse's head pushes through the curtains. The picture made the Swiss-born painter's name in London. · Shown at the Royal Academy in 1782. In a 2022 lecture, Martin Myrone of the Paul Mellon Centre notes that, unusually for Fuseli, it cited no poem or play. · Look at the woman's head and arm, hanging limp off the edge of the bed toward the floor. · Myrone links the curtained room to fashionable bedroom scenes by painters such as Jean-Baptiste Le Prince; the demon was Fuseli's twist. · The theme is the sleeping mind. Putting dreams and irrational fear on a public exhibition wall was a bold step that made the inner life a fit subject for painting. · First owned by Sir Brooke Boothby; the Detroit Institute of Arts bought it in 1955. · Royal Academy · Britain's royal academy of art, founded in London in 1768, with a yearly exhibition and a school; a separate body from the Society of Artists."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-ancient-of-days.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Ancient of Days","William Blake","blake-ancient-of-days","William Blake · 1794 · Color-printed relief and white-line etching with oil, watercolor, and pen and black ink on paper, plate 23.2 × 16.8 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · A bearded figure leans out of a blazing disk and stretches a pair of compasses down into the dark void. It opens one of the illuminated books Blake wrote, printed and colored himself. · Made as the frontispiece to Europe: A Prophecy; this sheet comes from Copy A. · Look at the wind sweeping the figure's white hair and beard to one side, though he crouches in a still circle of light. · The Tate catalogue reads the design as an answer to the frontispiece of a 1729 edition of Newton's Principia. · Blake invented his own gods. This creator, measuring out the world, belongs to a personal mythology from his prophetic books rather than to church teaching. · Part of the Paul Mellon Collection; kept in the print Study Room and shown on request."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/newton.html","The Dark Side of Reason","Newton","William Blake","blake-newton","William Blake · 1795–c. 1805 · Color print, ink and watercolor on paper, 46 × 60 cm. Tate, London · A muscular nude scientist sits hunched on a rock, drawing with compasses on a sheet at his feet. Blake turns the hero of the Enlightenment into a man shut inside his own diagram. · First shown in public at the Burlington Fine Arts Club, London, in 1876, long after Blake's death. · Look at the murky green setting and the patterned rock, which the Tate catalogue reads as a scene under water. · The pose comes from Michelangelo's Abias in the Sistine Chapel, which Blake knew from an engraving. · Blake's attack on Newton is an early sign of a lasting Romantic distrust of science that reduces the living world to measurement. · Presented to the Tate by W. Graham Robertson in 1939."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-great-red-dragon-and-the-woman-clothed-with-the-sun.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Great Red Dragon and the Woman Clothed with the Sun","William Blake","blake-great-red-dragon","William Blake · c. 1805 · Pen and gray ink with watercolor over graphite, 40.8 × 33.7 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A horned, winged dragon hovers over a woman wrapped in light, who kneels on a dark, rough mass at the foot of the sheet with her arms flung wide. The scene comes from the Book of Revelation, where the dragon waits to devour her newborn child. · Made for Thomas Butts, Blake's loyal patron, as part of a long series of Bible pictures. · Look at the dragon's spread wings, which arch across the top of the sheet like a canopy. · One of four Great Red Dragon subjects Blake painted, each with a different composition. · A watercolor on a Bible theme is given the ambition of history painting. Blake treated a medium then ranked below oil as fit for the grandest visionary subjects. · Given to the National Gallery of Art by Lessing J. Rosenwald in 1943."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-bard.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Bard","John Martin","martin-bard","John Martin · c. 1817 · Oil on canvas, 127 × 101.6 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · From a crag high above a river, a white-bearded harper curses the English army winding below. The scene comes from Thomas Gray's ode of 1757 about the last Welsh bard, who then leaps to his death. · Martin painted another, much larger version, over two meters tall, now in the Laing Art Gallery in Newcastle upon Tyne. · Look at the tiny soldiers at lower left, dwarfed by the cliffs, torrent and peaks that climb out of the top of the frame. · The castle across the river has been identified as Conwy, in North Wales. · A lone poet stands against an army in a sublime mountain setting. The artist as prophet, defying worldly power, became a favorite Romantic hero. · Sublime · The awe mixed with fear felt before something vast or overwhelming, such as mountains, storms or great height."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-harvest-moon.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Harvest Moon","Samuel Palmer","palmer-harvest-moon","Samuel Palmer · c. 1833 · Oil on paper laid on panel, 22.2 × 27.6 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Farm workers gather and bundle sheaves of grain late into the night under a full moon and a scatter of stars. Palmer made this small picture near the end of his years in the Kent village of Shoreham. · Harvesting by night was common practice at the time, the museum notes, so the scene itself is not invented. · Look at the workers: they show no strain, and seem to move in step, almost dancing. · The oil is laid on in small, thick touches, and the moon, high among the trees at upper left, washes the field in glowing orange-gold. · The countryside becomes a vision of plenty. For Palmer the natural world was a sign of God's handiwork, so an ordinary English farm gains an almost sacred calm."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-burning-of-the-houses-of-lords-and-commons-16-october-1834.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Burning of the Houses of Lords and Commons, 16 October 1834","Joseph Mallord William Turner","turner-burning-lords-commons","Joseph Mallord William Turner · 1835 · Oil on canvas, 92 × 123.2 cm. Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland · Fire tears through the old Parliament buildings at Westminster, lighting up the Thames and the bridge beyond. Turner painted it the year after the blaze, raising the flames above their real height. · This version looks at the fire from downriver; the other, looking across Westminster Bridge, is in the Philadelphia Museum of Art. · Look at the small craft pushing toward the blaze at lower right; the museum adds that low water at first held up the steam vessels hauling fire-fighting gear. · Loose strokes of color, glinting reflections and drifting smoke leave the buildings and bridge only half defined. · A real disaster becomes public spectacle. The dark mass of onlookers packed along the near bank is part of the subject, so the picture is as much about watching as about the fire. · Bought from Turner by a Leeds collector; bequeathed to Cleveland by John L. Severance."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/goya-witness.html","Goya, Witness · Overview","Goya, Witness","","goya-third-of-may","Francisco de Goya, 1799–1823 · Court painter to the Spanish crown, Goya lived through Napoleon's invasion of Spain and the brutal war that followed. His work moved from royal portraits to prints and private murals that face cruelty, fear and illness head on. · A state portrait at the court of Charles IV · Etched series on folly, war and its civilian cost · Late works made for himself and a friend · Francisco de Goya · The Third of May 1808 · 1814"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-family-of-charles-iv.html","Goya, Witness","The Family of Charles IV","Francisco de Goya","goya-family-of-charles-iv","Francisco de Goya · 1800 · Oil on canvas, 280 × 336 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · The Spanish royal family lines up across the canvas in fashionable dress, jewels and royal orders. Painted soon after his promotion to First Chamber Painter, it shows how close he now stood to the throne. · Painted at Aranjuez and Madrid over one spring and summer, and probably hanging in the Royal Palace the same year. · Look at the centre: the king and queen stand with their two youngest children. · The Prado names two models: Velázquez's Las Meninas and Van Loo's group portrait of Philip V's family. · The Prado praises how clearly each sitter's character comes through. Even in an official state portrait, the royals appear as distinct individuals rather than idealized types. · Palace inventories still record it there in 1814; ten years later it belonged to the Prado."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-sleep-of-reason-produces-monsters-el-sueno-de-la-razon-produce-monstruos.html","Goya, Witness","The Sleep of Reason Produces Monsters (El sueño de la razón produce monstruos)","Francisco de Goya","goya-sleep-of-reason","Francisco de Goya · 1799 · Etching and aquatint; plate 21.2 × 15.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A sleeping artist slumps over his desk while owls and bats crowd in behind him. It became the best-known plate of the Caprichos, an 80-print series of social satire sold as a set. · Before becoming plate 43, the design was probably meant to open the series, judging from a preparatory drawing now in the Prado. · Look at the lynx at lower right: it stays wide awake while the sleeper is swarmed. · Aquatint lays the grainy dark tone behind the swarm of creatures. · Prints let the artist put his own satirical commentary before a wide public. Frederick Ilchman (Museum of Fine Arts, Boston talk, 2014) notes that sueño means both sleep and dream, so the title reads two ways. · Given to the Met by the dealers M. Knoedler & Co. in 1918. · Aquatint · An etching method that uses a dusting of resin on the plate to print areas of tone rather than lines."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-second-of-may-1808.html","Goya, Witness","The Second of May 1808","Francisco de Goya","goya-second-of-may","Francisco de Goya · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 268.5 × 347.5 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · Madrid townspeople attack French cavalry with knives in a crowded street fight. Painted at the war's end, it looks back to the uprising that opened Spain's war against Napoleon's occupation. · Commissioned through the Regency Council; frame invoices billed to the King's Bedchamber suggest the king paid to frame it for the Royal Palace. · Look at the turbaned Mamelukes of Napoleon's Imperial Guard being dragged from their horses. · A truck carrying it crashed in 1938. Work in 2007–08 made the colors bright again and hid the losses, guided by old photographs. · Bodies and blades tangle around the white horse at centre, and nobody in particular leads the fight. Janis Tomlinson (UC Berkeley talk, 2011) reads the Spanish attackers as committing atrocities too, so neither side looks heroic. · Moved from the Royal Palace to the Prado, whose catalogue lists it from 1872."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-third-of-may-1808.html","Goya, Witness","The Third of May 1808","Francisco de Goya","goya-third-of-may","Francisco de Goya · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 268 × 347 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · At night a French firing squad shoots Madrid civilians seized after the uprising. Tomlinson (Berkeley) observes that this pair of canvases brings the brutality of the war etchings to the full size of a history picture. · Painted as the companion to the street-fight canvas; the Prado rejects the old story that both were made for street display. · Look at the man in white with raised arms, lit by a lantern and facing soldiers whose faces are hidden. · Broad brushwork and stark light and dark replace careful finish. · The scene honors anonymous townspeople as martyrs. That dignity, granted to people history would not otherwise name, made it a lasting model for later images of political violence. · Registered in the Prado's Large Deposit in 1834; the French writer Théophile Gautier saw it there in 1840."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/and-there-is-nothing-to-be-done-y-no-hai-remedio.html","Goya, Witness","And There Is Nothing to Be Done (Y no hai remedio)","Francisco de Goya","goya-y-no-hai-remedio","Francisco de Goya · 1810, published 1863 · Etching, drypoint, burin and burnisher; plate 14 × 16.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Two bodies lie in front, a firing squad shoots other prisoners behind, and a blindfolded man bound to a post waits his turn. The Met reads it as several moments of one execution in a single scene. · Plate 15 of the Disasters of War; the Real Academia de San Fernando published the first edition long after his death. · Look at the three rifle barrels poking in from beyond the right edge, held by shooters we never see. · The Met notes it was etched on the back of a plate already used for a landscape print. · Killing appears as cold routine, seen low and close among the dead and the next to die: war from the victims' side. Ilchman (MFA Boston) contrasts Jacques Callot's 1633 war prints, which keep such brutality at a distance. · Given to the Met by Mrs. Grafton H. Pyne in 1951."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/i-saw-it-yo-lo-vi.html","Goya, Witness","I Saw It (Yo lo vi)","Francisco de Goya","goya-yo-lo-vi","Francisco de Goya · 1810, published 1863 · Etching, drypoint and burin; plate 15.8 × 23.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Townspeople stream away from a hilltop town while a woman hauls a small child along by the arm. Whatever they are fleeing lies outside the frame. · Tomlinson (Berkeley) notes that the 1863 edition followed the order of the Ceán Bermúdez album of 85 proofs, now in the British Museum. · Look at the two men at left racing ahead, one clutching a bundle, while women and children follow behind. · Fine lines alone build the figures; the pale sky and hillside are left almost bare. · The caption claims eyewitness authority. Tomlinson (Yale University Art Gallery talk, 2016) thinks the artist probably imagined most scenes, though he did see the Madrid famine of 1811–12. · Entered the Met in the same 1951 gift as plate 15."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/saturn-devouring-his-son.html","Goya, Witness","Saturn Devouring His Son","Francisco de Goya","goya-saturn","Francisco de Goya · 1820–1823 · Mixed technique, mural transferred to canvas, 143.5 × 81.4 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · A wild-eyed giant tears at a headless body. The late murals it belongs to shaped the modern image of the artist and, says the Prado, fed into Expressionism, Surrealism and film. · Placed on the ground floor of the Quinta del Sordo, facing an image of Leocadia Zorrilla. · Look at the staring eyes and the darkness that swallows everything except the two bodies. · Chemical analysis found oil in the paint, which is why the Prado calls the technique mixed. · Made for the house he lived in rather than for a client or a public showing, it is art kept for the artist alone. The Prado suggests the god may stand for the fear of losing power. · Baron Émile d'Erlanger bought the house in 1873, had the murals moved to canvas at a heavy loss of paint, and gave them to the Spanish state. · Black Paintings · The later name for the dark murals painted directly on the walls of the Quinta del Sordo in Madrid, near the end of his life in Spain."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/self-portrait-with-dr-arrieta.html","Goya, Witness","Self-Portrait with Dr. Arrieta","Francisco de Goya","goya-self-portrait-arrieta","Francisco de Goya · 1820 · Oil on canvas, 114.6 × 76.5 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art · A doctor props up a gravely ill patient and offers him a glass of medicine. This is the artist's last self-portrait, a gift of thanks to the physician who saved his life. · The doctor was Eugenio García Arrieta, who treated him through an acute, dangerous illness late in 1819. · Look at the dim faces behind the bed; the Minneapolis Institute of Art (Mia) suggests they may be hallucinations. · An inscription along the lower edge calls the doctor a friend and gives the painter's age as 73. · Mia notes that pairing picture and inscription recalls the ex-voto, a devotional offering made in Spain to give thanks after surviving a calamity. Here the painter turns his own brush with death into the subject. · Bought by Mia through the Ethel Morrison Van Derlip Fund."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/painting-the-headlines.html","Painting the Headlines · Overview","Painting the Headlines","","gericault-raft-medusa","Gros, Géricault, Delacroix and Rude, 1804–1836 · This part follows French art from the official painting of Napoleon's reign to the generation that broke with it. It closes with a sculpture carved for a public monument, the Arc de Triomphe. · Gros and a state commission from Napoleon's campaigns · Géricault's soldiers, horses, castaways and a portrait of madness · Delacroix and the revolution of July 1830 · Théodore Géricault · The Raft of the Medusa · 1818–19"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/bonaparte-visiting-the-plague-stricken-in-jaffa.html","Painting the Headlines","Bonaparte Visiting the Plague-Stricken in Jaffa","Antoine-Jean Gros","gros-plague-jaffa","Antoine-Jean Gros · 1804 · Oil on canvas, 523 × 715 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A mosque courtyard serves as a military hospital for the Army of the Orient, and its commander walks among the dying. The picture links the grand style of David's school to the new regime of Napoleon. · Shown at the Salon of 1804. Bonaparte himself prompted the commission, which went through the interior ministry. · Look at his bare hand touching a sore on a patient's chest while an officer covers his nose. · Painted in a studio in the Jeu de Paume at Versailles and signed on a step at the left. · A living commander appears as a near-sacred healer, calm and upright among the dying, while a blind soldier at the right gropes his way toward him. · Paid for by the state in 1805, it sat in storage by 1821, reportedly deteriorating, then hung at the Luxembourg from 1830. · History painting · Large pictures of heroic, religious or historical scenes, ranked highest by the academies. · Salon · The official, juried art exhibition in Paris, where an artist's reputation was made or lost."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-charging-chasseur.html","Painting the Headlines","The Charging Chasseur","Théodore Géricault","gericault-charging-chasseur","Théodore Géricault · 1812 · Oil on canvas, 349 × 266 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A mounted officer of Napoleon's Imperial Guard rides into battle smoke with his sabre drawn. At its first showing, the catalog identified the sitter only by the initials M. D. · Shown at the Salon of 1812–13, then renamed A Hussar Charging when it returned to the Salon in 1814. · Look at the rider glancing back over his shoulder while the horse lunges the other way. · The smoky sky and ground are brushed in loosely, leaving the detail to the uniform and harness. · Energy wins over order: horse and rider are built on a steep, twisting diagonal, caught mid-turn, instead of the steady, balanced pose of earlier equestrian portraits. · Kept by the artist until his death, then owned by the duc d'Orléans, later King Louis-Philippe."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/wounded-cuirassier-leaving-the-field.html","Painting the Headlines","Wounded Cuirassier Leaving the Field","Théodore Géricault","gericault-wounded-cuirassier","Théodore Géricault · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 358 × 294 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A heavy cavalryman leads his horse down a slope on foot, his eyes turned up toward the sky. The canvas is even larger than the charging officer's, yet it shows no combat. · Shown at the Salon of 1814 under the same title it carries today. · Look at the sheathed sabre he leans on like a crutch and the rearing horse he struggles to hold. · The Louvre also holds a related oil sketch for the composition. · Defeat replaces victory: a wounded soldier turns his back on the battle, smoke still rising behind him. Shown in the year of Napoleon's first abdication, it puts doubt and loss where glory used to be. · Kept as a pair with the Chasseur, it entered the Louvre in 1851; the two cost 23,400 francs together at Louis-Philippe's estate sale."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-raft-of-the-medusa.html","Painting the Headlines","The Raft of the Medusa","Théodore Géricault","gericault-raft-medusa","Théodore Géricault · 1818–19 · Oil on canvas, 491 × 716 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Survivors of a French navy shipwreck crowd a makeshift raft and strain toward a sail on the horizon. Of about 150 people put on the raft in 1816, fifteen were alive when rescued. · First shown at the Salon of 1819 under the neutral title Scène de naufrage (Shipwreck Scene). · Look at the bodies: they rise in a pyramid from the dead at the front to a man waving a cloth. · Begun in a rented Paris studio in 1818 and finished in a theater foyer in July 1819. · No king, saint or general appears: a recent disaster and its anonymous victims are given the size of history painting, the most prestigious kind of art. · Shown to paying crowds in London and Dublin in 1820–21, then bought for the Crown at his estate sale in 1824."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-monomaniac-of-envy.html","Painting the Headlines","The Monomaniac of Envy","Théodore Géricault","gericault-monomaniac-envy","Théodore Géricault · c. 1819–22 · Oil on canvas, 72 × 58 cm. Musée des Beaux-Arts, Lyon · An elderly woman in a white cap stares sideways with red-rimmed eyes and a tight mouth. Her name and story are unknown; only the painter's close attention survives. · One of five surviving portraits of mentally ill sitters; the museum treats the story of a doctor's commission as unproven. · Look at the eyes, which avoid ours and fix on something outside the frame. · Painted close up against a plain dark ground, with no setting or props to explain her. · Mental illness becomes a subject for portraiture. The sitter is studied as an individual, not a curiosity, in the years when doctors in Paris were beginning to classify madness. · The Lyon museum bought it in 1908 at the sale of the collector Auguste Chéramy."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-derby-at-epsom.html","Painting the Headlines","The Derby at Epsom","Théodore Géricault","gericault-derby-epsom","Théodore Géricault · 1821 · Oil on canvas, 92 × 123 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Four jockeys race across open turf under a low, heavy sky. Horses were a lifelong subject for the painter, and this small canvas dates from the period of his stay in England. · Known as the Derby of 1821, though the museum says that name is only traditional. · Look at the legs, flung out front and back at once, with every hoof off the ground. · The horses' near-identical strides repeat across the canvas like a frieze, above a plain, low landscape. · Speed becomes a feeling. The flying gallop is physically impossible, as photography later showed, yet it conveys rush and excitement better than an accurate pose would. · Bought for the Louvre in 1866; the museum does not record the seller."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-barque-of-dante.html","Painting the Headlines","The Barque of Dante","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-barque-dante","Eugène Delacroix · 1822 · Oil on canvas, 189 × 241.5 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Two poets cross a river in hell in a small boat while the damned claw at its sides. The National Gallery's Christopher Riopelle says this first Salon picture made its young painter famous almost overnight. · Shown at the Salon of 1822 and bought from it by the royal museums for 2,000 francs. · Look at Dante in his red hood, recoiling, while a calm Virgil steadies him by the hand. · The crowded, tilting boat on dark water owes a clear debt to Géricault's raft, shown three years before. · Literature becomes a source: a scene from Dante's Inferno is painted with Rubens-like color and heavy, twisting muscle instead of the cool, drawn line taught in David's school. · Displayed at the Luxembourg for as long as Delacroix lived; transferred to the Louvre in 1874."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/scenes-from-the-massacres-at-chios.html","Painting the Headlines","Scenes from the Massacres at Chios","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-massacres-chios","Eugène Delacroix · 1824 · Oil on canvas, 419 × 354 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Greek families sit stunned and dying before an Ottoman horseman after the 1822 killings on the island of Chios. The event was only about two years old when the canvas went on view. · Shown at the Salon of 1824, where the booklet sent visitors to newspaper reports of the massacre. · Look at the baby reaching for its dead mother at the lower right. · Painted between January and July 1824, just in time for the Salon jury in August. · The canvas is often read as a plea for French support of the Greek war of independence. There is no single focus or climax; suffering spreads across the whole foreground. · Bought from the artist by the King's Household in 1824 for 6,000 francs; at the Louvre since 1874."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/liberty-leading-the-people.html","Painting the Headlines","Liberty Leading the People","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-liberty-leading","Eugène Delacroix · 1830 · Oil on canvas, 260 × 325 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A bare-breasted woman with the tricolor flag leads armed Parisians over a barricade and the bodies of the fallen. It records the three days of street fighting in July 1830 that toppled King Charles X. · Shown at the Salon of 1831; its other title was simply A Barricade. · Look at the boy with two pistols at her side and the man in a top hat holding a gun. · Photographed in May 2024, just after a cleaning and retouching of the paint surface. · An allegory walks beside real people: Liberty is a symbol, yet the men around her wear the everyday clothes of Parisians from different classes. · The state bought it in 1831, soon stored it and later entrusted it to the artist; it reached the Louvre in 1874. · Allegory · A figure or story that stands for an abstract idea."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-departure-of-the-volunteers-of-1792-la-marseillaise.html","Painting the Headlines","The Departure of the Volunteers of 1792 (La Marseillaise)","François Rude","rude-departure-volunteers","François Rude · 1833–36 · Stone, high relief. Arc de Triomphe de l'Étoile, Paris · A winged figure shouts and brandishes a sword above armed citizens rushing to defend France against foreign armies. It is one of four colossal groups carved for the piers of the Arc de Triomphe. · Commissioned under Adolphe Thiers as a political choice of the July Monarchy; the arch was inaugurated in 1836. · Look below her: a bearded veteran raises his arm in a rallying gesture and hugs a naked young recruit to his side, with an elder at the back offering counsel. · Ancient-style armor and nudity replace 1792 uniforms, so that the struggle reads as universal, not local. · Public sculpture gains a Romantic voice: violent motion and raw emotion take the place of the calm, balanced poses expected of official monuments. · It remains on the arch, which the Centre des monuments nationaux now manages. · Relief · Sculpture that projects from a flat background instead of standing free."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/passion-and-the-exotic.html","Passion and the Exotic · Overview","Passion and the Exotic","","delacroix-death-sardanapalus","Ingres, Delacroix, Chassériau and Barye, 1814–1861 · French painting in these decades split between Delacroix's color and loose handling and Ingres's polished line. Many of the subjects come from North Africa and the Middle East, places artists knew through travel, books or imagination. · Delacroix's 1832 journey to Morocco and what he made of it at home · Bible stories retold through Eastern dress or a storm at sea · Storms, lion hunts and animal combat in paint and bronze · Eugène Delacroix · The Death of Sardanapalus · 1827"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/la-grande-odalisque.html","Passion and the Exotic","La Grande Odalisque","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","ingres-grande-odalisque","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 91 × 162 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A nude harem woman lies on cushions and turns to look back over her shoulder. Its cool, exact surface makes it the calm opening to a part full of violent scenes. · Made in Rome for Caroline Murat, Queen of Naples, and signed with the place name Rom; Paris saw it at the Salon of 1819. · Look at the back and the right arm: both run longer than a real body would allow. · The brushstrokes vanish under a smooth, enamel-like finish, the kind of surface the academies prized. · The turban, peacock-feather fan and pipe dress the scene as a harem, but the harem is an invented one; Ingres never traveled to the East. · The Louvre bought it in 1899."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-death-of-sardanapalus.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Death of Sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-death-sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix · 1827 · Oil on canvas, 392 × 496 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A defeated Assyrian king watches from his bed as his servants kill his women and horses before his own death. Inspired by a play by Lord Byron, the picture caused a scandal. · A late addition to the Salon of 1827–28, it went on view in February 1828. · Look at the huge red bed, which slides diagonally down toward the viewer. · Delacroix worked on it in the second half of 1827, on canvas from the Haro firm; the Louvre restored it in 2022–23. · Bodies, jewels and animals tumble in every direction, with nothing to anchor the center. Christopher Riopelle of the National Gallery, London, in a 2016 lecture, describes the disorder as running through both the subject and the composition. · Unsold until 1846, it passed through several collections before the Louvre bought it in 1921."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/women-of-algiers-in-their-apartment.html","Passion and the Exotic","Women of Algiers in Their Apartment","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-women-algiers","Eugène Delacroix · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 180 × 229 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Three women rest on the floor of a dim, tiled room while a servant walks out at the right. Glowing reds, greens and golds against shadowed walls hold the eye more than any story. · First shown at the Salon of 1834. · Look at the water pipe on the floor and the slippers kicked off beside it, signs of a slow, private afternoon. · A watercolor study of Arab women from his travel album may have been used for the figures. · His costumes and objects rest on what he saw, but the closed, timeless harem is a European idea of the East. That blend of observation and fantasy runs through much Orientalist art. · The French State bought it at that Salon. · Orientalism · Western art and writing that pictured North Africa and the Middle East, often as exotic, sensual or violent."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-convulsionists-of-tangier.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Convulsionists of Tangier","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-convulsionists-tangier","Eugène Delacroix · 1837–38 · Oil on canvas, 95.57 × 128.59 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art, Minneapolis · Members of the Aissawa, a Sufi brotherhood, surge through a street in Tangier in a religious trance while townspeople look on. Here North Africa appears as a crowd in motion, not a quiet interior. · During the 1832 mission, he and the Comte de Mornay watched the procession pass from a boarded-up window under the roof. · Look at the raised arms and bowed heads at the center, and the onlookers crowding the rooftops above. · Painted in France about five years after the event. · Riopelle (National Gallery) notes that in Morocco Delacroix made only drawings and watercolors; the paintings came later, from memory, and here he heightened the color of the scene he had glimpsed. · Long known as The Fanatics of Tangier, it was once owned by James J. Hill and came to Mia as a bequest of J. Jerome Hill."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-toilette-of-esther.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Toilette of Esther","Théodore Chassériau","chasseriau-toilette-esther","Théodore Chassériau · 1841 · Oil on canvas, 45.5 × 35.5 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Two attendants help the Biblical heroine Esther prepare to be presented to the Persian king Ahasuerus. This small cabinet picture was painted when Chassériau was still in his early twenties. · Shown at the Salon of 1842. · Look at the pale, cleanly outlined body set between the deep blue robe at left, the red-orange dress at right, and the sky and trees behind. · A pencil study for the painting is also in the Louvre. · Chassériau, a pupil of Ingres, keeps his teacher's firm contour on the body but gives the robes and jewels the strong color linked with Delacroix. The two rival camps meet in one Eastern beauty scene. · Baron Arthur Chassériau bequeathed it to the Louvre in 1934."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/christ-on-the-sea-of-galilee.html","Passion and the Exotic","Christ on the Sea of Galilee","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-christ-galilee","Eugène Delacroix · 1854 · Oil on canvas, 59.8 × 73.3 cm. Walters Art Museum, Baltimore · Wind and waves toss a small boat while frightened disciples fight the sail and their teacher sleeps, a story told in the Gospels. Religious subjects took up much of Delacroix's late career. · It belongs to a group of versions from 1853–54, a time when French Catholic taste favored this episode. · Look at the haloed figure asleep at the left, the only still point in a boat heeled over by the swell. · Signed at the lower right with his name and the short date 54. · The critic Roger Kimball, in a 1998 lecture at the New York Studio School, links the stormy sky in this series to the disciples' crisis of faith. Sea and clouds set the emotional pitch. · William T. Walters bought it through the agent George A. Lucas in 1886; it reached the museum with his son Henry's bequest in 1931."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-lion-hunt.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Lion Hunt","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-lion-hunt","Eugène Delacroix · 1860–61 · Oil on canvas, 76.5 × 98.5 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · Hunters on horseback and on foot attack two lions in hilly, grassy country under a heavy, cloudy sky. It is one of several hunt scenes Delacroix returned to in his last years. · First exhibited after his death, at the 1885 Delacroix exhibition at the École des Beaux-Arts, Paris. · Look at the lion leaping onto a fallen horse at the left and the second lion roaring over the men beneath it. · The scene is invented: having never watched such a hunt, he assembled it from earlier studies of terrain, Islamic dress and lions at the zoo. · Strokes stay loose and visible even in manes and faces, so men, horses and lions blur into one tangled mass. The quick handling adds to the violence. · Bought by Mr. and Mrs. Potter Palmer in 1892 and bequeathed to the museum in 1922."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/lion-and-serpent-no-1.html","Passion and the Exotic","Lion and Serpent no. 1","Antoine-Louis Barye","barye-lion-serpent","Antoine-Louis Barye · model 1838, cast possibly about 1850 · Bronze (sand cast), 22.9 × 34.3 × 17.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A crouching lion pins a snake beneath one forepaw and lowers its head toward it as the snake rears to strike. This tabletop bronze is a reduced version of Barye's large group for the Tuileries Garden in Paris. · The large version stood in the Tuileries from 1835, after a purchase by the French government. · Look at the lion's wrinkled snout, open jaws and bristling mane as it faces the snake. · Issued in an edition by the publisher Émile Martin; the base is signed BARYE. · Animal combat, long a minor subject, here gets the care once kept for heroes and gods. A struggle in nature becomes worthy of serious sculpture. · The museum bought it in 1910 with money from its Rogers Fund."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/britain-weather-and-light.html","Britain: Weather and Light · Overview","Britain: Weather and Light","","constable-hay-wain","Turner, Constable, Barry and Pugin, 1812–c. 1870 · In these decades British painters made sky, storm and changing daylight serious subjects for large exhibition pictures. Constable worked from close study of places he knew, while Turner pushed toward catastrophe, night and the machines of his own time. · Constable's Suffolk, Essex, Hampstead and Salisbury, sketched outdoors and worked up in the studio · Turner's Royal Academy pictures of war, the sea and the railway · A new Parliament built in a medieval style · John Constable · The Hay Wain · 1821"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/snow-storm-hannibal-and-his-army-crossing-the-alps.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Snow Storm: Hannibal and his Army Crossing the Alps","J.M.W. Turner","turner-hannibal","J.M.W. Turner · exhibited 1812 · Oil on canvas, 146 × 237.5 cm. Tate Britain, London · A dark storm cloud curls over the Carthaginian army as it struggles through a mountain pass. The soldiers are tiny, and the weather, not the general, fills most of the canvas. · At the Royal Academy the painter insisted it hang low on the wall and threatened to withdraw it otherwise. · Look at the small orange sun, caught inside the dark arc of cloud. · The catalogue carried verses credited to his own poem Fallacies of Hope, the first time he did this; the poem was probably never finished. · Here the sublime comes from weather, a force no general can command or escape. Critics at the exhibition already praised the picture in exactly those terms. · Part of the Turner Bequest, the paintings he left to the nation, accepted in 1856; at Tate since 1910."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/wivenhoe-park-essex.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Wivenhoe Park, Essex","John Constable","constable-wivenhoe-park","John Constable · 1816 · Oil on canvas, 56.1 × 101.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A red-brick house sits beyond a wide lake under a sky of moving cloud, with swans on the water. It is a portrait of a real country estate. · Made for the owner, Major-General Francis Slater Rebow, his first important patron; shown at the Royal Academy in 1817. · Look at the cow at far left and the boat at right, where two men are setting a net. · He stayed on the estate to paint it. To take in more of the park for the family, he added strips at each side and masked the joins with that cow and boat. · The gallery's curator Franklin Kelly notes that he shifted trees and strengthened shadows and reflections. The gallery also stresses the play of light across it. · Kept by the Rebow family, bought by Peter A. B. Widener in 1906, and given to the gallery in 1942."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-hay-wain.html","Britain: Weather and Light","The Hay Wain","John Constable","constable-hay-wain","John Constable · 1821 · Oil on canvas, 130.2 × 185.4 cm. The National Gallery, London · A horse-drawn cart fords a shallow millpond on the river Stour on a summer day. The familiar name came later from his friend Archdeacon John Fisher. · Shown at the Royal Academy as Landscape: Noon; it was well reviewed but did not sell. · Look at the cottage at left, home of the farmer Willy Lott, at Flatford in Suffolk. · Painted in his London studio from earlier studies. In a 2017 gallery talk, the National Gallery's Colin Wiggins points out that the fields are yellower and bluer greens laid over a mid-green layer that shows through. · Ordinary farm work fills a canvas about six feet wide, a size normally kept for history painting. Scale claims serious attention for the native countryside. · The dealer John Arrowsmith took it to the 1824 Paris Salon, where Charles X awarded the painter a gold medal; Henry Vaughan gave it in 1886."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/cloud-study.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Cloud Study","John Constable","constable-cloud-study","John Constable · ca. 1821 · Oil on paper laid on card, 20.3 × 27.3 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Grey storm clouds pile up over a low strip of land, painted at speed on a small sheet of paper. Earlier catalogues gave it longer titles that also named the landscape and its trees. · Made on Hampstead Heath, outside London, as private study and never meant as a finished work. · Look at the trees, a dark silhouette at lower left, leaving most of the sheet to the sky. · He called this practice skying; notes on the back record the time, the direction he faced, the wind and the weather. · Studies like this taught him how skies actually form and move, from life rather than from older pictures. Wiggins (National Gallery) notes that weather experts can name the cloud types in his paintings. · Came to Yale with Paul Mellon's collection of British art. · Oil sketch · A quick painting in oil made to study a subject or plan a larger work, not to be sold or exhibited."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/salisbury-cathedral-from-the-bishops-grounds.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Salisbury Cathedral from the Bishop's Grounds","John Constable","constable-salisbury-cathedral","John Constable · ca. 1825 · Oil on canvas, 87.9 × 111.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · This is the Met's full-scale study, the same size as the finished picture of 1826 now in the Frick Collection, New York. The design exists in several versions; this one shows the painter working it out. · The finished picture was made for John Fisher, Bishop of Salisbury, uncle of the painter's close friend Archdeacon John Fisher; the commission goes back to 1822. · Look at the small figure of the bishop at lower left, gesturing toward the spire. · As he developed the design, he opened up the canopy of trees and brightened the sky around the spire. · Tall trees arch over the scene and frame the cathedral like a gateway, so nature sets the stage for the medieval building rather than serving as background. · Bequeathed to the Met by Mary Stillman Harkness in 1950."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/hadleigh-castle-the-mouth-of-the-thames-morning-after-a-stormy-night.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Hadleigh Castle, The Mouth of the Thames—Morning after a Stormy Night","John Constable","constable-hadleigh-castle","John Constable · 1829 · Oil on canvas, 121.9 × 164.5 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Two shattered towers of a medieval castle stand on a hill above a wide estuary, under clouds still heavy with rain. Light breaks over the distant water, but the foreground stays dark and broken. · Shown at the Royal Academy, his first show there after election as a full Academician that February. · Look at the figure and dog at lower left, small beside the crumbling stone, and a second figure with cows on the slope beyond. · One of his six-footers; he worked out the design first in a full-size study, now at Tate Britain. · Painted in the months after his wife Maria died in November 1828, and its bleak mood is often linked to that grief. A real place becomes a picture of feeling. · Sold from his studio after his death; Paul Mellon bought it in 1961 and gave it to Yale in 1977."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/keelmen-heaving-in-coals-by-moonlight.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Keelmen Heaving in Coals by Moonlight","J.M.W. Turner","turner-keelmen","J.M.W. Turner · 1835 · Oil on canvas, 92.3 × 122.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · At night, workers in small boats load coal into tall sailing ships crowded along a quay. Smoke from the work drifts across the moonlit river. · Painted for Henry McConnel of Manchester and shown at the Royal Academy and the Royal Manchester Institution. · Look at the full moon near the center and the orange fires at right; his initials are on the buoy at lower left. · Keelmen were the boatmen who carried coal out to larger ships in shallow barges called keels. · Hard labor and nature share one river at night, each with its own light, and both receive the same care. · Bought by Peter A. B. Widener in 1910 and given to the gallery with the Widener Collection in 1942."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-fighting-temeraire.html","Britain: Weather and Light","The Fighting Temeraire","J.M.W. Turner","turner-fighting-temeraire","J.M.W. Turner · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 90.7 × 121.6 cm. The National Gallery, London · A pale warship that fought at Trafalgar in 1805 is towed by a small dark steam tug to a shipbreaker's yard. The ship had been sold for scrap in 1838. · He most likely never saw the tow and worked from reports; the picture went to the Royal Academy the next year. · Look at the old ship, the palest shape in the picture, behind the dark tug, with the sunset blazing at right. · In a 2016 gallery talk, the National Gallery's Matthew Morgan points out that the water holds almost no blue and that the sunset paint is thick and heavily worked. · Critics then read it as a lament for the age of sail. Morgan (National Gallery) notes the painter had drawn steamboats with interest since 1815 and treats the meaning as open. · He kept it until his death and called it his darling."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/slave-ship-slavers-throwing-overboard-the-dead-and-dying-typhoon-coming-on.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Slave Ship (Slavers Throwing Overboard the Dead and Dying, Typhoon Coming On)","J.M.W. Turner","turner-slave-ship","J.M.W. Turner · 1840 · Oil on canvas, 90.8 × 122.6 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A sailing ship heads into a typhoon while chained bodies sink and struggle in the foreground. The cruelty of the slave trade is shown with no hero and no rescue. · Shown at the Royal Academy with catalogue lines from his own Fallacies of Hope describing the scene. · Look at the lower right: a shackled leg rises from the water among fish and gulls. · The ship itself is small and set back in the middle distance, not the focus of the scene. · Outrage comes through color and weather rather than story detail: blood-red light and a heaving sea make the crime felt rather than described. · John James Ruskin bought it in 1843 as a gift for his son, the critic John Ruskin; the museum bought it in 1899."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/rain-steam-and-speed-the-great-western-railway.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Rain, Steam and Speed – The Great Western Railway","J.M.W. Turner","turner-rain-steam-speed","J.M.W. Turner · 1844 · Oil on canvas, 91 × 121.8 cm. The National Gallery, London · A train rushes toward the viewer across a bridge over the Thames, half dissolved in rain and mist. It is an early and famous image of the railway age. · Shown at the Royal Academy; the painter George Leslie later reported watching him finish it. · Look at the dark diagonal bridge the train is on, not the arched bridge at left: it is Brunel's crossing between Taplow and Maidenhead, in use from 1839. The engine's dark front is a Firefly-class locomotive. · A hare runs ahead of the train, added late in thin paint that has turned nearly transparent; Robert Brandard's 1859 engraving still shows it. · Speed itself is the subject: the engine seems to tear through rain and space. The new railway draws the kind of awe once kept for mountains and storms."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/palace-of-westminster-houses-of-parliament.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Palace of Westminster (Houses of Parliament)","Charles Barry and A.W.N. Pugin","barry-pugin-palace-of-westminster","Charles Barry and A.W.N. Pugin · designed 1836; built 1840–c. 1870 · Architecture, Perpendicular Gothic style. Westminster, London · After the fire of 1834, Parliament was rebuilt as a vast complex on the Thames, keeping the surviving medieval Westminster Hall. Its towers and river front became a symbol of Britain itself. · A commission of 1835 required a Gothic or Elizabethan design; Barry's entry won the 1836 competition from 97 entries. · Look at the river front: rows of narrow vertical panels and pinnacles in late medieval style, with the clock tower at one end. · Barry was the architect; Pugin designed the Gothic detailing and interiors, down to furniture and small metalwork. · The Gothic requirement tied the nation's government to its medieval past rather than to the Greek and Roman models usual for public buildings. Romantic interest in history became a national style. · The Lords first sat in their new chamber in 1847 and the Commons in 1852; E. M. Barry finished the work about 1870. · Gothic Revival · The reuse of medieval Gothic forms, such as pointed arches and pinnacles, in buildings of the 1700s and 1800s."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/germany-and-the-north-nature-as-scripture.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture · Overview","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","","friedrich-monk-by-the-sea","Runge, Friedrich, Kersting and Dahl, 1805–1839 · In Hamburg and Dresden, a handful of painters used portraits and landscapes to think about God, death and the self. Their pictures usually hold few figures, but they were carefully built from studies and loaded with meaning. · Runge's portraits of children and his private symbolic system · Friedrich's lone figures, ruins, ice and moonlight · The circle around him: Kersting's studio portrait and Dahl's Dresden at night · Caspar David Friedrich · The Monk by the Sea · 1808–10"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-hulsenbeck-children.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","The Hülsenbeck Children","Philipp Otto Runge","runge-hulsenbeck-children","Philipp Otto Runge · 1805–06 · Oil on canvas, 131.5 × 143.5 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Three small children play outdoors on the edge of Hamburg. The Kunsthalle sees the picture as a turning point for the way German painters portrayed the young. · The sitters are three of the five children of Friedrich August Hülsenbeck, a business partner of the painter's brother Daniel. · Look at the horizon: church towers mark Hamburg, seen from the family's house in Eimsbüttel, then outside the city. · The viewpoint is set low, at a child's eye level, so adults look across at the sitters rather than down. · The children stand for stages of awareness, from a baby grasping at things to an older sister keeping watch. Childhood is treated as a state with its own worth, not as adulthood in miniature. · Given to the Kunsthalle in 1862 by Richard Hülsenbeck."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/morning-first-version-small-morning.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Morning (first version, \"Small Morning\")","Philipp Otto Runge","runge-morning","Philipp Otto Runge · 1808 · Oil on canvas, 109 × 85.5 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Aurora, goddess of dawn, floats above a wide landscape inside an ornamental painted border. Of a planned four-part cycle on the times of day, it is the only part ever carried out in oil. · The cycle began as the painter's own print series, The Times of Day, issued in editions of 1805 and 1807. · Look at the frame: the arabesque border is painted as part of the picture and carries its own imagery. · This is the smaller first version; a larger second version of 1808–09, also in Hamburg, survives only in fragments. · The center carries a cosmic, nature-based meaning and the border a Christian one about the soul rising to God. The painter built this personal language of symbols to renew Christian art. · Given to the Kunsthalle in 1891 by Paul Runge."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-monk-by-the-sea.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","The Monk by the Sea","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-monk-by-the-sea","Caspar David Friedrich · 1808–10 · Oil on canvas, 110 × 171.5 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · One small figure stands on a pale strip of dune under an enormous sky. The Met's Alison Hokanson and Joanna Sheers Seidenstein (curator talk, 2025) note that it shocked viewers in 1810 and still looks ahead of its time. · Shown at the Berlin Academy in 1810. Heinrich von Kleist's newspaper response, adapted from Clemens Brentano, is the best-known reaction to it. · Look at the bands of land, sea and sky: there are no framing trees and no path leading into depth. · About two years of repeated overpainting stripped the composition down and set aside normal perspective. · Emptiness is the subject. With almost nothing to hold the eye, the viewer shares the monk's position before a space too large to measure or explain. · King Friedrich Wilhelm III bought it in 1810 at the request of his son, the crown prince."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/abbey-in-the-oakwood.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Abbey in the Oakwood","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-abbey-oakwood","Caspar David Friedrich · 1809–10 · Oil on canvas, 110.4 × 171 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Monks carry a coffin through a snowy graveyard toward a ruined Gothic church among bare oaks. The painter Carl Gustav Carus, a friend, rated it among the deepest achievements of modern landscape. · Painted as the companion to The Monk by the Sea; the two were meant to be seen as a pair. · Look at the window: its model was the Eldena abbey ruin outside Greifswald, made more perfect here and fitted with a doorway. · Around 1830 the real ruin was restored to look much like the painted one, possibly under the crown prince's influence. · Faith and death meet here, and the painting works as a meditation on mortality rather than a record of a real place. · The crown prince kept it for about twenty years; it entered the Berlin museums in 1957 from the destroyed royal palace."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/caspar-david-friedrich-in-his-studio.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Caspar David Friedrich in His Studio","Georg Friedrich Kersting","kersting-friedrich-studio","Georg Friedrich Kersting · c. 1812 · Oil on canvas, 53.5 × 41 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · The landscape painter is shown at work in a Dresden room stripped almost bare. It is one of three studio portraits of him by the same artist, now in Hamburg, Berlin and Mannheim. · The two were friends from 1810; Hokanson and Seidenstein (Met) describe their joint sketching trips. · Look at the walls: two palettes, a straightedge and a drawing triangle are all that hang there. Shutters close half of one window and all of the other. · He holds brushes, palette and a mahlstick, a rod that steadies the hand, before a canvas turned away from us. · The bare studio suggests painting from the inner eye, not from the view outside. The sitter held that a painter should paint what he sees within himself. · Bought in 1906 from Johanna Friedrich of Greifswald, a descendant of the sitter, after the Berlin Centenary Exhibition brought it to notice."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Wanderer above the Sea of Fog","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-wanderer","Caspar David Friedrich · c. 1817 · Oil on canvas, 94.8 × 74.8 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · A man in a dark coat stands on a rocky summit above valleys filled with mist. Hokanson and Seidenstein (Met) call it a textbook case of the Romantic sublime. · Look at the figure: one large person fills the center of an upright canvas, both rare in the painter's work. · The view was put together in the studio: the foreground rock comes from a drawing made in Saxon Switzerland in June 1813, other landforms from studies of 1808. · The painting carries no signature or date, so its year is an estimate. · Because he turns away, we watch a person looking instead of meeting the scene directly, and seeing itself becomes the subject. Who he is cannot be established. · On permanent loan from a Hamburg foundation that bought it in 1970. The museum describes its ownership history as unresolved and a matter of concern. · Rückenfigur · German for a figure seen from behind, placed so that the viewer looks along with it."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/chalk-cliffs-on-rugen.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Chalk Cliffs on Rügen","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-chalk-cliffs","Caspar David Friedrich · 1818 · Oil on canvas, about 90 × 70 cm. Kunst Museum Winterthur, Reinhart am Stadtgarten, Winterthur · Three figures on a grassy clifftop, framed by leaning trees, look over white pinnacles and a calm sea. The museum counts it among the painter's most radical compositions, and among his most colorful and festive. · He and Caroline Bommer married that year; the picture followed their wedding journey, which his brother joined. · Look at the figures: the woman points downward, one man crawls to peer over the edge, and the other stands gazing out to sea. · Depth is almost abandoned. The museum reads the flat zones as life (green ground), death (white cliffs) and the infinite (sea fading into sky). · A private story may sit inside the landscape: the museum suggests the figures could be those three travelers, which would make the view a personal record. · Purchased in 1930; now in the Oskar Reinhart Foundation, whose collection the Winterthur museum shows."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-sea-of-ice.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","The Sea of Ice","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-sea-of-ice","Caspar David Friedrich · 1823–24 · Oil on canvas, 96.7 × 126.9 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Slabs of broken ice pile up into a jagged pyramid under a pale sky. The museum reads the clearing at the top as a hint of hope, or of something beyond this world. · Once called The Wreck of Hope, a name taken from a ship in a lost earlier version. · Look at the right: the dark hull of a crushed ship lies tipped on its side, half swallowed by the ice. · Built up from three small oil sketches of ice breaking up on the Elbe in winter 1820–21, all now in Hamburg. · Nature's force overwhelms human effort, and the ice is heaped up like a monument. The museum names William Edward Parry's Arctic voyages as a possible source and notes readings from religious to political. · Acquired by the Kunsthalle in 1905, the same year as one of the ice sketches."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/two-men-contemplating-the-moon.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Two Men Contemplating the Moon","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-two-men-moon","Caspar David Friedrich · c. 1825–30 · Oil on canvas, 34.9 × 43.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · On a rocky path beside a gnarled tree, two companions face a crescent in the evening sky. This small canvas is the last of three versions of the composition. · The famous first version, of 1819, is a separate painting in Dresden; a second, of about 1824, is in Berlin. · Look at the sky: the Met's Sabine Rewald (New York Studio School talk, 2002) found it pinker than Berlin's, its paint the thinnest, and this version the closest to Dresden. · She adds that he repeated small pictures, but not large ones with difficult skies. · Rewald (Studio School) reads the composition as partly about friendship: in the Dresden first version the pair are Friedrich and his friend August Heinrich. · Given to the Dresden doctor Otto Friedrich Rosenberg in exchange for medical care. His descendants kept it until 1999; the Met bought it in 2000."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/view-of-dresden-by-moonlight.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","View of Dresden by Moonlight","Johan Christian Dahl","dahl-dresden-moonlight","Johan Christian Dahl · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 78 × 130 cm. Galerie Neue Meister (Albertinum), Dresden · The city's churches and bridge stand dark against a night sky above the Elbe. The view had been famous since Bellotto's large painting of it in 1748. · The Norwegian-born painter settled in Dresden in 1818 and stayed nearly forty years, apart from trips to Italy and Norway. He was a close friend of Friedrich. · Look at the skyline: the Frauenkirche dome, the Hofkirche tower and the Augustus Bridge. · Light is the real subject: the moon itself, the bright edges of the clouds and the reflections on the river. · The city becomes a Romantic nocturne, or night piece: a well-known place offered as a mood to share, not as a guide to its buildings. · The gallery acquired it in 1937 through the Berlin dealer Karl Haberstock, in exchange for a painting by Nikolaus Gysis."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/america-wilderness-and-nation.html","America: Wilderness and Nation · Overview","America: Wilderness and Nation","","cole-oxbow","Cole, Durand, Leutze, Lane, Church and Bierstadt, 1836–1863 · In the United States, painters used landscape and history scenes to raise questions about the young country's land and its future. This part moves from Cole's moral allegories to the giant single-picture shows of Church and Bierstadt. · Wild land and cleared land as a question about the future · Painted cycles and history scenes that carry a lesson · Quiet harbor light and spectacular views of distant wonders · Thomas Cole · View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm—The Oxbow · 1836"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/view-from-mount-holyoke-northampton-massachusetts-after-a-thunderstorm-the-oxbow.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm—The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","cole-oxbow","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 130.8 × 193 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A storm moves away from a sharp bend in the Connecticut River, seen from a mountain lookout. Wild forest lies on one side of the valley and cleared, sunlit farmland on the other. · Look at the tiny figure beside the parasol in the lower foreground: Cole painted himself at his easel, glancing back at us. · The Met also owns a sketch for the composition, made the same year in oil and pencil on board. · Storm clouds and a shattered tree hang over the wild side; haystacks and sheep dot the fields, and the river loops around a flat tongue of farmland. The Met reads this contrast as settlers moving into land then thought of as untamed wilderness. · Given to the Met in 1908 by Mrs. Russell Sage. · Hudson River School · A later name for American landscape painters, about 1825–1875, centered on Cole and on scenery of the Hudson Valley and nearby mountains."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-course-of-empire-destruction.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","The Course of Empire: Destruction","Thomas Cole","cole-course-of-empire-destruction","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 161.3 cm. New-York Historical Society, New York · Invaders storm a grand classical city as fires burn and its people flee. It is the fourth stage in a five-picture cycle about how an empire rises and falls. · Commissioned in 1833 by the merchant Luman Reed for his home gallery; the full series was shown in New York in 1836. · Look at the headless giant statue of a warrior, which replaces the goddess that stood there in the previous canvas. · Cole carved his signature, unusually large, into a broken pedestal inside the scene. · Cole took the cycle's title from a 1729 poem by Bishop George Berkeley and its motto from Byron's Childe Harold. The Society also suggests echoes of John Martin's disaster scenes and of New York's Great Fire of 1835. · Reed died before it was finished; his collection later passed to the Society in 1858."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-voyage-of-life-childhood.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","The Voyage of Life: Childhood","Thomas Cole","cole-voyage-of-life-childhood","Thomas Cole · 1842 · Oil on canvas, 134.3 × 195.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A baby on a bed of flowers drifts out of a dark cave in a golden boat, guided by an angel. It opens a four-part series that follows one traveler down a river. · Painted in Rome and first shown there in 1842, at the Annual Exhibition of Modern Artists. · Look at the prow: a carved angel figurehead holds up an hourglass. · Cole signed and dated it at lower left, alongside the place where he made it. · Each stage of life, from childhood to old age, gets its own landscape and weather. The National Gallery points out that Cole presents prayer as the traveler's only hope of salvation. · Sold by Cole to a Cincinnati collector; the National Gallery bought it in 1971."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/kindred-spirits.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Kindred Spirits","Asher B. Durand","durand-kindred-spirits","Asher B. Durand · 1849 · Oil on canvas, 111.8 × 91.4 cm. Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art, Bentonville, Arkansas · Two men stand on a rock ledge high in the wooded Catskill Mountains. The picture honors Durand's fellow landscape painter Thomas Cole, whose death in 1848 it commemorates. · Look at the pair: Cole holds a portfolio and talks with the poet William Cullen Bryant. · Durand signed and dated it at lower left; the names Bryant and Cole are lettered at center left. · The tall, upright canvas stacks overhanging trees, cliffs, a distant waterfall and sky, keeping the two men small on their ledge above a rocky stream. · Crystal Bridges presents the scene as a place where people could feel close to God through nature. The museum also notes that Durand partly invented the view rather than copying a single spot."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/washington-crossing-the-delaware.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Washington Crossing the Delaware","Emanuel Leutze","leutze-washington-crossing","Emanuel Leutze · 1851 · Oil on canvas, 378.5 × 647.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The general stands in a crowded boat as his men push through floating river ice. A turning point of the Revolution is painted on a monumental scale. · It won acclaim in the United States and in Germany, where Leutze was born. · Look at the mixed crew named by Scott Manning Stevens, an Akwesasne Mohawk cultural historian at Syracuse University, in the Met's audio guide: a Native American man at the stern, an African American man, farmers and soldiers. · It hangs in a copy of its original frame, rebuilt from a Mathew Brady photograph. · Stevens says Leutze meant to show the nation at its best, all of society aboard except women, yet calls it not reportage but near propaganda. The Met's narrator calls it accurate but ennobling of the war's violence, including against Native peoples. · Given to the Met in 1897 by John Stewart Kennedy."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/lumber-schooners-at-evening-on-penobscot-bay.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Lumber Schooners at Evening on Penobscot Bay","Fitz Henry Lane","lane-lumber-schooners","Fitz Henry Lane · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 62.6 × 96.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A schooner stacked with lumber rests on a calm bay in Maine as the evening sky turns pink. The tiny crew and a far-off ship leave most of the canvas to sky and water. · First exhibited in 1963–64 in Maine and Its Artists, shown at Colby College, the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, and the Whitney. · Look at the four small sailors on deck working the sails. · Lane used thin, see-through glazes instead of thick paint, so his brushwork is hard to see. · The National Gallery sets this stillness against the more explosive style of Cole and Church. It links Lane's quiet, self-effacing method to Emerson's idea that artists should let nature speak through them. · Given to the National Gallery in 1980 by Mr. and Mrs. Francis W. Hatch, Sr. · Luminism · A later name for American landscapes of about 1850–1875 marked by still water, soft light and hidden brushwork."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/niagara.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Niagara","Frederic Edwin Church","church-niagara","Frederic Edwin Church · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 101.6 × 229.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Horseshoe Falls stretches across a long, low canvas, seen from the Canadian side. The National Gallery notes that artists had thought the falls almost unpaintable, and that reviewers praised how Church caught their scale and beauty. · First shown at a New York dealer's gallery in May 1857, titled The Great Fall, Niagara. · Look for one faint rainbow in two pieces: a short arc at upper left, picked up again in the spray in front of the falls. · Church sketched the falls from both the American and Canadian banks the year before. · Church left out any ground at the bottom edge, so the viewer seems suspended above the rushing water rather than standing on a bank. · Toured Britain, where John Ruskin saw it, and U.S. cities; the Corcoran bought it in 1876, and it joined the National Gallery in 2014. · Panorama · A very wide view that sweeps across a scene; in the 1800s also a huge painting shown in its own hall."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/heart-of-the-andes.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Heart of the Andes","Frederic Edwin Church","church-heart-of-the-andes","Frederic Edwin Church · 1859 · Oil on canvas, 168 × 305.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A tropical valley climbs through forest and plain to a snowy summit, showing hot, mild and frozen zones in one view. The German naturalist Alexander von Humboldt's studies of the region inspired it. · It was displayed in a darkened room, lit by gas jets behind silver reflectors, inside a window-like frame that is now lost. · Look closely at the plants; Church advised visitors to bring opera glasses for the detail. · It combines pencil and oil sketches from Ecuador, made on trips in 1853 and 1857, rather than one real view. · Presented on its own as a ticketed show at 25 cents, it drew a reported 12,000 to 13,000 paying visitors a month. · Margaret E. Dows bequeathed it to the Met in 1909."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-rocky-mountains-landers-peak.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak","Albert Bierstadt","bierstadt-rocky-mountains","Albert Bierstadt · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 186.7 × 306.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Shoshone camp spreads across a valley floor beneath a waterfall and a snow-capped peak in the Wind River Range. Karen Quinn of the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, notes that its huge scale was meant to overwhelm eastern audiences. · Bierstadt saw the region in 1859, traveling with Frederick W. Lander's government survey. · Look at the center: the waterfall drops into a still lake that mirrors it, just behind the camp. · Painted later in New York, working from sketches made on that trip. · The Met links the picture to Manifest Destiny: it presented the West as a frontier for White settlers to claim. The Met adds that this belief disregarded Indigenous peoples already there, with grave results, including for the Shoshone the painting shows. · Bought by the Met through the Rogers Fund in 1907."],[11,"/study/value-and-light.html","Start","Value and Light","","","Value and Light"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/a-short-history.html","A Short History · Overview","A Short History","","merrifield-pl01","From Leonardo to the drawing manual · Painters have studied how light builds form for five centuries: Leonardo in his notes, Caravaggio with one strong light, and later teachers with white models and printed plates. · Leonardo's Relief · Caravaggio's Single Light · The Drawing Manual · Mary P. Merrifield · Plate 1, Handbook of Light and Shade · about 1880"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-leonardos-relief.html","A Short History · History","Leonardo's Relief","","cross-fig21","Leonardo da Vinci treated light and shade as the way a flat picture gains relief: the look of solid, rounded form. · He studied how light falls on faces and folds of cloth and wrote about it at length. · Studies of drapery like this one let him work out light on folds in detail. · In 1897 the drawing teacher Anson Cross reproduced this study to show the care great artists took over light and shade."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-caravaggios-single-light.html","A Short History · History","Caravaggio's Single Light","","caravaggio-calling-of-saint-matthew-detail","Around 1600 Caravaggio lit his figures with one strong light against deep darkness. · He painted from live models posed under that light. Curator Letizia Treves of the National Gallery, London, says the light \"always really underpins the meaning\" in his pictures. · His first public works, in the Contarelli Chapel in Rome, caused a sensation when they were unveiled about 1600. · Visiting artists took the style home. The candlelit night scene grew out of his dark manner, though, as Treves notes, he never painted a candle himself. · He fell out of favor by the mid-1600s and was rediscovered only in the 1900s."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/the-calling-of-saint-matthew.html","A Short History","The Calling of Saint Matthew","Caravaggio","caravaggio-calling-of-saint-matthew","Caravaggio · 1599–1600 · Oil on canvas, about 324 × 340 cm. Contarelli Chapel, San Luigi dei Francesi, Rome · The story is told by the light: it enters from high at the right, beyond the frame, and slants down toward the tax collector Christ is calling. · The figures at the table are caught in the beam; the room around them stays dark. · The painted window, which one source describes as covered with oilskin, adds a diffused light; the same source suggests it recalls a painter's studio window."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-the-drawing-manual.html","A Short History · History","The Drawing Manual","","merrifield-pl01","In the 1800s, drawing manuals taught light and shade with plain white models: cubes, cylinders, spheres and plaster casts. · White models show light and shade without color getting in the way. Mary Merrifield's handbook, published by the London colormen George Rowney & Co., was in its twelfth edition by about 1880; Anson Cross published his in Boston in 1897. · Cross's book prints photographs of one white group under six kinds of light. This course uses them and Merrifield's plates. · Both books build up from a single object under a single light."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/value.html","Value · Overview","Value","","zurbaran-still-life-lemons","The ruler for light and dark · Value is how light or dark a tone is, from white to black. Light and shade are built from it. For value in color, see Color Theory. · The Value Scale · Local Value and Light · High Key and Low Key · Francisco de Zurbarán · Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose · 1633"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-the-value-scale.html","Value · Concept","The Value Scale","","merrifield-pl02-scale","A value scale runs in steps from the lightest light to the darkest dark. · It gives a painter a ruler for tone. Merrifield also named five broad steps: light, half light, middle tint, half dark and dark. · Teachers use five, seven, nine or ten steps; even spacing matters more than the count. · On any lit object, Merrifield observed, middle tints take up most of the surface; the brightest and darkest spots are small."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-local-value-and-light.html","Value · Concept","Local Value and Light","","cross-fig07","Local value is a surface's own lightness or darkness, apart from the light falling on it; light and shade are added on top. · Cross's photograph shows a white sphere and a red one under the same light. He notes both show the same pattern of light and dark, but the range on the red one is narrower. · Cross pointed out that the lit side of a dark object can look lighter than the shadow side of a light one. · The highlight shows more strongly on the dark sphere, against its deep color. · To judge a tone, ask two questions: how light is this surface, and how much light is it getting?"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/still-life-with-lemons-oranges-and-a-rose.html","Value","Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose","Francisco de Zurbarán","zurbaran-still-life-lemons","Francisco de Zurbarán · 1633 · Oil on canvas, 62.2 × 109.5 cm. Norton Simon Museum, Pasadena · Light from the left falls on objects of very different local value: pale yellow citrons, darker oranges, a white cup and reflective pewter plates. · Each object keeps its own value in light and in shadow, so the row reads clearly against the dark background. · The museum notes that the orange leaves create a \"rhythm of light and shadow\". · A 2008 cleaning removed opaque varnish; this older photograph probably shows the duller surface from before it."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-high-key-and-low-key.html","Value · Concept","High Key and Low Key","","key-high-low","A picture's key is where most of its values sit: mostly light is high key, mostly dark is low key. · Key does much to set the mood. It is separate from contrast: a high-key picture can still hold a few strong darks, and a low-key one a few bright lights."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/summers-day.html","Value","Summer's Day","Berthe Morisot","morisot-summers-day","Berthe Morisot · about 1879 · Oil on canvas, 45.7 × 75.2 cm. The National Gallery, London, in partnership with Hugh Lane Gallery, Dublin (on long loan to Dublin, 2026–31) · Almost every value here is light, so the picture reads as bright outdoor air. · The dark blue coat at the left is the largest dark in the picture. · The National Gallery says her zigzag strokes catch \"the play of light on the water\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/philosopher-in-meditation.html","Value","Philosopher in Meditation","Rembrandt","rembrandt-philosopher-in-meditation","Rembrandt · 1632 · Oil on wood, 28 × 34 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Most of this small panel is dark. · Broad window light falls on the old man; the fire at the right is a small, separate glow. · Yellowed varnish probably makes the whole picture warmer and dimmer than it was painted."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/light-on-form.html","Light on Form · Overview","Light on Form","","merrifield-pl09","What one light does to a solid · Under one light, every rounded solid shows the same families of light and dark. Once you can name them, you can find them on anything. · The Light and Shadow Sequence · Highlights · The Core Shadow · Reflected Light · Cast Shadows · Flat Planes and Round Forms · Mary P. Merrifield · Plate 9, Handbook of Light and Shade · about 1880"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-the-light-and-shadow-sequence.html","Light on Form · Concept","The Light and Shadow Sequence","","merrifield-pl09","On a round form under one light, values come in a set order: highlight, light, halftone, core shadow, reflected light, and the cast shadow on the ground. · Surfaces facing the light get the most; the rest turn away and get less. Some drawing courses call this light logic. · The order holds on an apple, a head or a fold of cloth. · Merrifield noted that a line through the highlight and the center of a sphere crosses the middle of its darkest band. · Halftone · the grays between full light and shadow, where the surface begins to turn away. · Terminator · the line on a form where light gives way to shadow; also called the shadow edge."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/quince-cabbage-melon-and-cucumber.html","Light on Form","Quince, Cabbage, Melon and Cucumber","Juan Sánchez Cotán","sanchez-cotan-quince-cabbage","Juan Sánchez Cotán · about 1602 · Oil on canvas. The San Diego Museum of Art · Simple fruits and vegetables against black show the sequence plainly. · The cabbage and melon turn from lit fronts into deep shadow on their far sides. · The cut melon shows flat cut faces and the round rind taking light differently."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-highlights.html","Light on Form · Concept","Highlights","","cross-fig16","A highlight is the light source reflected off a surface. · Cross's black bottle shows two highlights because the window it faced was divided in two. · Highlights appear only where a surface reflects the light straight to the eye, so they shift as the viewer moves."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/still-life-with-oysters-a-silver-tazza-and-glassware.html","Light on Form","Still Life with Oysters, a Silver Tazza, and Glassware","Willem Claesz Heda","heda-still-life-oysters","Willem Claesz Heda · 1635 · Oil on wood, 49.8 × 80.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Glass, silver and oyster shell each catch the light in their own way. · The Met notes Heda worked \"almost exclusively in shades of gray\". · Small, sharp highlights on the tall glass and the overturned silver cup read as hard, polished surfaces; the oysters' wet sheen is broader and softer."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-the-core-shadow.html","Light on Form · Concept","The Core Shadow","","merrifield-pl07","The core shadow is the darkest band on the form itself, just past the turn into shadow. · In Merrifield's first cylinder this \"extreme dark\" sits a little in from the edge, not at the outline. · Cross stressed careful drawing of \"the dividing line of light and shade\". · Under the same light, a soft core means a gentle curve and a crisp one a sharper turn."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-reflected-light.html","Light on Form · Concept","Reflected Light","","merrifield-pl10","Reflected light is light bounced back into a shadow from nearby surfaces. · It lightens the shadow near the edge and helps the form turn. Cross reminded students that, seen as part of the whole picture, reflected lights \"are not lights but darks\". · Merrifield found it often \"warmer in colour\" than the rest of the object. · Cross warned that students tend to exaggerate it."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-cast-shadows.html","Light on Form · Concept","Cast Shadows","","merrifield-pl03","A cast shadow is the shape an object blocks out on another surface. · It is usually darkest and sharpest where the object meets the surface, and softer farther away. · Its direction and length show where the light is."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/dust-motes-dancing-in-the-sunbeams.html","Light on Form","Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams","Vilhelm Hammershøi","hammershoi-dust-motes","Vilhelm Hammershøi · 1901 · Oil on canvas, 63 × 52.5 cm. Ordrupgaard, Copenhagen · Here the light itself is the subject: an empty room, a window, and shafts of sun. · Ordrupgaard says the light lies \"like a geometrical form across the floor\". · The shafts show in the air, crossing the room diagonally from the window."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-flat-planes-and-round-forms.html","Light on Form · Concept","Flat Planes and Round Forms","","merrifield-pl05-pl07","Flat faces change value mostly at their edges; curved surfaces change value gradually. · Compare Merrifield's Plate 5 prisms, above, with her Plate 7 cylinders, below. · Many painters block in round forms as a few flat planes, then soften the joins."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/light-sources.html","Light Sources · Overview","Light Sources","","cross-figs-09-12","Where the light comes from and what kind it is · The same objects look different under every light. Direction, size and number of sources each change the pattern of light and dark. · Direction of Light · Hard and Soft Light · Backlight · Candlelight · Two Lights at Once · Anson K. Cross · Figs. 9 and 12, Light and Shade · 1897"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-direction-of-light.html","Light Sources · Concept","Direction of Light","","cross-figs-08-10-11","Where the light comes from decides how much of a subject is in light and how much in shadow. · Cross's photographs show one white group lit three ways: from the upper left (top), from behind the viewer (middle) and from in front of the viewer (bottom). · Side light divides each form into light and shadow, which makes its shape easy to read. · Light from behind the viewer flattens form; Cross wrote that its contrasts come mostly from local color."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-hard-and-soft-light.html","Light Sources · Concept","Hard and Soft Light","","cross-figs-09-12","A source that looks small from the subject, such as the distant sun, gives hard light; one that looks large, such as an overcast sky or a nearby big window, gives soft light. · Hard light makes sharp-edged shadows and strong contrast. Soft light wraps around forms and its shadows fade at the edges. · Outdoors, Cross noted, light reflected from every direction makes shadows lighter than indoors."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/sewing-the-sail.html","Light Sources","Sewing the Sail","Joaquín Sorolla","sorolla-sewing-the-sail","Joaquín Sorolla · 1896 · Oil on canvas, 222 × 300 cm. Ca' Pesaro, International Gallery of Modern Art, Venice · Sun filtering through plants scatters hard-edged patches of light and shade across a white sail. · The sunlit patches on the sail are among the lightest values in the picture; the figures, largely in shade, stay readable against them."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-backlight.html","Light Sources · Concept","Backlight","","cross-fig11","Backlight comes from behind the subject, toward the viewer. · Most of what we see is in shadow; light mainly catches the tops and outlines. · Cast shadows run toward the viewer and become large, dark shapes."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-candlelight.html","Light Sources · Concept","Candlelight","","latour-magdalen-smoking-flame-detail","A candle or lamp inside the picture lights nearby things strongly; its light fades quickly with distance. · When the flame is in view it becomes part of the subject, and the picture gets one bright center. · Candlelight was ordinary evening light in Wright of Derby's day, Christine Riding of the National Gallery, London, points out, so such scenes showed something familiar. · A hidden source can still be read from what it lights and the shadows it throws."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/the-magdalen-with-the-smoking-flame.html","Light Sources","The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame","Georges de La Tour","latour-magdalen-smoking-flame","Georges de La Tour · about 1635–37 · Oil on canvas, 117 × 91.8 cm. Los Angeles County Museum of Art · Her face, hand and sleeve, nearest the flame, are brightest; the lap and skull are dimmer, and the legs fade into shadow. · LACMA calls the candle the painting's \"chief symbol and true subject\". · The skull's polish and the books' leather answer the flame in different ways. · His sharp turns from light to dark descend from Caravaggio, whose manner reached him through the painter Jean Le Clerc."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/an-experiment-on-a-bird-in-the-air-pump.html","Light Sources","An Experiment on a Bird in the Air Pump","Joseph Wright of Derby","wright-air-pump","Joseph Wright of Derby · 1768 · Oil on canvas, 183 × 244 cm. The National Gallery, London · One candle the viewer cannot see, hidden behind a glass on the table, lights a ring of faces. · The National Gallery traces colors darkening with distance: the lecturer's robe is \"salmon pink in the candlelight\", then magenta farther off. · Lighting this forceful, the Gallery says, was \"new in British art\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-two-lights-at-once.html","Light Sources · Concept","Two Lights at Once","","sargent-carnation-lily-detail","When two sources light a scene, each makes its own lights and shadows, often of different color. · Cross warned that daylight and lamplight together make effects so varied that rules become \"impossible\". · Sort out which surfaces face which source. · A warm source and a cool one make the difference easy to see."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/carnation-lily-lily-rose.html","Light Sources","Carnation, Lily, Lily, Rose","John Singer Sargent","sargent-carnation-lily","John Singer Sargent · 1885–6 · Oil on canvas, 174 × 153.7 cm. Tate, London · Two lights meet here: the fading mauve of dusk and the orange glow of paper lanterns. · Tate notes that a lily or leaves in front of a lantern become back-lit shapes. · The effect lasted only minutes a day: ten by Sargent's own letter, up to twenty-five by a friend's account."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/edges.html","Edges · Overview","Edges","","cross-fig13","Sharp, soft and lost · An edge is the line where one value meets another. How sharp it is helps show form and space. · Hard, Soft and Lost Edges · Sfumato · Anson K. Cross · Fig. 13, Light and Shade · 1897"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-hard-soft-and-lost-edges.html","Edges · Concept","Hard, Soft and Lost Edges","","cross-fig13","A hard edge is sharp, a soft edge is blended, and a lost edge disappears where two values match. · In Cross's photograph the cylinder's outline is lost for a short stretch inside the block's shadow. He wrote that a contour should not be drawn where it cannot be seen. · Losing some edges, Cross argued, is part of \"the charm of the best artistic work\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/juan-de-pareja.html","Edges","Juan de Pareja","Diego Velázquez","velazquez-juan-de-pareja","Diego Velázquez · 1650 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 69.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Compare the edges: crisp at the white collar, soft where the face and beard turn into the hair and where the dark coat meets the dark background. · The white collar is the lightest value in the picture and sets off the face."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-sfumato.html","Edges · Concept","Sfumato","","leonardo-virgin-of-the-rocks-detail","Sfumato, from the Italian for \"to turn to smoke\", blends light into shadow with no visible line. · Cross quoted Leonardo's advice that lights and shadows should meet \"as smoke loses itself in the air\". In a 2019 lecture Caroline Campbell, then of the National Gallery, London, stressed that his lighting is a set of deliberate choices, not a copy of nature. · Cross added that even Leonardo's advice should be tested against nature, not taken literally."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/the-virgin-of-the-rocks.html","Edges","The Virgin of the Rocks","Leonardo da Vinci","leonardo-virgin-of-the-rocks","Leonardo da Vinci · about 1491/2–9 and 1506–8 · Oil on wood, 189.5 × 120 cm. The National Gallery, London · Out of the dark grotto the figures appear by soft degrees; the Virgin's temples and nose have no hard outline. · A shaft of light enters from the upper left. · The far mountains are painted blue, with softened edges, so they look hazy and distant."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/styles-of-light.html","Styles of Light · Overview","Styles of Light","","rembrandt-aristotle","Balanced or extreme · Two terms describe painting built on strong light and shade. They differ in how far they push the contrast. · Chiaroscuro · Tenebrism · Rembrandt · Aristotle with a Bust of Homer · 1653"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-chiaroscuro.html","Styles of Light · Concept","Chiaroscuro","","rembrandt-aristotle-detail","Chiaroscuro, Italian for \"light-dark\", is the balance and pattern of light and shade that models forms. · Lit and shadowed parts are arranged into one design; the turns between them can be gradual or abrupt. · The word also names drawings and prints made in light and dark tones."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/aristotle-with-a-bust-of-homer.html","Styles of Light","Aristotle with a Bust of Homer","Rembrandt","rembrandt-aristotle","Rembrandt · 1653 · Oil on canvas, 143.5 × 136.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Light picks out the face, the white sleeves, the gold chain and the hand resting on the bust; the rest sinks into brown shadow. · The Met describes his \"dark palette and almost sculptural buildup of paint\". · Shadow falls across part of the face, so the eyes stay half hidden."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-tenebrism.html","Styles of Light · Concept","Tenebrism","","gentileschi-judith-detroit-detail","Tenebrism pushes chiaroscuro to an extreme: figures lit sharply against near-black, with few middle tones. · The name comes from the Italian tenebroso, \"dark\". · The darkness hides the setting and pushes the figures toward us."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/judith-and-her-maidservant-with-the-head-of-holofernes.html","Styles of Light","Judith and Her Maidservant with the Head of Holofernes","Artemisia Gentileschi","gentileschi-judith-detroit","Artemisia Gentileschi · about 1623–25 · Oil on canvas, 187.2 × 142 cm. Detroit Institute of Arts · A single candle lights the scene; Judith raises a hand beside the flame, as if to screen its light. · The Detroit Institute of Arts says its theatrical lighting echoes Caravaggio. · Almost everything outside the candle's reach is lost in darkness."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/working-in-value.html","Working in Value · Overview","Working in Value","","cross-figs-22-23","Planning light and dark before color · Draftsmen and painters have practical ways to work in value first: simplify, start from a middle tone, or paint in grays. · Simplifying What You See · Drawing on Toned Paper · Grisaille · Anson K. Cross · Figs. 22 and 23, Light and Shade · 1897"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-simplifying-what-you-see.html","Working in Value · Concept","Simplifying What You See","","cross-figs-22-23","Simplifying groups many small values into a few large ones. · Cross set a photograph of shingled buildings beside a sepia sketch of the same view: the shingles are only suggested, while the masses of light and shadow remain. · Cross told students to blur their eyes and decide whether each detail reads as part of the light or the dark. · For planning a picture in values, see Color Theory and Composition."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-drawing-on-toned-paper.html","Working in Value · Concept","Drawing on Toned Paper","","prudhon-blue-paper-detail","On toned paper the paper's own color serves as a middle value; the artist works down to the darks and up to the lights. · Black and white chalk on a mid-toned sheet cover the whole range of values. · Artist Pamela Lawton's drawing demonstration for the Met does the same in graphite: tone the whole sheet, erase out the lights, add the darks."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/study-of-drapery.html","Working in Value","Study of Drapery","Pierre-Paul Prud'hon","prudhon-blue-paper","Pierre-Paul Prud'hon · about 1813 · Black and white chalk with stumping on blue paper, 25.5 × 26 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A study for the hem of a gown falling over a chair: white is kept for the ridges, black for the deepest folds, and the bare paper shows between. · Stumping, or rubbing the chalk to blend it, gives what the Met calls \"a velvety yet radiant quality\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-grisaille.html","Working in Value · Concept","Grisaille","","ingres-odalisque-grisaille-detail","Grisaille is painting in shades of a single color, usually gray. · Used as an underpainting, it sets the values before color goes on; Leonardo's figures in the Virgin of the Rocks were built this way, over black and white. · A grisaille can also stand on its own as a finished work."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/odalisque-in-grisaille.html","Working in Value","Odalisque in Grisaille","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","ingres-odalisque-grisaille","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · about 1824–34 · Oil on canvas, 83.2 × 109.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · An unfinished, reduced repeat of his Grande Odalisque of 1814, with the form modeled almost entirely in grays. · The Met notes grisailles often guided engravers making black-and-white prints; this one's purpose is uncertain, and it may have served his teaching studio. · A faint blush of color shows on her back and right ear."]]};
